1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,884
Learn software system design in past interviews.

2
00:00:03,278 --> 00:00:11,524
This course provides a practical five-step framework for structuring your thinking and tackling vague requirements under pressure.

3
00:00:11,982 --> 00:00:21,280
Additionally, the course walks through real-world examples, designing systems similar to Spotify, Booking.com, and WhatsApp from scratch.

4
00:00:21,646 --> 00:00:23,680
Heike Simon Yan developed this course.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,920
Most software engineering roles today require system design knowledge.

6
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:34,948
Whether it's for the job itself or for interviews, you are expected to know how to design systems at scale.

7
00:00:35,534 --> 00:00:41,412
And especially for senior and staff level positions, it's one of the deciding factors for the company.

8
00:00:42,126 --> 00:00:51,588
That's why I put together this complete system design interview guide with real production system examples that show up the most often in system design interviews.

9
00:00:52,334 --> 00:00:58,084
First, we'll start with the framework to know how to structure your thinking, what clarifying questions to ask,

10
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:04,068
and how to make sure you cover everything the interviewer is looking for without going off track.

11
00:01:04,718 --> 00:01:14,212
And then we'll apply this same framework across three different system design examples so that you can see how the same framework applies to different production systems.

12
00:01:14,734 --> 00:01:19,332
This is the most practical framework that I know for passing system design interviews.

13
00:01:19,790 --> 00:01:30,404
Early in my career, I failed a lot of these interviews when applying to senior roles, and looking back now, there is one pattern that was the same across all of them,

14
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:35,012
which is system design was almost always part of the conversation.

15
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:47,236
And you can argue that nowadays this is also a requirement for mid-level positions, especially now when all of the software engineers use AI to write the code implementation.

16
00:01:47,502 --> 00:01:56,160
Companies would like to know whether you understand the bigger picture and how components talk to each other, how to design solutions from scratch

17
00:01:56,320 --> 00:01:57,796
and discuss the trade-offs.

18
00:01:58,446 --> 00:02:08,420
Of course, the depth of knowledge is different depending on whether you apply for mid to senior roles, but still you have to be prepared for this type of interviews,

19
00:02:08,814 --> 00:02:11,360
whether you're mid, senior or above.

20
00:02:11,694 --> 00:02:14,560
So this shows up in different ways actually.

21
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:23,680
The most common one that we know is the whiteboarding style, where there is a session where you design a full system or a feature from scratch.

22
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:26,308
But there are actually three more types to this.

23
00:02:26,798 --> 00:02:35,076
Another way can be discussion about bottlenecks in the company's existing infrastructure and see if you can suggest solutions to this

24
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:38,340
and you have the basic knowledge of system design.

25
00:02:38,894 --> 00:02:49,188
Another type is walking through your past projects, so questions about your past projects to see if you actually understand the architecture of what you built there.

26
00:02:49,774 --> 00:02:56,260
And it can also be conceptual questions that test the depth of your knowledge about system design concepts.

27
00:02:57,102 --> 00:03:07,600
But even if you learn all the concepts of system design, you still need a framework to navigate a 45 or 60 minute discussion and make the most out of that time.

28
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:10,564
That's exactly why we have this framework and guide.

29
00:03:11,022 --> 00:03:19,588
And another important thing before we'll get to discuss the framework is that most engineers think they are only being evaluated on the system they design,

30
00:03:19,950 --> 00:03:24,068
which is just one out of five dimensions that you're being tested on.

31
00:03:24,654 --> 00:03:31,236
And just by knowing what these dimensions are will help you way more to prepare for these type of interviews.

32
00:03:32,046 --> 00:03:41,124
Before getting to the dimensions, let's first understand why this feels hard compared to other type of interviews like behavioral rounds or live coding.

33
00:03:41,614 --> 00:03:50,532
For example, if it's a live coding round, this usually is either a lead code style problem or some real world challenge, but either way,

34
00:03:50,734 --> 00:03:53,280
there are clear inputs and outputs, right?

35
00:03:53,390 --> 00:04:02,948
Let's say if it is a lead code style challenge, then you have a function which accepts these type of arguments, you know exactly what the type of the argument is,

36
00:04:03,310 --> 00:04:07,876
you know exactly what you're expected to return as an output from the function.

37
00:04:08,238 --> 00:04:14,080
So it is clear whether your solution works or not, because you can copy-paste this code and try to run it,

38
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:20,964
and you can also double check by running test cases on a whiteboard without even running the code.

39
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:25,476
But system design is actually the opposite of all of that.

40
00:04:25,966 --> 00:04:32,260
First of all, the requirements are very vague, and it is your job to clarify those requirements and lock in the scope.

41
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:38,468
And also the output, which is the solution that you will propose, there is no single right answer to it.

42
00:04:39,182 --> 00:04:47,120
And this type of interview is testing how you think, not just what you know, or not whether you can come up with the right solution,

43
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:52,452
because there is no single right answer to the question that you will get during system design interview.

44
00:04:52,814 --> 00:04:56,228
With that, let's see what are you actually being evaluated on.

45
00:04:56,718 --> 00:05:05,120
Most candidates prepare by just memorizing a couple of architectures like Spotify, WhatsApp, and so on, which is, of course, a step forward

46
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:16,452
if you know a couple of variants of systems and what components go into this, but you need to also understand the other four dimensions that interviewers score you on.

47
00:05:16,878 --> 00:05:20,804
So let's start with the first one, which is communication and collaboration.

48
00:05:21,294 --> 00:05:29,764
This is whether you ask clarifying questions, whether you validate assumptions as you go, or you just make assumptions and start building the system.

49
00:05:30,222 --> 00:05:34,084
And it's kind of testing how you would work in a real-world setup as well.

50
00:05:34,638 --> 00:05:42,276
Let's say your manager assigns a task to you, like a big feature or a big system service you need to build from scratch.

51
00:05:42,606 --> 00:05:44,400
What do you do in this kind of situations?

52
00:05:44,560 --> 00:05:52,640
If some things are not clear, do you just make assumptions, build a solution, and then figure out that it was not what they were expecting?

53
00:05:52,800 --> 00:06:01,188
So now you just wasted a lot of time in between and you need to come back and write another solution, which is actually what they were looking for.

54
00:06:01,550 --> 00:06:12,356
Or you clarify this in the beginning. So they are trying to also understand whether it's easy to work with you or not, whether you are good at communication skills apart from

55
00:06:12,974 --> 00:06:14,052
technical knowledge.

56
00:06:14,638 --> 00:06:23,364
The next one is whether you have structured way of thinking and approaching problems, or can you break down the vague problem that you just got

57
00:06:23,920 --> 00:06:30,240
into specific outlined actions that you will take, or you just jump between random decisions.

58
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:34,560
you go back and forth between different designs, phases, and so on.

59
00:06:34,670 --> 00:06:41,520
So strong candidates here usually show that they can organize their faults even when the problem is not very clear.

60
00:06:41,806 --> 00:06:50,116
They at least have an outline of what are the steps we are going to take, and at every step is clear what we're working on right now and what's up next.

61
00:06:50,862 --> 00:06:53,124
The next one is your technical judgment.

62
00:06:53,390 --> 00:07:00,292
So when you pick a solution, let's say database, a cache, a message queue, can you explain your decision why?

63
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:09,604
Like why you chose specifically SQL database, why you chose the caching mechanism or the specific type of message queue that you chose.

64
00:07:10,478 --> 00:07:21,280
This part shows that if you choose some tools to be used in a system, you choose them based on the requirements, not just out of habit or not just because you know what Kafka is,

65
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:25,476
but maybe there was another message queue that was a better fit for this type of system.

66
00:07:26,190 --> 00:07:29,380
The fourth dimension is your practical depth.

67
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:40,260
This part differentiates between two candidates who might have same theoretical knowledge, but one of them also has practice with building these type of systems.

68
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:51,012
So it will show up by seeing whether you think about failure modes, about the scale, about things that can go wrong in the system, because if you just learned at the theory level,

69
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:55,364
you wouldn't be able to catch these type of failures in the system.

70
00:07:55,918 --> 00:08:05,348
So this part specifically is for interviewers to spot engineers who have actually shipped production systems versus those who just learned from theory.

71
00:08:05,934 --> 00:08:12,676
And then lastly, it's the time management as well, because these type of interviews are usually either 45 or 60 minutes,

72
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:20,160
and here you need to not get stuck on one area and run out of time by doing high-level design only.

73
00:08:20,398 --> 00:08:31,332
And it's worth asking your interviewer early where they want to focus on, or proposing it yourself and getting alignment to make sure that you're managing your time effectively.

74
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:40,480
And along the process of an interview, you need to always know what's the time and how much is left, how much you spent on this section,

75
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:46,800
and if you're spending a lot of time on it, it's time to move on. So you need to also show time management, which again.

76
00:08:47,598 --> 00:08:53,188
All of these five pillars translate to how you will work in a real environment.

77
00:08:53,486 --> 00:08:59,140
If you can't manage your time during an interview, then you're also not good at managing your time during work.

78
00:08:59,630 --> 00:09:10,180
If you don't have practical depth, then you also won't be able to ship projects confidently if you only know the theory, right? And same applies to all of these five pillars.

79
00:09:10,894 --> 00:09:21,188
And now that we know these five pillars, let's focus on the time management part and what are the stages you need to take to complete a system design solution.

80
00:09:21,518 --> 00:09:24,516
There are five steps for any system design problem.

81
00:09:24,974 --> 00:09:29,892
This is a repeatable structure you can apply to any whiteboarding system design interview.

82
00:09:30,096 --> 00:09:37,892
And the goal here is to stay organized and show the interviewer how you think through the problems throughout these 45 or 60 minutes.

83
00:09:38,702 --> 00:09:43,696
60 minutes is actually more common because there is a lot to cover in this type of interviews.

84
00:09:43,776 --> 00:09:49,732
So assuming it's 60 minutes, let's see what are the steps we will take and how much time we will dedicate to each step.

85
00:09:50,478 --> 00:09:53,252
The very first one is clarifying requirements.

86
00:09:53,742 --> 00:09:57,540
You don't want to start designing anything before you understand the scope.

87
00:09:57,806 --> 00:10:00,496
And this is the most common mistake candidates make.

88
00:10:00,718 --> 00:10:06,084
So let's see what these five steps are at a high level, and then we'll get to discuss each of them in more detail.

89
00:10:06,606 --> 00:10:14,176
The very first one is you need to clarify requirements at least for 5 to 10 minutes, depending on the length of the interview.

90
00:10:14,336 --> 00:10:20,964
You need to make sure you have all the functional and non-functional requirements before moving on to designing anything.

91
00:10:21,454 --> 00:10:30,736
After that, you can either start by data and API layer and then get into the high-level architecture, or this part is kind of variable.

92
00:10:30,816 --> 00:10:37,892
You can also start from architecture and get into the data and API layer afterwards, whichever feels easier for you.

93
00:10:38,574 --> 00:10:49,696
For each of these, you would spend 15 to 20 minutes, not more, and then you would move on to discussing scale and edge cases towards the end once you have the high-level architecture,

94
00:10:49,856 --> 00:10:51,652
the data layer and APIs.

95
00:10:51,936 --> 00:11:03,780
So here you might discuss either how you would scale this system, or if reliability is more important for that type of system, you would discuss other edge cases and how you would handle the failures.

96
00:11:04,302 --> 00:11:12,256
And the last part is to wrap up and talk about anything that you could improve on this system, but you didn't have time for it.

97
00:11:12,366 --> 00:11:15,876
So just briefly mentioning this before wrapping up the interview.

98
00:11:16,558 --> 00:11:25,444
Let's start with the first step, which usually takes up to 10 minutes or less, and this is where you clarify the problem first before getting into the solution.

99
00:11:25,742 --> 00:11:32,836
You do not want to jump into designing anything yet before clarifying the functional and non-functional requirements.

100
00:11:33,678 --> 00:11:41,860
By functional requirements, we mean what are the main use cases of this system that we're building, what is explicitly out of scope,

101
00:11:42,176 --> 00:11:48,516
because if you're given something to design like Spotify, you can never design it in 60 minutes.

102
00:11:48,656 --> 00:11:56,416
This usually takes multiple teams, months of efforts, if not years, to build and design all of this, right?

103
00:11:56,576 --> 00:12:00,132
So of course you can't do it as a single engineer during 60 minutes.

104
00:12:00,590 --> 00:12:09,476
The goal here is to see what are the main use cases we are focusing on, what we leave out of scope, and it also helps to clarify who are the users

105
00:12:09,856 --> 00:12:11,908
and what type of platform we are building.

106
00:12:12,590 --> 00:12:18,576
Then you also focus on non-functional requirements, which answers what are the constraints for this system.

107
00:12:18,830 --> 00:12:28,196
For example, how many daily or monthly active users you will have, what is the read-to-write ratio, you would clarify the latency requirements,

108
00:12:28,416 --> 00:12:35,364
and also what is the acceptable downtime, which refers to availability of the system, before moving on to the next stage.

109
00:12:35,982 --> 00:12:42,016
And it's a good idea to always check at the end of each phase whether you're good to move on.

110
00:12:42,096 --> 00:12:46,916
Because if you missed some things during this stage, interviewer can give you hints.

111
00:12:47,150 --> 00:12:52,068
For example, you may fail to clarify some of the important functional requirements.

112
00:12:52,878 --> 00:13:00,484
This is a good place to ask something like Does this scope and these requirements make sense before I move forward to high level design?

113
00:13:00,816 --> 00:13:03,268
If they say yes, then you clarified everything.

114
00:13:03,566 --> 00:13:12,196
If they give you a hint, then usually you missed something in functional and non-functional requirements, so you would clarify that before moving on to the next stage.

115
00:13:12,750 --> 00:13:20,096
And next is step two, which is either the data and API layer or high-level design, depending on which one is easier for you.

116
00:13:20,256 --> 00:13:29,092
If you think it helps to start with data layers, understand how entities connect to each other and then get to the high-level design, that's the approach to take.

117
00:13:29,390 --> 00:13:35,716
If you think imagining it high level first helps you understand the user flow, then you start with high-level design.

118
00:13:36,096 --> 00:13:42,628
I personally start with high-level design, then get here, but sometimes I also start with data and API layer.

119
00:13:42,976 --> 00:13:48,004
This part is to model what you're storing and how clients will interact with your system.

120
00:13:48,686 --> 00:13:54,596
When it comes to data model, we should focus on what are the main entities for this type of system.

121
00:13:54,990 --> 00:14:00,260
Let's say if it's something like YouTube, then the main entities are videos and users.

122
00:14:00,750 --> 00:14:08,996
And you would also map out the relationship between them, what are the primary keys, foreign keys, key indexes that you will use in the database,

123
00:14:09,456 --> 00:14:19,300
and how big each record can get. This is also a good place to clarify which data needs strong consistency and where you can afford eventual consistency.

124
00:14:19,918 --> 00:14:27,876
Next part of this stage is to also define the API contracts, like what are the main endpoints with request and response formats.

125
00:14:28,430 --> 00:14:40,004
Here it will also be good if you can talk about pagination, error handling, rate limiting, versioning, and also follow the best practices of REST API in case you're using REST type of APIs,

126
00:14:40,176 --> 00:14:42,468
which is in most cases that's the case.

127
00:14:43,214 --> 00:14:51,056
And this is also where you will make the database choice, whether it's PostgreSQL, DynamoDB, MongoDB, whatever you pick.

128
00:14:51,216 --> 00:14:59,376
You should also explain why, given the requirements, you went with that specific option and also walk through the trade-offs

129
00:14:59,536 --> 00:15:02,564
of what are other things you considered but you didn't choose.

130
00:15:03,182 --> 00:15:07,844
The other important part is the high-level architecture, which takes another 15 minutes.

131
00:15:08,718 --> 00:15:15,204
This is where you spend a big portion of a time, design the components and show how data flows through them.

132
00:15:15,918 --> 00:15:24,260
In case nothing comes to your mind, you can start with these four components which are almost always present in majority types of systems.

133
00:15:24,846 --> 00:15:34,948
These are API servers which will be accepting the requests, a load balancer that distributes traffic between them, database where you persist the data,

134
00:15:35,296 --> 00:15:42,308
and optionally cache layers that caches some common data that you're fetching from the database.

135
00:15:43,150 --> 00:15:50,628
At this step, it usually helps to map out the user journey from front end to backend all the way to the database.

136
00:15:50,976 --> 00:15:52,896
So was the front end?

137
00:15:53,134 --> 00:16:03,076
It's some sort of web app or a mobile app or desktop app, and then it connects to your API gateway and load balancers, which will distribute among API servers.

138
00:16:03,502 --> 00:16:06,372
You might have caching layer among these servers.

139
00:16:07,214 --> 00:16:15,268
Optionally, you may have message queues that decouple the services, and you almost always have a database to store the data in the backend.

140
00:16:15,598 --> 00:16:24,336
So walk the interviewer through how a request moves through your system from client through load balancer, API server into cache and database

141
00:16:24,590 --> 00:16:27,616
and back all the read and write operations.

142
00:16:27,918 --> 00:16:33,988
If you're using caching, make sure to also mention the TTL and what invalidation mechanism you use.

143
00:16:34,414 --> 00:16:43,044
And for different parts of your system, you should also walk through your choice between consistency, availability, and partition tolerance,

144
00:16:43,376 --> 00:16:45,572
which is coming from the CAP theorem.

145
00:16:46,254 --> 00:16:57,188
Which means some of your components may need different approaches, and explaining that shows that you understand the theorem and also the trade-offs that you're making for this system.

146
00:16:57,902 --> 00:17:10,116
The fourth step is discussing bottlenecks, scale and reliability, which can be anywhere from 5 to 10 minutes depending on how much time you spend on high level design, data, and API layer.

147
00:17:10,702 --> 00:17:18,564
This is one of the important parts where you show that you can think about what breaks under heavy load, not just the happy paths.

148
00:17:19,056 --> 00:17:26,116
And this is also one of the clearest signals that separates who's a mid level candidate here, who's a senior level candidate.

149
00:17:26,734 --> 00:17:30,084
This is where you discuss things like single points of failure.

150
00:17:30,352 --> 00:17:35,972
You find them and propose solutions on what we can do to fix this single point of failure.

151
00:17:36,430 --> 00:17:39,460
This is where you will talk about fallbacks and failures.

152
00:17:39,822 --> 00:17:47,108
For example, whether you have mechanisms to fall back to a slower but functional path in case something goes wrong.

153
00:17:47,470 --> 00:17:55,428
Here you can also expect to talk about redundancy like multi-availability zone replicas for high availability,

154
00:17:55,792 --> 00:18:03,748
application servers to auto-scale across availability zones, distributed caching with replication, and so on.

155
00:18:04,334 --> 00:18:11,072
Another point is whether you can spot how you will be monitoring, logging, and alerting issues for this system.

156
00:18:11,232 --> 00:18:16,356
This part is often overlooked, but it's a strong signal that you think like a senior engineer.

157
00:18:16,686 --> 00:18:24,752
This is where you would cover API latency as well, error rates and thresholds, what alerts you will set up for this type of system,

158
00:18:24,912 --> 00:18:25,380
and so on.

159
00:18:25,998 --> 00:18:34,180
And again, it is always a good idea to check in with the interviewer whether there are specific failure scenarios, for example, if you were discussing failures

160
00:18:34,672 --> 00:18:36,740
that they would like you to address.

161
00:18:36,974 --> 00:18:44,228
And same goes for high-level design, whether you covered everything they were looking for, and same goes for database and API design.

162
00:18:44,910 --> 00:18:49,028
The last part is where you can expect to discuss trade-offs and extensions.

163
00:18:49,312 --> 00:18:56,004
So this might be an open-ended question, like whether you can spot some things that can be improved in the system.

164
00:18:56,462 --> 00:19:06,564
You usually wrap up by summarizing the key decisions you made, what you prioritized for this system, what you sacrificed because it's always about trade-offs in system design,

165
00:19:07,072 --> 00:19:11,780
and you should always be explicit about the why behind the sacrifices.

166
00:19:12,558 --> 00:19:18,372
And this part is also to see whether you will propose some possible extensions for the future.

167
00:19:18,958 --> 00:19:23,876
For example, if you had more time and the load scaled to 10x or more,

168
00:19:24,526 --> 00:19:27,972
what would you do to extend this service or system?

169
00:19:28,430 --> 00:19:36,740
So this is for you to show that you're thinking beyond the problem that was discussed here. So, what if we scale this? What if something goes wrong?

170
00:19:37,166 --> 00:19:41,872
And remember that you cannot propose a perfect design in 60 minutes.

171
00:19:41,952 --> 00:19:45,060
So, of course, there are always things that you can improve.

172
00:19:45,518 --> 00:19:50,436
The goal here is not to check whether your design was perfect or not, because it is not.

173
00:19:50,832 --> 00:19:57,796
You just need to show that you could extend this if you had more time, you had more ideas on how to improve this system.

174
00:19:58,478 --> 00:20:06,340
Now that's the five step system to manage your time, but let's also come back to these five dimensions that you're being evaluated on

175
00:20:06,766 --> 00:20:11,524
and see what you should do and what you should avoid doing during the interview.

176
00:20:12,014 --> 00:20:16,592
So, first thing I recommend you do is ask clarifying questions before designing.

177
00:20:16,752 --> 00:20:17,956
This is the first step.

178
00:20:18,272 --> 00:20:25,988
So if you proceed, jump into a solution before understanding the scope, that is already a red flag for the interviewer.

179
00:20:26,112 --> 00:20:30,948
So always this is a mandatory step before you get into designing anything.

180
00:20:31,470 --> 00:20:36,068
Another common issue I see is engineers get stuck into one of these extremes.

181
00:20:36,432 --> 00:20:48,752
They either stay silent and draw diagrams without talking to the interviewer, or they talk all the time and try to fill in all the silence without inviting the interviewer

182
00:20:48,992 --> 00:20:49,828
for their opinion.

183
00:20:50,478 --> 00:20:54,308
What you should do instead is treat the interviewer as your colleague.

184
00:20:54,672 --> 00:21:00,992
This is like two software engineers in the same company who are working on a solution together.

185
00:21:01,230 --> 00:21:10,564
So after each major decision or each of the phases, it's a good idea to first double check whether this is what they were looking to get out of this part.

186
00:21:11,232 --> 00:21:20,112
And if you missed anything, for example, if it was functional requirements that you were clarifying, whether there are some more things that you didn't ask for,

187
00:21:20,654 --> 00:21:23,204
but those are still expected to be part of the system.

188
00:21:23,712 --> 00:21:32,324
The other thing is always suggest multiple approaches before committing, especially when there are multiple ways to go about things.

189
00:21:32,592 --> 00:21:41,952
For example, if you chose SQL, and even if it's a very obvious choice, for example, it was let's say payment system

190
00:21:42,352 --> 00:21:45,552
where consistency is the most important thing.

191
00:21:46,286 --> 00:21:50,656
If you choose SQL without explaining why, we just assume you didn't know.

192
00:21:50,830 --> 00:21:57,124
Or the interviewer then has to follow up and ask a question why you chose SQL, but they may also not.

193
00:21:57,376 --> 00:22:06,020
So if you don't explain, they would assume you don't know. If you chose SQL, still mention why you didn't go with something like No SQL.

194
00:22:06,256 --> 00:22:09,060
And what are the trade-offs between those solutions?

195
00:22:09,550 --> 00:22:15,972
And same goes for any other decision that you will make where there are more than one approaches to the problem.

196
00:22:16,590 --> 00:22:22,896
So this also ties to the trade-offs, which is you should always explain trade-offs when picking any technology.

197
00:22:23,056 --> 00:22:28,836
If you just pick them without justifying the choice, then it's again another red flag during the interview.

198
00:22:29,294 --> 00:22:35,044
And this last one overlaps with the second point a bit, which is you need to check in at each major step.

199
00:22:35,502 --> 00:22:43,684
For example, if you did the high-level design and you just move on without asking if there are things we forgot to address, then there might be things that you miss

200
00:22:43,856 --> 00:22:44,676
during this step.

201
00:22:45,486 --> 00:22:56,580
So to summarize, use this framework to manage your time and remember to always involve the interviewer in the discussions whenever you're working on any of the stages of this interview.

202
00:22:57,262 --> 00:23:05,536
Remember that this is a conversation between two engineers, not a monologue where you talk all the time, and also not a take-home assessment

203
00:23:05,696 --> 00:23:10,052
where you just work on the solution without communicating with them.

204
00:23:10,446 --> 00:23:20,452
And to quickly recap what are these five steps, it's first to clarify the requirements, functional and non-functional, and understand what's in scope and what's out of scope.

205
00:23:21,038 --> 00:23:24,772
Next you get to the models of data and API layers.

206
00:23:25,326 --> 00:23:32,996
Then you come up with the high-level design with all the components that are involved and how the data flows for read and write operations.

207
00:23:34,062 --> 00:23:38,980
Next, you discuss the scale, failure modes, and reliability of the system.

208
00:23:39,456 --> 00:23:47,936
And last part is usually open-ended, which is where you reflect on the system you built, what are the trade-offs you made, and how

209
00:23:48,096 --> 00:23:50,244
would you improve this if you had more time.

210
00:23:50,734 --> 00:23:59,776
Building a music streaming platform like Spotify is a classic system design problem, and that is because it includes audio delivery, metadata management,

211
00:23:59,936 --> 00:24:04,932
object storage, and everything in between that you need to know to design things from scratch.

212
00:24:05,422 --> 00:24:13,572
And especially if you're a mid-level developer who struggles to design systems or features from scratch, then this will be very helpful for you.

213
00:24:14,030 --> 00:24:17,188
So let's see how we can design this in seven simple steps.

214
00:24:17,774 --> 00:24:26,256
First, we'll start by understanding the requirements and planning the capacity, then we'll come up with the high-level architecture and the system

215
00:24:26,416 --> 00:24:34,148
flow of read and write operations, after which we'll need to design the API and also the storage and database.

216
00:24:34,816 --> 00:24:42,756
And then finally, we'll move on to scalability and understanding how we can scale this system for let's say 50 million users or above.

217
00:24:43,374 --> 00:24:51,136
And at the end, I will also show you the pyramid that separates senior engineers from mid-level developers so that you can understand what are the skills that

218
00:24:51,136 --> 00:24:59,940
you need to learn to be able to design such systems from scratch, no matter if it's system design for Spotify or for Google Doc or for any other system.

219
00:25:00,526 --> 00:25:08,016
Let's start from understanding requirements as a first step because without this we cannot move any further if we don't know what we are building here.

220
00:25:08,176 --> 00:25:18,564
So we are looking to build a music streaming platform for about 500,000 users, and it will handle over like 30 million songs in this platform.

221
00:25:18,926 --> 00:25:25,188
So the core requirements here will be that artists need to be able to upload their songs to the platform.

222
00:25:25,678 --> 00:25:32,068
On the other end, users need to be able to search and play the songs that are uploaded by these artists.

223
00:25:32,576 --> 00:25:37,316
They need to be able also to create and manage playlists on their accounts.

224
00:25:38,094 --> 00:25:47,072
For this, we also need to have a profile for users so that they can maintain their own playlists and some basic monitoring and observability

225
00:25:47,232 --> 00:25:51,844
like health checks for the servers, error tracking, performance metrics, and so on.

226
00:25:52,398 --> 00:26:01,380
For audio formats, we will use OGG and AAC type of files, which are just audio file formats that compress the music without losing the quality,

227
00:26:01,552 --> 00:26:03,364
and they also take up less space.

228
00:26:03,918 --> 00:26:12,592
For example, we can use 64 kilobits per second for mobile data saving and 128 for standard quality, and for premium

229
00:26:12,832 --> 00:26:16,004
users we can deliver 320 kilobits per second.

230
00:26:16,558 --> 00:26:22,532
And on average, we also need to know the size of these files, like what is the average size of a music file.

231
00:26:22,894 --> 00:26:35,108
And let's assume that for normal audio quality, which is the 128 kilobits per second, it is 3 megabytes on average, but this is also something you need to clarify before moving on to the next stage.

232
00:26:35,438 --> 00:26:43,792
As we have the requirements, the next step is to plan the capacity of this system, and especially for song storage, strong metadata,

233
00:26:44,112 --> 00:26:48,484
and also the user metadata and daily bandwidth that we'll be dealing with.

234
00:26:49,038 --> 00:26:57,792
For song storage, since we assumed that 3 MB is the average size of a song, and we have 30 million songs that we are working with,

235
00:26:58,032 --> 00:27:07,364
this translates to 90 terabytes on average of raw audio data, and this doesn't include the replicas across different regions,

236
00:27:07,632 --> 00:27:10,432
which might be duplicates of the same song.

237
00:27:10,672 --> 00:27:13,552
and it also doesn't include the versioning overhead.

238
00:27:13,712 --> 00:27:21,092
when artists re-upload the songs for certain reasons. Let's say they want to change something and they just end up re-uploading the same song.

239
00:27:21,550 --> 00:27:29,476
So that's why we will also need to estimate this like at least two or three times higher than the amount that we have here, which is 90 terabytes.

240
00:27:30,350 --> 00:27:37,892
For song metadata, let's say each song needs a title, artist references, duration, file URLs, and so on.

241
00:27:38,350 --> 00:27:44,004
So we will need at least like 100 bytes per song to store the metadata on average.

242
00:27:44,398 --> 00:27:51,588
And if we assume that it's 100 bytes, then we'll need to multiply that by the 30 million, which will end up being three gigabytes.

243
00:27:51,952 --> 00:27:56,516
So this is practically like nothing compared to the song storage, as you can see.

244
00:27:56,832 --> 00:28:03,172
The main storage that we'll be dealing with in this system is the song storage, which will be an object storage.

245
00:28:03,406 --> 00:28:11,472
For user metadata, again, this won't be something huge, like let's say we need to obtain the user profiles, preferences, and playlist data.

246
00:28:11,822 --> 00:28:21,792
If we assume it's like 1 kilobytes per user profile data that we'll be storing, and we have the 500k users, which we clarified in the first requirements

247
00:28:22,032 --> 00:28:22,352
here.

248
00:28:22,638 --> 00:28:30,276
That means that we'll be dealing with 500 times 1 kilobyte, which will be just half gigabytes of storage for profiles.

249
00:28:30,894 --> 00:28:35,076
And the fourth thing we need to estimate is the daily bandwidth that we'll be using.

250
00:28:35,312 --> 00:28:38,980
The average listen time, let's say, is 3.5 minutes.

251
00:28:39,152 --> 00:28:43,204
This is also something that needs to be clarified in the previous step.

252
00:28:43,472 --> 00:28:52,452
And if you are using the 128 to 160 kilobits per second, that is roughly 3 to 4 megabytes per streaming.

253
00:28:52,912 --> 00:29:00,164
And let's assume each user streams like 10 to 15 songs daily, this will lead to significant egress costs.

254
00:29:00,366 --> 00:29:07,044
Egress costs are the fees that cloud providers charge when data leaves their network to reach to the users.

255
00:29:07,694 --> 00:29:14,980
Overall, the key insight that we get from the capacity planning is that audio will dominate both storage costs and bandwidth.

256
00:29:15,232 --> 00:29:19,588
The metadata is just a small part or portion of this system.

257
00:29:20,174 --> 00:29:25,764
So with that, we can move on to the high level architecture, like a very high level architecture for now.

258
00:29:26,190 --> 00:29:31,152
We'll need to have some sort of mobile app or let's say the web interface of the application.

259
00:29:31,312 --> 00:29:38,820
This will be the user facing app that handles the UI, the user searches, the playback controls, and playlist management.

260
00:29:39,662 --> 00:29:49,764
It will make HTTP calls to the REST APIs, but before that we will have a load balancer that will distribute these incoming requests across multiple API servers,

261
00:29:50,062 --> 00:29:52,740
and these servers will be the RESTful APIs.

262
00:29:52,912 --> 00:30:00,036
As an algorithm for the load balancer, we can use either round robin or we can use the list connections algorithm.

263
00:30:00,462 --> 00:30:07,492
And the load balancer will also make the health checks of the API servers and make sure that they are always available.

264
00:30:08,110 --> 00:30:11,620
Next, we have the API layer where we have our RESTful APIs.

265
00:30:12,174 --> 00:30:19,972
Here, as a validation, we can use JWT tokens and query the database song for metadata and URLs.

266
00:30:20,494 --> 00:30:27,812
We'll use an SQL database for storing the metadata, and these servers will be querying it from the SQL database.

267
00:30:28,302 --> 00:30:35,472
But for audio files, we'll need some sort of blob storage to store these files since they are the biggest portion of our system.

268
00:30:35,712 --> 00:30:44,708
And this will be queried with generated signed URLs, which is a one-time URL that expires after some time so that we keep the URLs safe

269
00:30:44,912 --> 00:30:48,032
and they are not always accessible through the same URL.

270
00:30:48,272 --> 00:31:00,644
An example of this blob storage can be like AWS's S3 storage, which is a way for storing large files like the OGG files that we need to store as part of this Spotify design.

271
00:31:01,072 --> 00:31:11,204
And these types of blob storages offer virtually unlimited scalability, and it also comes with built-in durability and cost effectiveness for the large files.

272
00:31:11,312 --> 00:31:15,364
That's why this is a good solution for storing this type of audio files.

273
00:31:16,462 --> 00:31:22,032
With this high-level design, now we can think about the read and write workflows of the system.

274
00:31:22,460 --> 00:31:26,884
And Let's start from the read workflow, which is users playing a song.

275
00:31:27,694 --> 00:31:33,412
So the very first step is user hits the play button and they play a song in the mobile app.

276
00:31:33,966 --> 00:31:37,712
This will make a request like get to songs/slash ID.

277
00:31:37,792 --> 00:31:42,116
This is also something we need to design in the API design very soon in the next step.

278
00:31:42,702 --> 00:31:48,196
And then after that, we'll hit the load balancer, which will route the request to the API server.

279
00:31:48,718 --> 00:31:54,468
API server as a first step it will validate whether the user is authenticated with the JWT token.

280
00:31:55,022 --> 00:32:01,028
And if they are authenticated, then we'll get into this block where we will be starting to play the song.

281
00:32:01,454 --> 00:32:08,868
So it will first query the song metadata from SQL database where we store the metadata of the song and user profiles.

282
00:32:09,486 --> 00:32:11,268
It will return the song details.

283
00:32:11,566 --> 00:32:16,484
Once we have the metadata, we will also generate the signed URL here in the API server.

284
00:32:16,912 --> 00:32:22,532
And then we will return the metadata plus the signed URL of the song to the mobile app.

285
00:32:23,182 --> 00:32:29,700
Then mobile app will range requests for audio chunks to the blob storage using the signed URL.

286
00:32:30,158 --> 00:32:33,796
And blob storage will stream the audio back to the mobile app.

287
00:32:34,254 --> 00:32:38,084
Let's say we are also tracking some analytics for each played song.

288
00:32:38,414 --> 00:32:46,672
For that reason, we will also make another post request to songs slash id of the song slash play to track the analytics,

289
00:32:46,912 --> 00:32:50,992
and here we can send the metadata that we want to store for later.

290
00:32:51,342 --> 00:33:00,900
And we will return authentication error in case the JWT is invalid, so all of the steps above won't be handled if the user is not authenticated.

291
00:33:01,518 --> 00:33:09,924
Next we have the write workflow of this same system, which is artists uploading a media file, which is the audio file.

292
00:33:10,542 --> 00:33:13,060
So the first point again is the mobile app.

293
00:33:13,232 --> 00:33:24,836
It will make a post request to store the song with metadata, and this will again reach to the load balancer first, then it will forward the upload request to the API server.

294
00:33:25,550 --> 00:33:28,708
In the API we will likely have some file size limits.

295
00:33:28,846 --> 00:33:32,452
So we will first validate the file format and also the size.

296
00:33:32,752 --> 00:33:36,100
And if it is valid, then we will continue with the next steps.

297
00:33:36,782 --> 00:33:44,580
If it is invalid, then we will just return the rejected payload with unsupported format or unsupported size of the file.

298
00:33:45,038 --> 00:33:52,164
Otherwise, as a first step, we need to store the audio file in the blob storage. So we'll send it to the blob storage which we are using.

299
00:33:52,750 --> 00:34:01,152
And then after that, we will extract the audio metadata like the duration, the byte rate, and also the other things which we will get in the post

300
00:34:01,326 --> 00:34:04,292
body, like the title of the song, and so on.

301
00:34:04,814 --> 00:34:11,972
And once we have all the metadata like the song type, the artist, the title, we can insert this into the SQL database.

302
00:34:13,166 --> 00:34:24,228
Also, once it is uploaded, we can also move it by hierarchical structure if we want to, like if we want to store the file inside of the artists, then by artist ID,

303
00:34:24,544 --> 00:34:28,996
then the album, then the album ID, we can do that also in the blob storage.

304
00:34:29,454 --> 00:34:33,344
Technically, the object storages don't have hierarchical structures.

305
00:34:33,584 --> 00:34:41,284
This is just an illusion that you have it, but this is also useful for us to keep track of things like they are in a folder format.

306
00:34:41,966 --> 00:34:47,184
Then if we are going to use any sort of CDN, we'll need also some CDN invalidation.

307
00:34:47,502 --> 00:34:57,668
CDN here will be used for caching copies of the content like artist pages, album metadata, song details, which will be closer to the users for fast delivery.

308
00:34:58,062 --> 00:35:04,772
And it will also clear the old cached data so users see the latest songs and artist updates right away.

309
00:35:05,104 --> 00:35:10,884
And that will also be used in this request to make sure we are keeping everything up to date in the CDNs.

310
00:35:11,438 --> 00:35:18,436
And lastly, we will also send a response to the mobile app notifying that the upload has been successful.

311
00:35:19,086 --> 00:35:24,944
The next step for us will be to design this API and the database in a bit more detail.

312
00:35:25,104 --> 00:35:27,204
So let's start from the API design.

313
00:35:27,854 --> 00:35:33,744
For the search and discovery, we'll need a way to search different content types with pagination.

314
00:35:34,190 --> 00:35:44,164
Let's say user searches for a specific song, we will need to make a request to slash search endpoint and provide the query, which will be the exact thing they search for.

315
00:35:44,718 --> 00:35:51,012
And here we can pass more details, like if we know that the user is searching for a song, we can pass the type as song,

316
00:35:51,470 --> 00:36:00,036
and we'll pass the limit, which can be like 20 by default, and the offset, which means that from where we need to start the counter in the songs.

317
00:36:00,686 --> 00:36:07,332
Next, we will also get the trending songs, and optionally this can also be filtered by genre or by some other things.

318
00:36:07,504 --> 00:36:15,364
So it will be a GET request to slash songs slash trending, and then we can pass the additional filters if we want to,

319
00:36:15,744 --> 00:36:19,876
and the limit on how many songs we want to query from the API.

320
00:36:20,782 --> 00:36:24,624
Users will also need to get all songs by a specific artist.

321
00:36:24,784 --> 00:36:31,204
Let's say they are browsing the songs for a specific artist and they want to see the songs only of this artist.

322
00:36:31,694 --> 00:36:41,380
For that, we'll need to have an endpoint for slash artists slash ID of the artist and then slash songs, which will return the songs that only belong to this artist,

323
00:36:41,904 --> 00:36:45,540
and the limit as always to limit the response from the API.

324
00:36:46,254 --> 00:36:49,156
Next, we need to also control the content access.

325
00:36:49,646 --> 00:36:55,556
The first endpoint here will be to get the song metadata and the URL of the audio file.

326
00:36:55,950 --> 00:37:02,884
It will be a GET request to slash songs slash ID of the song, which will return all the metadata and the streaming URL.

327
00:37:03,246 --> 00:37:13,764
We also need to have a direct streaming endpoint like slash songs slash ID of the song and then slash something like stream, which will be an alternative to the signed URLs.

328
00:37:14,414 --> 00:37:22,304
And we need to also design for the playlist since the users can maintain playlists and they will be querying it back optionally

329
00:37:22,464 --> 00:37:25,988
with also the song list that is included in the playlist.

330
00:37:26,542 --> 00:37:32,644
So it can be a GET request to slash playlists slash ID, which will just return the playlist details.

331
00:37:33,166 --> 00:37:40,356
And then if you also include the query like include songs, then it will also return the songs that are included in this playlist.

332
00:37:40,942 --> 00:37:44,132
Now let's see what are the user actions that we can expect.

333
00:37:44,366 --> 00:37:48,144
For example, regular users will also be creating playlists, right?

334
00:37:48,238 --> 00:37:51,972
So for that we need some sort of post endpoint to slash playlists.

335
00:37:52,590 --> 00:38:01,156
As a metadata, we can expect like something simple like just the name of the playlist and whether it is public or only available to this user.

336
00:38:01,774 --> 00:38:10,544
Then once they create it, they will be also adding songs to it, which will be a put request to the same playlist with the ID and then slash songs.

337
00:38:10,624 --> 00:38:19,264
And here we can include the IDs of the songs, like some new songs that they are adding to this playlist, and the position where we are adding this.

338
00:38:19,344 --> 00:38:30,084
Let's say they already have four songs in the playlist or five songs, and then we are adding from position of five or six, so we will be sending the position along with it as well.

339
00:38:30,574 --> 00:38:38,620
Similarly, they will also remove a song from the playlist, which will be the same request URL, but at the end we will also pass the song it

340
00:38:38,864 --> 00:38:40,996
that we want to remove from this playlist.

341
00:38:41,584 --> 00:38:50,464
And they can also like or unlike a song so that it is added to their favorites, and that will be done with a post request to slash songs,

342
00:38:50,702 --> 00:38:56,676
slash ID of that song, and slash like which will be maintaining their favorites in the database.

343
00:38:57,582 --> 00:39:00,624
And lastly, we'll need some way to manage the users.

344
00:39:00,814 --> 00:39:08,932
For example, we will need to fetch the current users' playlists with some requests like get to slash users slash me slash playlists.

345
00:39:09,264 --> 00:39:14,404
And we can also limit this with some limit if the playlists are too many for the user.

346
00:39:15,086 --> 00:39:18,144
Similarly, we need the songs liked by the user.

347
00:39:18,304 --> 00:39:20,548
So here they are adding it to their favorites.

348
00:39:21,038 --> 00:39:32,452
Here we can use like users slash me slash either likes or liked songs, but whatever we are using here, it will be also nice if we use the same plural here as well, so that it's

349
00:39:33,294 --> 00:39:35,396
following the restful API principles.

350
00:39:35,854 --> 00:39:42,372
And then the limit, which can be like 50 or something else, and the offset where we are starting to retrieve this from.

351
00:39:42,734 --> 00:39:50,944
And they need to also be able to follow artists to be notified from their latest songs, which will be done with a post request to the users

352
00:39:51,214 --> 00:39:56,900
slash me slash follow, and then we will pass the artist ID to follow this specific artist.

353
00:39:57,422 --> 00:39:59,824
That is it for the API basic design.

354
00:39:59,904 --> 00:40:02,532
Now we can move on to the data storage.

355
00:40:02,926 --> 00:40:05,584
First, let's talk about the blob storage we will be using.

356
00:40:05,664 --> 00:40:10,404
Let's say it's an S3 in AWS or it can be like Azure's blob storage.

357
00:40:10,862 --> 00:40:22,724
We will need to use this for storing the audio files because such large files cannot be stored in SQL or some simple databases. So we need some blob storage or object storage for this.

358
00:40:23,310 --> 00:40:28,624
The audio files here are immutable, meaning once you upload them, you cannot change them.

359
00:40:28,814 --> 00:40:34,724
So if you need to make a change to the uploaded file, you need to re-upload it and remove the old one.

360
00:40:35,214 --> 00:40:39,824
And we can also organize them in a hierarchical structure as we saw before.

361
00:40:39,904 --> 00:40:47,876
So we can store them like artist slash the artist ID slash album slash album ID and then slash the song ID.

362
00:40:48,206 --> 00:40:51,780
This will be easier for us for retrieving the files later.

363
00:40:52,302 --> 00:40:58,500
As a database, we will be using some sort of relational database like either PostgreSQL or MySQL.

364
00:40:58,944 --> 00:41:02,436
And this is the basic structure we will have in the database.

365
00:41:02,830 --> 00:41:11,424
Let's start with users, where we will be storing the user ID and some metadata about them, like the email, the password hash created

366
00:41:11,584 --> 00:41:20,384
at, which will be the creation or like the sign up date for this user, when they last logged in, subscription type and country.

367
00:41:20,544 --> 00:41:26,180
and this can be many more metadata, but this is just the bare minimum that we need to have.

368
00:41:26,830 --> 00:41:32,644
And with the user ID, we will have a foreign key linked to the owner ID of playlists.

369
00:41:33,326 --> 00:41:39,556
So since we will have playlists, we also need to have the owner of which playlist belongs to which user.

370
00:41:40,110 --> 00:41:45,476
And we will also have the playlist ID for linking this to the playlist items.

371
00:41:46,126 --> 00:41:49,508
We need the name and create it for this playlist.

372
00:41:49,966 --> 00:41:52,864
Then each playlist will have some songs in it, right?

373
00:41:53,024 --> 00:41:55,396
So here we can use a join table.

374
00:41:55,694 --> 00:42:04,224
This can be something like playlist items, which will contain first of all the playlist ID and also the song ID and the position,

375
00:42:04,384 --> 00:42:11,780
like where is this song positioned in the playlist, and when is it added to the playlist if we need to also maintain that?

376
00:42:12,142 --> 00:42:17,092
This will basically be the table that joins the playlists and the songs together.

377
00:42:17,806 --> 00:42:25,156
For the songs, again we will have the ID, which is the most important thing here, and some metadata like the title, duration, and so on.

378
00:42:25,582 --> 00:42:34,276
And another important part here is the file URL, which we'll be using to query the audio file to stream it from the blob storage.

379
00:42:35,022 --> 00:42:46,384
Then we will also have artists which we can store in another type of table other than the users for keeping this separate, so we can have the artist ID and then the metadata about them, like the name,

380
00:42:46,544 --> 00:42:47,556
country, and so on.

381
00:42:48,078 --> 00:42:51,524
And we'll need to connect the artists to the songs.

382
00:42:51,758 --> 00:43:00,132
For that, we can use another join table like artist songs, where we can keep track of most importantly the song ID that they uploaded

383
00:43:00,558 --> 00:43:04,260
and the artist ID, like which song belongs to which artist.

384
00:43:04,846 --> 00:43:11,812
And additionally, we can store some more metadata here, but this is the bare minimum that we need to have in this table.

385
00:43:12,558 --> 00:43:14,224
And why we choose SQL here?

386
00:43:14,464 --> 00:43:20,292
Because we need a lot of joints, as you can see, for playlists and songs, for songs and artists.

387
00:43:20,544 --> 00:43:25,188
So this is a natural selection for us to go with SQL type of database.

388
00:43:26,062 --> 00:43:34,852
We can do even more complex queries here, like find all songs by artists that a user follows, or some other complex queries like that,

389
00:43:35,024 --> 00:43:37,860
and that is possible with SQL type of databases.

390
00:43:38,990 --> 00:43:45,060
And once the database scrolls, we'll need to also add indexes for the important properties here,

391
00:43:45,838 --> 00:43:48,388
which will be the frequently accessed columns.

392
00:43:48,718 --> 00:43:53,728
And in our case, we can, for example, index the title in the song table.

393
00:43:53,902 --> 00:43:57,028
We can index the name in the artists table.

394
00:43:57,710 --> 00:44:04,100
And we can also index the song IDs in this join table where we'll be joining the playlists and songs.

395
00:44:04,910 --> 00:44:12,688
These indexes will enable us to have fast search capabilities, which will also handle the typos and partial matches that

396
00:44:12,848 --> 00:44:16,292
user expects when they are searching for a song or for a playlist.

397
00:44:17,038 --> 00:44:25,328
And the playlist item index will speed up the queries when fetching songs for a specific playlist or finding which playlists contain

398
00:44:25,488 --> 00:44:26,532
a specific song.

399
00:44:27,214 --> 00:44:32,068
That's it for the planning of general object storage and also the SQL database.

400
00:44:32,654 --> 00:44:41,408
Now let's see how we can scale this. Let's say the user base scrolls to 50 million users now and the songs grow to 200 million songs.

401
00:44:41,568 --> 00:44:49,648
This means our current architecture cannot handle it more, which means if we calculate the requirements again, we will have 100 bytes

402
00:44:49,888 --> 00:44:54,244
for 200 million songs for song metadata, that will be 20 gigabytes.

403
00:44:54,894 --> 00:45:00,036
For user metadata, it will be 1 kilobyte times the user base, which will be 50 gigabytes.

404
00:45:00,622 --> 00:45:05,476
But for audio storage, you can see we estimated 3 megabytes per audio file.

405
00:45:05,870 --> 00:45:14,468
And if you multiply that by the amount of song, which is now 200 million, let's say, it will be equal to something like 600 terabytes,

406
00:45:14,768 --> 00:45:21,568
and this is not even including the replicas, the regional copies or the multi-file uploads by artists.

407
00:45:21,742 --> 00:45:25,060
So in reality, this will be like two or three times more.

408
00:45:25,646 --> 00:45:28,420
So let's see how our scale phase might look like.

409
00:45:28,686 --> 00:45:34,128
As a first step, we can add CDN edges for different regions of the world.

410
00:45:34,350 --> 00:45:39,648
For example, we can have one for North America, we can have in Europe, we can have in South America.

411
00:45:39,808 --> 00:45:43,524
This will speed up the process of retrieving it from CDN.

412
00:45:43,854 --> 00:45:47,568
And similar to that, we can have load balancers in different regions.

413
00:45:47,662 --> 00:45:56,448
Let's say we have at least two load balancers in two different regions that are still mapping the incoming requests to the API servers that we have.

414
00:45:56,654 --> 00:46:04,036
Before getting to API servers, for the CDNs, we need to use some sort of eviction policy for cache management.

415
00:46:04,448 --> 00:46:16,208
And here it's a good use case to use the LRU cache method, which is automatically removing the least accessed content when storage fills up, and the cache would fetch from the origin

416
00:46:16,368 --> 00:46:17,828
storage on a cache miss.

417
00:46:18,528 --> 00:46:26,528
For the API servers, since we are not maintaining any type of user session or any storage, these are completely stateless,

418
00:46:26,688 --> 00:46:31,652
meaning we can scale them as many as needed to handle the incoming traffic.

419
00:46:32,174 --> 00:46:34,928
So adding more capacity here is straightforward.

420
00:46:35,008 --> 00:46:38,052
We are just replicating the existing API servers.

421
00:46:38,638 --> 00:46:48,196
The main thing here is that when designing the API server, we need to design for truly stateless servers so that it's possible for us to scale this easily.

422
00:46:48,368 --> 00:47:00,164
Next, we need to also scale the database because the single instance cannot handle that many metadata storage, which means we need to separate the reads and the writes in the databases.

423
00:47:00,368 --> 00:47:09,732
For the writes, we can use a leader replica where we will be writing the new files of the songs or new playlists that users are creating.

424
00:47:10,158 --> 00:47:17,248
But when reading it, we can have some slave or follower databases which will be only used for reading.

425
00:47:17,422 --> 00:47:20,516
So this will be in sync with the leader database.

426
00:47:21,008 --> 00:47:30,692
But whenever we get any read operations like getting the song metadata, getting the playlist metadata, we will be reading them from the follower databases.

427
00:47:31,630 --> 00:47:36,176
As a next step, if this is not enough, we can also consider sharding the database.

428
00:47:36,366 --> 00:47:40,016
Sharding will split the data across many database instances.

429
00:47:40,176 --> 00:47:46,496
Let's say we will have one shard for the US users and one shard for the European users.

430
00:47:46,656 --> 00:47:52,612
We can split it by geographically, and then we will partition songs by the artist ID ranges.

431
00:47:53,294 --> 00:47:59,780
This will give us horizontal scalability and it will improve the performance by keeping the related data together.

432
00:48:00,096 --> 00:48:08,896
And also for the blob storage, we can replicate this to be in different regions, like for Europe users, they will be uploading to a blob storage

433
00:48:09,056 --> 00:48:10,576
that is in Europe region.

434
00:48:10,976 --> 00:48:20,292
For North America, they will be uploading songs to the blob storage that is in their own region, and so on for the other geographics in the world.

435
00:48:20,576 --> 00:48:27,204
And all of them will still be using signed URLs for accessing the audio files and streaming the music chunks.

436
00:48:27,886 --> 00:48:34,084
Of course, there are many things that can be improved on this system, like improving the reliability or monitoring,

437
00:48:34,256 --> 00:48:43,296
but the key as you can see is starting simple, we don't want to over-engineer for small amount of users and then gradually increasing the scale as we go.

438
00:48:43,470 --> 00:48:53,700
And lastly, let's see how you can also be able to design such systems or features from scratch, and what's the real difference that separates senior engineers from mid-level developers.

439
00:48:54,286 --> 00:49:02,468
If you think the designing part as this pyramid, on the bottom, which is the main like most important part, is the planning,

440
00:49:02,656 --> 00:49:10,976
which is the blueprinting phase, the technical design, understanding requirements, as you saw, estimating capacity, choosing the architecture.

441
00:49:11,216 --> 00:49:15,716
This is where everyone needs to start when designing such large-scale systems.

442
00:49:16,206 --> 00:49:27,536
And only then we move on to the tech stack selection, which is like picking the right database, whether it's SQL or no SQL, and if it's SQL, then what exact type of SQL database?

443
00:49:27,662 --> 00:49:35,492
It will be the caching layers, eviction policies, storage solutions, and all of that tech related stock selection.

444
00:49:36,206 --> 00:49:45,700
And only last stage, which is the most smallest part actually at this designing, is the coding, which is like starting to implement the things.

445
00:49:46,190 --> 00:49:49,936
But that is actually the thing that requires the least brain power.

446
00:49:50,350 --> 00:49:58,336
And especially if coding is the only thing that you can do, then we have now these cloud or GBT models which can do it faster,

447
00:49:58,496 --> 00:50:05,856
better, and cheaper. So, what's the point of any company to pay you if the only thing that you can do is coding?

448
00:50:06,016 --> 00:50:13,936
That is where the most mid-level developers are stuck because they have this completely upside down, they start developing things

449
00:50:14,336 --> 00:50:20,896
without even having any plan, without having any text-tack selection, they just start coding and see where it takes them.

450
00:50:21,166 --> 00:50:30,948
Whereas the fundamental stickers, who learn to think through problems before even touching a keyboard or before even starting to build any code,

451
00:50:31,310 --> 00:50:40,576
these are the ones that will never struggle to find roles, because all the companies are always in need for such developers who can think through problems from

452
00:50:40,750 --> 00:50:48,228
users' perspective and also bring rough ideas to reality by understanding the requirements.

453
00:50:48,496 --> 00:50:54,016
By the way, if you'd like to learn all these concepts that I'm using for designing these systems from scratch,

454
00:50:54,176 --> 00:51:01,316
I have a whole system design guide covering all these concepts that you need for system design interviews on my YouTube channel.

455
00:51:01,742 --> 00:51:09,696
You can check out the first link in the description to find the link to my channel, and the first pinned video in my channel is that system

456
00:51:09,696 --> 00:51:14,564
design guide explaining all these concepts you need for passing these system design interviews.

457
00:51:14,766 --> 00:51:22,660
Let's design a hotel reservation system similar to booking.com or Airbnb, which is a typical system design interview problem.

458
00:51:23,118 --> 00:51:34,244
The mistake that most developers make here is when they ask to design such a system, they treat it like a typical CRUD application with some caching and replication for the availability,

459
00:51:34,736 --> 00:51:37,348
but that's not the correct way to think about it.

460
00:51:37,696 --> 00:51:45,296
For instance, one of the problems that you might have is a moment when two users are trying to book the last room available at the same time,

461
00:51:45,518 --> 00:51:51,332
and you try your simple select then insert approach, which creates the double booking problem.

462
00:51:51,726 --> 00:52:01,508
So you end up charging two credit cards for one room, and now you're dealing with refunds, with angry customers, and a system that cannot be trusted for the next time.

463
00:52:01,998 --> 00:52:09,188
That's why hotel reservation systems are hard because they also test the exact skills that separate mid-level developers from seniors.

464
00:52:09,550 --> 00:52:16,900
Things like handling concurrency without breaking it under heavy load, keeping the data consistent across multiple systems,

465
00:52:17,088 --> 00:52:21,636
and also designing for failure in case something goes wrong in parts of the system.

466
00:52:21,808 --> 00:52:31,812
And it's not about memorizing these patterns, but rather understanding the actual problems that might occur, what are the solutions to those, and what are the trade-offs between those solutions.

467
00:52:32,366 --> 00:52:35,204
So we are going to design this system in four simple steps.

468
00:52:35,726 --> 00:52:45,008
First, we will gather the design requirements, estimate the storage and queries per second, transactions per second, and anything else that we need for designing the system.

469
00:52:45,294 --> 00:52:52,048
Then we'll get into the basic high-level design, including the API design, database schema, reservation flows, and so on.

470
00:52:52,270 --> 00:53:03,088
And in the first step, we will scale the system to handle 1000 queries per second and 17 transactions per second, which are the numbers that are close to the real booking.com scale.

471
00:53:03,328 --> 00:53:11,748
And then we will handle the failures under heavy load and discuss the system recovery whenever some things or some parts of the system fail.

472
00:53:12,238 --> 00:53:19,236
So if you're tired of system design explanations that skip the hard part and only give you the surface level, then this one is for you.

473
00:53:19,438 --> 00:53:22,468
Let's start with the first step, which is the design requirements.

474
00:53:22,688 --> 00:53:27,108
In such challenges, we usually follow a framework to come up with the solution.

475
00:53:27,438 --> 00:53:37,604
And the very first step is always to ask clarifying questions to lock in the scope that we are going to design, and then also about functional and non-functional requirements.

476
00:53:38,190 --> 00:53:40,928
So let's start with the clarifying questions that we might have.

477
00:53:41,088 --> 00:53:49,988
For example, one of the questions can be about the payments, which is are we going to allow them to pay at the platform or it is going to be a payment in checking?

478
00:53:50,382 --> 00:53:54,468
And this will let us know whether we support payments in the platform or not.

479
00:53:54,862 --> 00:53:56,128
For this one, let's say yes.

480
00:53:56,208 --> 00:54:01,728
So we are going to allow them to pay at the platform, which means we need to have some sort of payment system inside.

481
00:54:01,888 --> 00:54:04,128
Another question can be about booking channels.

482
00:54:04,208 --> 00:54:13,860
So is this a website only web app where they are going to book the hotels, or we also support the mobile app, which might have different rates and different pricings?

483
00:54:14,382 --> 00:54:19,396
So let's say we are going to design both like web version and mobile version of this application.

484
00:54:19,918 --> 00:54:22,500
Then about cancellations and overbooking.

485
00:54:22,608 --> 00:54:27,492
So do we support cancellations of the reservations and also overbooking?

486
00:54:27,808 --> 00:54:35,248
Because hotels typically allow some sort of overbooking, taking into account that there might be some cancellations.

487
00:54:35,328 --> 00:54:45,156
So if they are getting, let's say, 10 bookings per month, then out of these, like two or three are going to be cancelled, so they allow users to also overbook

488
00:54:45,488 --> 00:54:47,008
because of the cancellations.

489
00:54:47,168 --> 00:54:51,940
Once they get some cancellations, the room is free now, so they will allow overbooking as well.

490
00:54:52,398 --> 00:54:59,588
Then about the dynamic pricing, so are the prices fixed or the price changes based on the demand and the dates.

491
00:55:00,078 --> 00:55:02,756
And for this, let's say we are going to have dynamic pricing.

492
00:55:02,848 --> 00:55:11,088
Whenever the demand is higher, so it's let's say Christmas time, which is around now, we are going to increase the prices.

493
00:55:11,248 --> 00:55:21,860
For example, this room where I'm recording from, it was triple of the price, as in usual, because that is the Christmas time, so they are going to increase the numbers, and similar to that,

494
00:55:22,288 --> 00:55:29,924
any other popular dates that people are reserving, we are going to increase the prices based on the demand that we are getting.

495
00:55:30,350 --> 00:55:32,644
Then we can get to the functional requirements.

496
00:55:33,614 --> 00:55:36,288
So one question might be about the hotel management.

497
00:55:36,462 --> 00:55:44,208
Are we going to support the admin panel like adding, updating, removing hotels, or we are only focused on the user part?

498
00:55:44,494 --> 00:55:52,848
Admin panels might be included in this, but the core functionality evolves usually around the users which are booking the hotels, canceling

499
00:55:53,008 --> 00:55:53,168
them.

500
00:55:53,486 --> 00:55:57,572
Let's say we are going to also support some sort of admin panel in this system.

501
00:55:58,094 --> 00:56:04,484
Then we get to the search and discovery questions, like what are the core functionalities we are going to support.

502
00:56:05,008 --> 00:56:15,088
And a way you can think about this is what are the functionalities that if we remove from booking.com, it doesn't become booking.com anymore.

503
00:56:15,248 --> 00:56:19,268
This might be a good approach for identifying what are the core functionalities.

504
00:56:19,758 --> 00:56:27,648
For example, if users are not able to search hotels by location, then it becomes some sort of useless application, right?

505
00:56:27,822 --> 00:56:35,888
So of course, one of the core functionalities we will support is that users need to be able to search by location, by dates, by guest number,

506
00:56:36,128 --> 00:56:39,364
price range, and some other filters that we might set.

507
00:56:39,886 --> 00:56:50,048
Then about the reservation flow, how usually it happens is users book the rooms, then they view the reservations, they should be able to cancel the bookings

508
00:56:50,208 --> 00:56:52,100
because we also clarified it above.

509
00:56:52,608 --> 00:56:55,684
And also the system should prevent double bookings.

510
00:56:55,808 --> 00:57:00,804
This is one of the challenges we need to identify here so that we also solve it in the next steps.

511
00:57:01,486 --> 00:57:03,232
Then about the payment processing.

512
00:57:03,392 --> 00:57:12,580
Since we clarified that we are going to support payments in the app, then we need to integrate some sort of payment processing like Stripe, PayPal or something else.

513
00:57:13,232 --> 00:57:21,872
And optionally, it might also include notifications, so sending confirmations for bookings or cancellations or payments to both user

514
00:57:22,112 --> 00:57:24,996
and admins brokers who are uploading these hotels.

515
00:57:25,582 --> 00:57:33,604
Then we get into the non-functional requirements, which are more about the consistency, load, and latency that we are going to support.

516
00:57:34,094 --> 00:57:35,952
Let's start with the consistency.

517
00:57:36,192 --> 00:57:45,508
We are going to have strong consistency because two users shouldn't be able to book the same room for the same dates, that is non-negotiable.

518
00:57:46,032 --> 00:57:52,356
But whenever they are searching or discovering the rooms there, the eventual consistency is fine.

519
00:57:52,752 --> 00:57:56,132
And you will see why also when we get to the designing phase.

520
00:57:56,782 --> 00:58:05,604
Then about the concurrency, so we need to handle thousands of users trying to book the same hotel simultaneously and without it breaking the system.

521
00:58:06,032 --> 00:58:18,752
Also, about the latency, we need to provide low latency, so search results should appear under 500 milliseconds, and booking confirmations are fine if they are handled between two

522
00:58:18,912 --> 00:58:19,844
or three seconds.

523
00:58:20,494 --> 00:58:27,620
You should also clarify the availability and scale. Let's say for availability, we are going to support the three nines uptime.

524
00:58:28,032 --> 00:58:35,332
And when it comes to scale, we need to be able to scale this horizontally without redesigning the system from scratch in the future.

525
00:58:35,758 --> 00:58:44,388
Once we gather the design requirements, we can move on to the estimation of data and capacity, and after that, we can start designing the system.

526
00:58:44,750 --> 00:58:55,872
Now, when it comes to the data estimation, the most important things we are interested here is the queries per second that are going to happen in the system and also transactions per second,

527
00:58:55,952 --> 00:58:59,872
which are the rights to the database when users are booking hotels.

528
00:59:00,112 --> 00:59:04,932
So in this kind of system, you can think about it as this funnel here.

529
00:59:05,262 --> 00:59:09,232
The users that come to the platform are at the top here.

530
00:59:09,326 --> 00:59:11,780
So these are the users who are viewing the hotel room.

531
00:59:11,886 --> 00:59:15,300
This is the 100% of the traffic somewhere here.

532
00:59:15,790 --> 00:59:25,380
And then next, we have out of these users, probably like only 10% will go into browsing the hotels and searching for some good

533
00:59:26,112 --> 00:59:28,032
matches and booking the hotel.

534
00:59:28,272 --> 00:59:33,700
So that will be the second layer here. And then third, we will have the reservations.

535
00:59:33,872 --> 00:59:44,192
So only out of this, only the 10% are actually going to book anything because most of the visitors are tire kickers, they are not looking to book anything, they are just browsing,

536
00:59:44,718 --> 00:59:52,740
but we still need to design the systems for them because they are also going to increase the queries per second that are happening in the system.

537
00:59:53,262 --> 00:59:57,380
So, based on this, there are two ways for us to calculate the load.

538
00:59:57,806 --> 01:00:07,232
One approach is, let's say, to take a look at the booking.com, and you can use some sort of extension like SimilarWeb to see the traffic that it's getting per month.

539
01:00:07,312 --> 01:00:12,292
For example, this month it got 500 million visitors in the website.

540
01:00:12,846 --> 01:00:23,428
That means that 500 million users were browsing the system this month, so at the top, and then you can start calculating it down based on this

541
01:00:23,662 --> 01:00:28,432
to see also the transactions per second and what are the queries per second based on this load.

542
01:00:28,686 --> 01:00:40,580
And the other way is the bottom-up approach, which is to get the number of reservations per year, let's say, and for booking.com that is 1.1 billion reservations per year,

543
01:00:41,006 --> 01:00:43,556
and then we can calculate this upwards.

544
01:00:43,822 --> 01:00:50,212
So starting from here, how many visits we get per month or per second to the platform?

545
01:00:50,542 --> 01:00:56,772
And we will also be able to calculate the transactions per second because we have the number for the year reservation.

546
01:00:57,134 --> 01:01:03,588
So each of these numbers is fine, you just need to get one of them from the interviewer, and then you can start the calculations.

547
01:01:04,032 --> 01:01:08,580
And also take into account that these numbers are also rough estimates.

548
01:01:08,752 --> 01:01:18,592
So you might get these numbers exactly to calculate, or you might get just the visits per year and the bookings per year, and you should be able to calculate it after that.

549
01:01:18,832 --> 01:01:21,828
The other important metric that we need is the average stay.

550
01:01:22,032 --> 01:01:25,988
So, what is the average stay that users are booking the hotels?

551
01:01:26,478 --> 01:01:34,468
Let's just say two or three nights per booking. So let's go with the two nights per booking, and then we will be able to calculate the rest.

552
01:01:34,688 --> 01:01:37,156
So let's start with the daily reservations.

553
01:01:37,710 --> 01:01:44,068
We have the number of bookings per year, which is 1.5 billion rooms per night. Let's just start with this approach.

554
01:01:44,528 --> 01:01:51,908
Then we can divide this by the nights per booking, and that comes down to 550 million bookings per year.

555
01:01:52,368 --> 01:01:56,452
Because this is the room nights in total, this is not the bookings.

556
01:01:56,782 --> 01:02:02,884
So we need to divide this by the nights per booking to get the actual number that we are getting booked per year.

557
01:02:03,310 --> 01:02:12,644
Then we can divide this by 365, which is the days in the year, and we'll get the number of bookings per day, which is 1.5 million.

558
01:02:13,168 --> 01:02:21,124
And after that, we can divide the 1.5 million by the seconds per day, which is 86,400,

559
01:02:21,568 --> 01:02:29,284
and that means that we are getting 17 bookings per second, which is the transactions per second that we are going to support in the database.

560
01:02:29,998 --> 01:02:40,388
Then when it comes to the traffic funnel part and the reads, as you saw with similar web, we are getting something like 500 million visits per month in the booking.com

561
01:02:40,848 --> 01:02:49,060
with about 8 pages per visit, which means that on each visit the users are going to browse around 8 pages before they leave the website.

562
01:02:49,582 --> 01:03:00,548
Based on this, we can first get the visits per day, and we can get that by dividing the monthly traffic with 30, which are the days in the month, and we'll get to 17 million visits per day.

563
01:03:01,006 --> 01:03:03,408
After that, we can get the visits per second.

564
01:03:03,488 --> 01:03:10,848
So if we divide that by the seconds in the day, we will get 200 visits per second to our website.

565
01:03:11,008 --> 01:03:19,300
And if we assume that they are going to browse around 8 pages per visit before they leave, let's say that it comes down to 5 back-end reads

566
01:03:19,488 --> 01:03:23,524
per visit, which can be things like search queries, availability checks.

567
01:03:23,982 --> 01:03:33,060
So we need to just increase this number by multiplying it with the 5, and we'll get around 1000 read requests per second.

568
01:03:33,486 --> 01:03:41,328
And based on these calculations, we can clearly see that this is going to be a strong read-heavy system because we have 1000 Qps

569
01:03:41,742 --> 01:03:49,028
queries per second that we need to support and only 17 TPS, which are the transactions per second that we will get.

570
01:03:49,582 --> 01:03:57,988
So our challenge here will be to optimize the architecture for reads while still keeping the writes consistent to avoid the double booking problem.

571
01:03:58,478 --> 01:04:06,208
These are the core numbers we need to start designing the system, and then the rest we can look at while designing the API database,

572
01:04:06,288 --> 01:04:16,004
and if there are any more things that we need, it's fine to calculate those there, but at least we need to get the main things that we need before starting with the high-level design.

573
01:04:16,462 --> 01:04:22,788
After the scale and data size calculations, we can now design an architecture that fits these numbers.

574
01:04:23,406 --> 01:04:32,708
So we can start by using a service-oriented architecture with clear boundaries, but also keep the tightly coupled data in the same database.

575
01:04:33,294 --> 01:04:42,052
So as a starting point, let's say we have the mobile app or the web app where users are browsing these hotels and making the reservations.

576
01:04:42,478 --> 01:04:51,408
We'll use CDN in front here for the static assets like rendering the images, media files, also the JavaScript content

577
01:04:51,568 --> 01:04:53,252
and the hotel images.

578
01:04:53,806 --> 01:04:59,408
Then we have an API gateway, which will be the single entry point to our application.

579
01:04:59,648 --> 01:05:07,556
This is where we will handle the authentication, the rate limiting, and also routing the requests between all the services that we have.

580
01:05:08,110 --> 01:05:12,420
It can also serve as a load balancer across service instances.

581
01:05:12,624 --> 01:05:23,424
So if you have multiple instances of hotel service, let's say, this API gateway can serve as a load balancer and route the traffic between the instance 1 and

582
01:05:23,584 --> 01:05:24,164
instance 2.

583
01:05:24,718 --> 01:05:30,340
When it comes to services, we can separate this with boundaries for hotel service.

584
01:05:30,544 --> 01:05:36,772
This is where we will have hotel and room information, mostly static and heavy cacheable service.

585
01:05:37,184 --> 01:05:40,464
An important service here is a reservation service.

586
01:05:40,624 --> 01:05:46,404
This will handle the booking logic, inventory checks, reservation creation and cancellation.

587
01:05:46,784 --> 01:05:51,396
And this is also where concurrency will be handled in the application.

588
01:05:51,950 --> 01:05:54,052
Next, we have a rate service.

589
01:05:54,784 --> 01:06:02,116
This will be for room pricing by date because depending on the date and when you book the hotel, the price will be different.

590
01:06:02,224 --> 01:06:07,076
So this will be applying dynamic pricing based on the demand of the rooms.

591
01:06:07,598 --> 01:06:16,384
To integrate the payment gateways, we will also need a payment service that will implement, let's say, Stripe payments, PayPal payments, Apple Pay,

592
01:06:16,544 --> 01:06:18,628
Google Pay, and otherment providers.

593
01:06:19,150 --> 01:06:25,904
And we also have a notification service that talks with the reservation service and with the payment service.

594
01:06:26,064 --> 01:06:28,996
This is what sends the emails and push notifications.

595
01:06:29,710 --> 01:06:38,144
For the data layer, initially, we can start by using some sort of SQL database like PostgreSQL, primarily because

596
01:06:38,382 --> 01:06:44,132
we will need the ACID guarantees, and this will be also useful for the transaction store.

597
01:06:44,526 --> 01:06:52,484
And we will be using many transactions, so we need the consistency of SQL databases, that's why we go with SQL in this case.

598
01:06:53,104 --> 01:06:59,172
We also need some sort of caching like Redis for the data that doesn't change that often.

599
01:06:59,664 --> 01:07:02,468
But we can also improve this a bit further.

600
01:07:02,784 --> 01:07:14,784
So, first of all, if we think about the scale of the reads that we will have, which is around 1000 queries per second, PostgreSQL database won't be able to handle that load, so

601
01:07:14,944 --> 01:07:17,348
we need to give some sort of solution to this.

602
01:07:17,966 --> 01:07:25,988
For such situations, tools like Elasticsearch are a good solution for handling the massive scale for the search.

603
01:07:26,318 --> 01:07:35,428
So we will introduce a search service that will be responsible for retrieving the hotels by the filters that are set on the front end.

604
01:07:35,886 --> 01:07:42,116
And Elasticsearch will handle the full text-based search on hotel names and descriptions.

605
01:07:42,624 --> 01:07:51,972
Also, it will be able to filter hotels, let's say within 5 kilometers of location or within some range to that location that is set,

606
01:07:52,526 --> 01:07:58,564
and it will be able to filter by the price, range, ratings, and any other filters that we need.

607
01:07:59,054 --> 01:08:06,788
The thing with Elasticsearch is that it's way faster than the PostgreSQL database that we will use, let's say for listing the hotels,

608
01:08:07,424 --> 01:08:15,264
and also it is eventually consistent, meaning that eventually it gets updated with the database and gets the

609
01:08:15,264 --> 01:08:23,264
latest data in the Elasticsearch and indexes everything properly to be able to search quickly based on locations

610
01:08:23,424 --> 01:08:26,564
or based on the price range and any other filters.

611
01:08:27,086 --> 01:08:29,664
We can also decouple the services a bit.

612
01:08:29,904 --> 01:08:39,504
Another approach is to use different databases for each microservices, like for the payment service, we can have payment database, for the hotel service, we can have

613
01:08:39,664 --> 01:08:44,420
hotel database, and we'll discuss what's the pros and cons of this.

614
01:08:45,104 --> 01:08:52,708
But to also decouple them, we can introduce a message queue that will be responsible, for instance, for payments.

615
01:08:53,102 --> 01:09:01,344
Whenever a payment is processed in a payment service, we will process that and publish a message in a Kafka RabbitMQ

616
01:09:01,646 --> 01:09:03,492
or some other messaging queue.

617
01:09:03,984 --> 01:09:12,704
And this will first of all send email push notification to the user or to the owner as well, as an email or as an

618
01:09:12,846 --> 01:09:17,540
in-app notification that the payment was successful and the hotel is reserved.

619
01:09:18,064 --> 01:09:22,784
And it will also update the availability, meaning the status of the hotel.

620
01:09:22,944 --> 01:09:32,144
So it will push that to the search service, which will update it in the Elasticsearch, and that is again getting eventually consistent.

621
01:09:32,384 --> 01:09:44,144
So right after the payment, maybe if users search, they will still see that hotel available, but they won't be able to book it because we are using the hotel database to double

622
01:09:44,304 --> 01:09:46,276
check whether it's really available or not.

623
01:09:46,990 --> 01:09:55,012
And eventually, after some time, this will be persisted with the database and it will have the same data as the PostgreSQL database.

624
01:09:55,598 --> 01:10:01,904
Next, when it comes to choosing whether all these services will be like microservices or a single monolith.

625
01:10:02,542 --> 01:10:05,440
Or they can also be pragmatic microservices.

626
01:10:05,600 --> 01:10:14,320
For example, in our case, a single monolith would simplify the transactions, but it will become hard to scale and deploy independently.

627
01:10:14,560 --> 01:10:25,120
because if many developers, let's say hundreds of developers, are working on this single monolith, then it becomes very hard to deploy independently and work

628
01:10:25,360 --> 01:10:27,780
on things without stepping on each other.

629
01:10:28,206 --> 01:10:36,548
Another approach is full microservices like this, where you have RAID service, notification service, and so on, with separate databases

630
01:10:36,880 --> 01:10:44,420
which will introduce distributed transactions and consistency problems that don't exist at the monolith level.

631
01:10:44,974 --> 01:10:52,612
That is because each service, as you can see, like hotel service owns its own database, reservation service owns its own database.

632
01:10:53,006 --> 01:11:02,180
So another approach is to keep the tightly coupled data together in one database and share it between the services.

633
01:11:02,734 --> 01:11:09,280
For example, in our case, the reservations and the hotel databases can be kept together.

634
01:11:09,486 --> 01:11:19,428
This will let us to use the local ACID transactions instead of the distributed transactions, and we will have a service level separation at the application layer,

635
01:11:19,840 --> 01:11:23,908
but we will still have the shared data for tightly coupled entities.

636
01:11:24,430 --> 01:11:30,660
After you agree on the high-level design, the next step is the API and database design.

637
01:11:30,960 --> 01:11:35,120
And you can start with API design then database or vice versa.

638
01:11:35,280 --> 01:11:39,556
Whichever way it's easier for you to talk about the flow.

639
01:11:39,982 --> 01:11:43,108
So in our case, let's start with the API design.

640
01:11:43,566 --> 01:11:50,596
We will use RESTful APIs for all the operations because that is what makes sense in this kind of system.

641
01:11:51,086 --> 01:11:59,760
Although, as you can see here, the frontend directly makes calls to hotel API, to other APIs as well that we have.

642
01:11:59,920 --> 01:12:03,012
Maybe sometimes it will make calls to reservation API.

643
01:12:03,470 --> 01:12:12,996
Another approach here, a very common one, is when you have multiple microservices. Usually you would use a GraphQL layer in front of the frontend.

644
01:12:13,120 --> 01:12:19,680
So frontend will only make requests to the GraphQL and it also will know the schema based on the GraphQL schema.

645
01:12:19,886 --> 01:12:25,956
And then the GraphQL layer will be responsible for making calls to all these services that you have behind the scenes.

646
01:12:26,446 --> 01:12:31,140
So it kind of extracts away the complexity and the routing between these services.

647
01:12:31,662 --> 01:12:37,200
But to keep it simple on the diagram, let's just assume that front end makes the calls directly.

648
01:12:37,486 --> 01:12:46,560
So we have here admins who are listing the properties, we have users who are browsing the hotels and making reservations, and we have the front

649
01:12:46,720 --> 01:12:50,308
end app, which is either a mobile app or the web version.

650
01:12:50,830 --> 01:12:57,252
The very first step for admins is they need to be able to log in to be able to use the features for the admins.

651
01:12:57,440 --> 01:13:06,560
While users can be browsing the hotels without being logged in, but whenever it comes the point for the reservation, they need to also create an account or login.

652
01:13:06,990 --> 01:13:15,364
For listing the available hotels, we will make a get request to slash hotels with any necessary filters that the user sets,

653
01:13:15,920 --> 01:13:18,660
and we will get the available hotels.

654
01:13:19,278 --> 01:13:27,600
And then if they want to browse a specific hotel, then we will make a request, same get request to slash hotels slash specific ID of the hotel,

655
01:13:27,790 --> 01:13:31,844
which will return us the hotel that has that specific ID.

656
01:13:32,366 --> 01:13:38,692
Now how it will happen behind the scenes is hotel API will fetch the hotel details from the database.

657
01:13:39,406 --> 01:13:44,868
So for specific hotel, we can also read this from the PostgreSQL database.

658
01:13:45,520 --> 01:13:56,804
But in the initial call where we are making a request to slash hotels, that is where we will be using the Elasticsearch to quickly filter based on the filters that are set on the frontend.

659
01:13:57,294 --> 01:14:07,908
And at this step for a single hotel, we just basically forward the hotel details to the front end, and then frontend is responsible for parsing and showing the details in the page.

660
01:14:08,654 --> 01:14:15,684
For admins who want to add a new hotel, they will need to make a post request to slash hotels endpoint.

661
01:14:16,256 --> 01:14:20,068
And this is where we will insert it into the SQL first.

662
01:14:20,496 --> 01:14:28,324
And then once we get a successful insert, we will just forward the 201 status that the hotel is created.

663
01:14:28,878 --> 01:14:32,932
And later this will be eventually persisted with the Elasticsearch database.

664
01:14:33,216 --> 01:14:38,756
But we will show the confirmation at this step to the admin that the hotel has been created.

665
01:14:39,296 --> 01:14:48,496
And similarly, if they want to update the hotel information and they make some edits, we can make a put or patch request to hotels

666
01:14:48,814 --> 01:14:50,628
slash ID of that hotel.

667
01:14:50,896 --> 01:14:59,492
And again, update it in the database, get the update result and send the confirmation and show it for the admin.

668
01:14:59,950 --> 01:15:07,300
A similar flow for removing hotels, again a delete request in this case to slash hotels slash ID of that hotel.

669
01:15:07,630 --> 01:15:11,364
We remove it from the database and we show it to the user.

670
01:15:11,696 --> 01:15:15,396
And later this will be removed from the Elasticsearch.

671
01:15:15,854 --> 01:15:20,996
And within hotels, we will also have rooms because one hotel has many available rooms.

672
01:15:21,550 --> 01:15:24,016
We will treat them as a sub-resource.

673
01:15:24,416 --> 01:15:34,416
So under hotels and specific ID of the hotel, we will have slash rooms first of all, which will give you all the rooms that are available under

674
01:15:34,736 --> 01:15:35,696
this hotel.

675
01:15:35,936 --> 01:15:47,044
And to fetch the specific hotel, you can make a request to slash ID and get more details from that hotel, which will again fetch from the database and then you will forward it to the client.

676
01:15:47,406 --> 01:15:52,612
And basically, here we are just repeating whatever we have for the hotels, but for the rooms.

677
01:15:52,816 --> 01:15:59,780
So if you want to other room, you will make a request to post slash ID of the hotel slash the rooms.

678
01:16:00,110 --> 01:16:03,620
This is again following the general RESTful principles.

679
01:16:04,110 --> 01:16:08,576
We will insert the room in the database and provide with a successful response.

680
01:16:08,736 --> 01:16:17,860
Of course, you need to also handle the failures whenever something goes wrong, showing the error on why something went wrong in the API.

681
01:16:18,256 --> 01:16:28,260
But when talking about the happy paths or at least the available endpoints, these are what we will have. We will also have a put endpoint which is for editing the room type.

682
01:16:51,566 --> 01:16:57,156
Then for the reservations, this is where we will make requests to the reservation service.

683
01:16:57,646 --> 01:17:01,028
Let's say a user wants to see their reservation history.

684
01:17:01,422 --> 01:17:05,536
We need to provide an endpoint here to fetch the reservations.

685
01:17:05,616 --> 01:17:14,788
So a get request to slash reservations, which will read it from the SQL database and then forward the response to the client.

686
01:17:15,406 --> 01:17:20,548
Then they want to see, let's say, a reservation details and the invoice that they paid.

687
01:17:21,006 --> 01:17:30,656
So that is where we will have the get endpoint to slash reservations slash ID of a single reservation, which will fetch the details from the database.

688
01:17:30,736 --> 01:17:37,540
We will get the details back and we will forward it to the front end and we will parse and show it to the client.

689
01:17:37,966 --> 01:17:49,764
Next, when creating new reservations, they will fill the reservation form in the front end. Then we will send this form details in a post request to reservations.

690
01:17:50,176 --> 01:17:56,656
One important thing here is that at this step we can already prevent double booking for single user.

691
01:17:56,816 --> 01:18:04,900
There are two scenarios like one user can try to double click or click the same reservation room twice.

692
01:18:05,390 --> 01:18:12,016
So that's one scenario which we can handle here. The other one is that two users can try to book the same room.

693
01:18:12,654 --> 01:18:13,028
Twice.

694
01:18:13,294 --> 01:18:16,064
We will handle that in the later step.

695
01:18:16,304 --> 01:18:19,140
But at this step, for example, one solution is

696
01:18:19,790 --> 01:18:29,664
we can include a key like a reservation ID, and this will be generated on the front end, which will be used to prevent double bookings when

697
01:18:29,824 --> 01:18:32,228
user clicks submit multiple times.

698
01:18:32,974 --> 01:18:43,904
So if you have the hotel ID and the room ID that this user is trying to book, basically we generate a reservation ID once, and then if they try to double book it,

699
01:18:44,064 --> 01:18:51,184
like book it again, we will be able to catch it in the database and see that the reservation ID is the same.

700
01:18:51,344 --> 01:18:56,452
So we will block them from making a request, or we can even do it on the frontend side.

701
01:18:56,910 --> 01:19:00,132
Another solution is you can also do it on the back-end side.

702
01:19:00,590 --> 01:19:09,124
For example, when they make a request to book something, you can combine the hotel ID in combination with room ID,

703
01:19:09,504 --> 01:19:12,292
the user ID who tries to book this,

704
01:19:13,024 --> 01:19:16,740
and also the date or some sort of timestamp.

705
01:19:17,038 --> 01:19:21,444
Why? Because in the future this user might try to book it again.

706
01:19:21,904 --> 01:19:30,468
And if you just use this in combination, then basically you are blocking them to make a future reservation on the same hotel.

707
01:19:30,894 --> 01:19:35,204
So you can also use this in combination to generate some sort of hash.

708
01:19:35,584 --> 01:19:40,964
And whenever the hash matches from the database, then you block them from making the reservation.

709
01:19:41,504 --> 01:19:49,264
But this is a way simpler solution on the front end side to just generate some sort of UUID for the reservation ID.

710
01:19:49,344 --> 01:19:57,348
If you are talking only about a single user and preventing them from booking again, then this is a very lightweight

711
01:19:57,424 --> 01:20:04,196
solution and way simpler than inserting this hash into the database and checking it every time in the backend.

712
01:20:04,814 --> 01:20:08,676
So here we will first check for existing reservation ID.

713
01:20:08,814 --> 01:20:12,260
If it's not found, then we will insert a new reservation.

714
01:20:12,814 --> 01:20:20,356
So assuming it's not a double booking and we get the reservation confirmation, we will just show them the confirmation and behind the scenes

715
01:20:20,750 --> 01:20:28,068
the Kafka message will be published and it will send the notifications to the user and to the owner of the hotel.

716
01:20:28,624 --> 01:20:36,260
But if it's a duplicate reservation attempt, then they will make a request and this will contain that reservation ID.

717
01:20:36,718 --> 01:20:45,476
So we will check for the existing reservation ID, and if a transaction like that already happened and we have the reservation and it already exists,

718
01:20:45,998 --> 01:20:51,460
then we will return the existing reservation and show it to the user that this is already reserved.

719
01:20:52,046 --> 01:20:58,852
Similarly, they might want to either cancel the reservation or change the dates of the reservation.

720
01:20:59,310 --> 01:21:01,956
So we need to provide endpoints for that as well.

721
01:21:02,382 --> 01:21:08,644
In case of canceling, it will be a delete request to reservations slash ID of that reservation.

722
01:21:09,038 --> 01:21:13,424
And in the database, we just need to check whether it's allowed to cancel.

723
01:21:13,614 --> 01:21:22,948
Because if they try to cancel a week before the appointment, then it's allowed, or if they try to cancel it last minute, then it's probably not allowed.

724
01:21:23,342 --> 01:21:26,464
So in case it is allowed, we will just send the confirmation.

725
01:21:26,638 --> 01:21:34,436
Otherwise, we will send a response that we cannot delete the reservation, meaning we cannot cancel it, and show the options to the user.

726
01:21:34,990 --> 01:21:41,348
Similar to this, you might also have edit reservation with put request, which will update the dates.

727
01:21:41,504 --> 01:21:48,388
For example, if plans have changed and you want to update the dates, then we will make a put request to that ID reservation.

728
01:21:48,718 --> 01:21:51,524
Let's also see how the search will happen.

729
01:21:51,744 --> 01:21:58,784
So another service we will have for this is the search service, which will be connected with the Elasticsearch.

730
01:21:59,150 --> 01:22:08,384
So let's say user sets the filters like the location where they want this hotel to be located at, the dates, start and end date, and so on.

731
01:22:08,686 --> 01:22:11,744
And we make a GET request to slash /search.

732
01:22:11,918 --> 01:22:20,224
This will include the location, the check-in, checkout time, how many guests you have, how many pets you want, the price range, and any other

733
01:22:20,750 --> 01:22:21,860
necessary filters.

734
01:22:22,446 --> 01:22:25,764
This is where we will query the available hotels and room types.

735
01:22:26,304 --> 01:22:30,544
But an important thing is we will query this from the Elasticsearch.

736
01:22:30,638 --> 01:22:40,384
In this case, we won't query it from the database because it's way faster, and that's the point of us setting up Elasticsearch so that we can quickly return the search results.

737
01:22:40,704 --> 01:22:48,740
And this will return the available hotels within the range of that location and within that

738
01:22:49,102 --> 01:22:55,556
price range and the check in checkout time, and frontend will be parsing and showing it to the users.

739
01:22:56,782 --> 01:23:01,604
Next, let's move on to the data models and the database design.

740
01:23:02,126 --> 01:23:07,920
One important thing here is that you book the room type and not the specific room.

741
01:23:08,078 --> 01:23:18,628
So you book a specific type of room, like is it a double queen bed or something else, and not a specific room, because the room numbers are assigned at the check-in

742
01:23:18,880 --> 01:23:19,556
and not here.

743
01:23:20,078 --> 01:23:24,868
But let's start from the hotels because it will make more sense this way.

744
01:23:25,422 --> 01:23:32,484
So we will have, of course, the ID of the hotel, which is the indicator of the unique ID for this hotel.

745
01:23:32,878 --> 01:23:36,804
It can have a name, address, location, description, and so on.

746
01:23:37,120 --> 01:23:41,732
Now there might be also hotels that are under the same brand name.

747
01:23:42,160 --> 01:23:48,580
In this case, you can either have the same hotel name but different hotel IDs.

748
01:23:48,880 --> 01:23:57,280
So it will be a different hotel LLC in US, let's say it will be a different hotel LLC in Europe, the name will be the same,

749
01:23:57,440 --> 01:23:58,980
but the ID will be different.

750
01:23:59,470 --> 01:24:04,548
Or you can use a helper table like hotel groups and then group them under

751
01:24:05,198 --> 01:24:11,844
a brand name if there are brands that have multiple hotels at different locations.

752
01:24:12,494 --> 01:24:16,036
Then the important part is the room type that we need to have.

753
01:24:16,160 --> 01:24:24,880
So the ID of the room type, its name, description, and then how many people it can serve, the ID of the hotel

754
01:24:25,040 --> 01:24:26,820
which connects back to the hotel.

755
01:24:27,200 --> 01:24:33,508
So one hotel can have multiple room types, and this is a one-to-many relation.

756
01:24:34,030 --> 01:24:41,440
Then each room type is also connected with room type rate because each of these rooms will have a different rate.

757
01:24:41,520 --> 01:24:49,828
Let's say we have a room which is the basic package, and as a basic example, let's say it costs 100 bucks per day.

758
01:24:50,126 --> 01:24:51,780
So this is the basic package.

759
01:24:52,270 --> 01:24:55,524
Then you can have a room with a better view.

760
01:24:55,790 --> 01:24:59,908
In this case, it will be something like 150 per day.

761
01:25:00,240 --> 01:25:07,920
You can have a hotel room with breakfast included, and that will be something like 200 and so on.

762
01:25:08,080 --> 01:25:16,004
So that is the reason why we have this room type rates, because for different room type we will have a different rate

763
01:25:16,240 --> 01:25:19,940
that will be multiplied by the base number that we have here.

764
01:25:20,398 --> 01:25:27,876
And this is again one, too, many relation because one room type can have multiple room type rates.

765
01:25:28,334 --> 01:25:30,596
Then this is where we have the actual rooms.

766
01:25:30,880 --> 01:25:32,720
So, what is the room ID?

767
01:25:32,910 --> 01:25:37,280
This is the exact room that we are going to assign at the check-in.

768
01:25:37,934 --> 01:25:42,640
Then it will have some more metadata like floor, number, whether it's available.

769
01:25:42,766 --> 01:25:54,788
This is the important part that will be tracking the status, and this is also what we are reading when checking the availability, and the room type ID, which again refers back to the room type.

770
01:25:54,880 --> 01:25:57,988
So one room type can have multiple rooms in it.

771
01:25:58,606 --> 01:26:00,240
Which means you can have a hotel.

772
01:26:00,400 --> 01:26:11,556
Under that hotel, you have multiple room types, like you have that say the basic type, and in the basic type, you might have three rooms available that are all basic rooms.

773
01:26:12,110 --> 01:26:18,660
This also connects to the hotel ID so that you can determine which exact hotel this belongs to.

774
01:26:19,022 --> 01:26:27,428
You could also join it with the room types and then get the hotel ID like that, but it will be helpful for us to have the hotel ID here.

775
01:26:27,694 --> 01:26:33,920
So this will be like which hotel does it belong to, the room that we are assigning at the check-in.

776
01:26:34,446 --> 01:26:36,740
Next, we will have the room type inventory.

777
01:26:37,230 --> 01:26:43,520
Inventory is tracked per hotel, per room type and day, so not the physical room.

778
01:26:43,680 --> 01:26:53,440
This will enable us to have bulk availability updates, also scalable pricing and search, and it will be simple overbooking logic

779
01:26:53,600 --> 01:26:59,396
because we will also support overbooking, considering the cancellations that we will have in the rooms.

780
01:26:59,918 --> 01:27:09,316
So here we will track the room ID type, the hotel ID, and this is again one to many relationships between the room type and between the hotel,

781
01:27:09,920 --> 01:27:14,948
and some more things like the date, the total inventory, total reserved, and so on.

782
01:27:15,502 --> 01:27:23,616
Another important table here is the reservations because once user selects the hotel room, specific room,

783
01:27:23,790 --> 01:27:25,296
we will need to reserve it.

784
01:27:25,456 --> 01:27:30,276
So we will track the reservation ID, which we will link later to the transactions.

785
01:27:30,798 --> 01:27:35,204
We will have the room type ID, this refers to the room types table.

786
01:27:35,662 --> 01:27:41,216
We will have the exact hotel ID where they booked this, which refers to the hotels table.

787
01:27:41,536 --> 01:27:44,356
The guest ID, which is the user who booked it.

788
01:27:44,622 --> 01:27:54,980
So for that we will have a guests table, and we can additionally have, let's say, admins or brokers table, which will be the users who are publishing these rooms.

789
01:27:55,662 --> 01:28:02,436
So here the most important thing for us is the guest ID, the first name, last name, email, and so on.

790
01:28:02,958 --> 01:28:13,700
So we will connect it with one-to-many relationship here because one user, one guest can reserve multiple hotels, so that's why it is one-to-many.

791
01:28:14,336 --> 01:28:21,572
Then some more metadata like start date, end date, status, whether it's reserved, whether it's cancelled, and so on.

792
01:28:22,126 --> 01:28:27,652
And another important piece here is that we will separate transactions into a separate table.

793
01:28:28,206 --> 01:28:31,684
The reason here is that let's say you reserve the hotel first.

794
01:28:32,096 --> 01:28:34,820
So first the status is reserved.

795
01:28:35,758 --> 01:28:45,540
Then the user can either change their mind, and here they can either cancel the reservation or they might request a change in the date.

796
01:28:45,710 --> 01:28:48,740
So they might request to change the start and end date.

797
01:28:49,296 --> 01:28:56,164
In the second case, there will be a price difference that they will either have to pay or we have to refund them.

798
01:28:56,494 --> 01:28:58,896
So that will be another transaction, right?

799
01:28:59,056 --> 01:29:03,172
And that's why one reservation can have multiple transactions.

800
01:29:03,376 --> 01:29:14,096
So the first transaction is when they reserve, and then the second one is when they decide to change the dates, let's say, and update the reservation, they make another payment here,

801
01:29:14,256 --> 01:29:15,780
which is a second transaction.

802
01:29:16,256 --> 01:29:24,388
Or if they decide to cancel, that is another transaction where the amount in this case is minus, so we are refunding the payment,

803
01:29:24,656 --> 01:29:26,896
but that is still a separate transaction.

804
01:29:26,990 --> 01:29:36,416
And that's why we will have separate transactions table where we will track the ID of transaction, which reservation does this belong to, the type of the transaction,

805
01:29:36,576 --> 01:29:42,084
like refund, change or the new transaction, and the amount which can be both

806
01:29:42,816 --> 01:29:44,676
positive and a negative number.

807
01:29:45,166 --> 01:29:53,060
With this schema, the room type inventory table has one row per hotel, room type and date combination.

808
01:29:53,550 --> 01:30:02,276
We will pre-populate this for the next two years, and then a daily cron job can add new dates as the time moves forward.

809
01:30:02,926 --> 01:30:06,372
So we can also take a look at some example data here.

810
01:30:06,734 --> 01:30:17,668
We'll have the hotel ID where these room types belong to, then the room type ID, let's say these three rooms are from the same type, like the basic type,

811
01:30:18,510 --> 01:30:24,228
dates where we are checking this for, like December 15, 16, 17, and so on.

812
01:30:24,816 --> 01:30:32,996
And then we will see the total inventory and also total reserve, which will give us an idea on how many rooms we have available.

813
01:30:33,518 --> 01:30:42,628
When checking the availability, if you're using the SQL database, let's say, for December 23 to 27, we will select the date,

814
01:30:42,816 --> 01:30:50,576
total inventory, and total reserved, which is this column here, and then the total inventory, which is the available.

815
01:30:50,894 --> 01:31:01,092
We will select it from the room type inventory where the hotel ID and room type ID match, and we will set the date to be between the range that we want to.

816
01:31:01,582 --> 01:31:05,060
This will give us the total reserved and total inventory.

817
01:31:05,536 --> 01:31:14,336
And by using this, we can check that if the total reserve plus rooms to book is still less than equal to the total inventory,

818
01:31:14,496 --> 01:31:15,812
then we allow the booking.

819
01:31:16,176 --> 01:31:25,444
Which means if let's say here we have 100 and here we have 92, so we still have eight rooms available, and if user is trying to book just one room,

820
01:31:25,776 --> 01:31:34,980
that will be 92 plus 1, and we are checking this against 100, so the condition will be true and we will allow the booking.

821
01:31:35,552 --> 01:31:43,552
And coming back to the initial requirements, since we allow let's say 10% overbooking or whatever that number is,

822
01:31:43,872 --> 01:31:46,832
we can include that overbooking factor here.

823
01:31:46,992 --> 01:31:54,112
Let's say, if you allow 10% overbooking, then we will allow 10 %, like said the 10% here.

824
01:31:54,414 --> 01:32:02,592
meaning if we have 100 total inventory available, then we will allow them to book up to 110 rooms

825
01:32:02,752 --> 01:32:08,708
because we know that the other 10 will probably be cancelled until the date of the reservation.

826
01:32:09,166 --> 01:32:12,900
And lastly, let's also discuss why we move with relational database.

827
01:32:13,072 --> 01:32:18,980
In this case, we kind of touched on this, but let's get a bit deeper in why we choose this.

828
01:32:19,630 --> 01:32:27,620
So, first, the most important thing we need here is the ACID guarantees and specifically the consistency of SQL databases.

829
01:32:27,872 --> 01:32:33,392
This is important for us for preventing double poking issues, which we will handle soon.

830
01:32:33,902 --> 01:32:37,988
From the calculations, we can also see that this is a very read-heavy system.

831
01:32:38,478 --> 01:32:46,116
So relational databases handle this kind of reads well, and we can always combine it with Elasticsearch for scaling it.

832
01:32:46,672 --> 01:32:56,452
But with 1000 reads, queries per second versus 17 writes per second, this clearly shows that we need to go with an SQL database here

833
01:32:56,672 --> 01:33:01,220
in combination with the consistency factor that we need from the ACID.

834
01:33:01,742 --> 01:33:09,712
And then the relationships are more clear this way, like between rooms, hotels, the room types, reservations,

835
01:33:09,952 --> 01:33:11,268
transactions, and so on.

836
01:33:11,792 --> 01:33:19,792
And we'll need to make complex queries to check the availability of the rooms or make multiple joins for inventory

837
01:33:19,982 --> 01:33:23,812
with pricing, filter by dates, aggregate totals, and so on.

838
01:33:24,142 --> 01:33:26,672
So SQL will handle this well as well.

839
01:33:26,832 --> 01:33:29,232
We also need to handle the concurrency.

840
01:33:29,472 --> 01:33:35,872
We already discussed the first scenario where the same user is trying to book the same room twice.

841
01:33:36,174 --> 01:33:38,916
And the solution here is just to have that reservation key.

842
01:33:39,278 --> 01:33:45,060
So any second request will get duplicate key error and we will return the existing reservation.

843
01:33:45,232 --> 01:33:48,192
So that's the simple one that we can solve.

844
01:33:48,462 --> 01:33:57,860
But then the second scenario is we have two different users who are trying to book the same room type, which is only one spot left for that hotel.

845
01:33:58,318 --> 01:34:07,632
And if you know about the SQL isolation layers, by default this will be read committed, and concurrency bugs still happen at read-committed

846
01:34:07,792 --> 01:34:13,604
isolation layer, which means that you only see data after other transactions commit.

847
01:34:13,952 --> 01:34:20,432
But there is a gap between when you read, like check the availability, and when you write, which is you book the room.

848
01:34:20,672 --> 01:34:29,152
And in that gap you might have two different transaction happening, like user A reads the total reserved, he sees that one room is available

849
01:34:29,312 --> 01:34:30,756
because the total is 100.

850
01:34:31,246 --> 01:34:39,152
And user B also reads and sees that there are 99 rooms reserved and 100 available, which means that there is one available.

851
01:34:39,312 --> 01:34:48,420
So for both of them, the checks shows successful, like the condition is true, which means that we allow them to proceed with the booking.

852
01:34:49,006 --> 01:34:58,660
So read committed by itself is unsafe for bookings because two transactions can both read valid data, pass checks, and then overwrite each other.

853
01:34:59,054 --> 01:35:01,508
And that's exactly how double bookings happen.

854
01:35:01,742 --> 01:35:05,316
As a solution to this, we have two main approaches in this case.

855
01:35:05,838 --> 01:35:16,672
One approach here is that we can use serializable isolation layer, which means that when beginning the transaction at the code level, we can set the isolation layer to be serializable.

856
01:35:16,832 --> 01:35:18,724
By default, it is read committed.

857
01:35:19,278 --> 01:35:30,496
This is simple to implement and it will work fine for low TPS, meaning transactions per second, because these won't be blocking each other if you don't have that much high volume.

858
01:35:30,766 --> 01:35:33,572
So it's fine to use serializable in that case.

859
01:35:34,190 --> 01:35:37,924
The downsides to this is that transactions will block each other.

860
01:35:38,256 --> 01:35:41,936
So if you have high TPS, then this won't scale well.

861
01:35:42,158 --> 01:35:48,196
And also you introduce potential deadlocks if locking multiple resources in different orders.

862
01:35:48,750 --> 01:35:58,628
However, you can use, let's say, lock timeouts and set it to some number to prevent indefinite weights and also keep the transactions short.

863
01:35:59,216 --> 01:36:09,220
But still, there is a better solution in this case, which is to use read committed isolation layer, which is still the default mode, in combination with row locking.

864
01:36:09,582 --> 01:36:21,136
This is when you're using select for update and it is used to lock the inventory rows while we are checking and booking them. So that other users have to wait their turn.

865
01:36:21,216 --> 01:36:29,412
If you have two transactions who try to log the same row, the second one needs to wait for this transaction to be processed.

866
01:36:29,806 --> 01:36:41,924
But if there are other transactions which are not trying to write to this same row, then they are free to move forward and basically they can go in parallel instead of the serializable

867
01:36:42,096 --> 01:36:44,484
where the transactions need to go one by one.

868
01:36:45,070 --> 01:36:52,096
So the for update here prevents the race condition by serializing access to specific rows in this

869
01:36:52,096 --> 01:36:58,340
case. The read committed is still the PostgreSQL's default and works fine here.

870
01:36:58,766 --> 01:37:08,388
And serializable is fine to be used if you have low TPS and not that much traffic coming to write to the database every second.

871
01:37:09,134 --> 01:37:18,628
The next phase is talking about scaling the system, things like database sharding, whether it's needed, caching strategy, how we are going to optimize the Elasticsearch queries.

872
01:37:18,990 --> 01:37:20,836
So let's start from the database sharding.

873
01:37:21,326 --> 01:37:31,364
Since we're going to handle 1000 queries per second and 17 transactions per second, we need to eventually shard this, and that will be obvious

874
01:37:31,696 --> 01:37:35,492
by the time it takes for typical queries to run.

875
01:37:36,016 --> 01:37:38,436
And there are tables here that we can split.

876
01:37:39,022 --> 01:37:47,172
When it comes to sharding, the important decision is what will be the shard key and by which field you are going to shard.

877
01:37:47,726 --> 01:37:50,852
One example here is to shard it by hotel ID.

878
01:37:51,342 --> 01:37:59,716
Why? Because most queries will filter by hotel first to see their location, whether it matches with the search query filter,

879
01:38:00,096 --> 01:38:05,092
and then they are going to fetch the room types available in this hotel.

880
01:38:05,582 --> 01:38:13,056
All data for one hotel will still stay on the same shard, so we will not be cross-sharding the joints.

881
01:38:13,296 --> 01:38:17,124
And this will also enable local transactions within the shard.

882
01:38:17,710 --> 01:38:23,376
For initial deployment, we can use modulo sharding, which is the formula for sharding.

883
01:38:23,456 --> 01:38:32,196
So the shard number will be equal to this, won't be consistent hash initially, so it will be just the hash of the hotel ID

884
01:38:32,496 --> 01:38:35,876
in combination with the number of shards that we will have.

885
01:38:36,366 --> 01:38:40,452
This will work well when you know your shard count won't change often.

886
01:38:40,814 --> 01:38:48,896
So if we decide to, let's say, split this 1000 read queries per second between 16 shards, then this will range about

887
01:38:49,262 --> 01:38:56,132
62 reads meaning queries per second and one writes transactions per second per shard.

888
01:38:56,336 --> 01:38:59,684
So in that case, the modulo sharding will work fine.

889
01:39:00,016 --> 01:39:07,012
And then later, that is where we can integrate consistent sharding if needed to minimize the data movement.

890
01:39:07,246 --> 01:39:16,496
So the thing with modulo sharding is that if we decide to add another database, let's say number 17, this will require us to reshuffle

891
01:39:16,656 --> 01:39:19,428
almost all the data from 1 to 16.

892
01:39:19,854 --> 01:39:27,936
Whereas if we use consistent hashing, this only moves about one of the 17th of the data.

893
01:39:28,654 --> 01:39:33,444
Which is the data that is going to be stored in the 17th shard.

894
01:39:33,966 --> 01:39:39,428
And you can check out the video in the system design course where I talk about consistent hashing.

895
01:39:39,918 --> 01:39:50,752
Basically, it uses a hash ring where both servers and keys map to points on a circle, and each key goes to the next server clockwise to the ring.

896
01:39:50,894 --> 01:39:59,712
So if you have a ring like this, then you calculated the key which is ending up here, and the closest server to that is here,

897
01:39:59,872 --> 01:40:02,180
then this will be stored in that server.

898
01:40:02,734 --> 01:40:10,948
So with consistent hashing, adding a new server only affects the keys between it and the previous server, which is the number 16.

899
01:40:11,278 --> 01:40:16,032
Whereas with modulo sharding, you need to basically reshuffle all the data.

900
01:40:16,238 --> 01:40:28,036
So with small number of shards, modulo sharding is the way to go. And if you know that you will be typically adding new shards to the database, then consistent hashing is a better solution here.

901
01:40:28,430 --> 01:40:32,612
Now let's see the caching strategy that we can use for the hotels.

902
01:40:33,262 --> 01:40:40,388
We will need to cache the hot inventory data in Redis or Memcache for smart invalidation.

903
01:40:40,878 --> 01:40:50,592
So this is the cache structure that we can have, which is for the key, we will store the inventory, which will be the combination of hotel ID,

904
01:40:50,832 --> 01:40:52,772
room type ID, and the date.

905
01:40:53,392 --> 01:40:57,392
And for the value, we will store the inventory available.

906
01:40:57,614 --> 01:41:05,232
Let's say in this hotel again 100 are available, and the total reserved, which can be something like 87.

907
01:41:06,032 --> 01:41:16,032
And we will also have in combination with this the overbooking rate, which will be 10%. But of course, we don't store this in the cache, we have this as a fixed number somewhere.

908
01:41:16,270 --> 01:41:21,316
And when calculating, we will use this in combination with total inventory and total reserved.

909
01:41:21,934 --> 01:41:28,356
And we will assign the cache a time to leave, which can be something like 5 minutes per inventory.

910
01:41:29,358 --> 01:41:32,164
Now the cache flow will look something like this.

911
01:41:32,686 --> 01:41:38,992
User will first search for hotels, and this is where we will check the Redis cache first for the hotel inventory.

912
01:41:39,246 --> 01:41:50,692
If the cache is hit, meaning we stored this data in the cache, if that is true, then we will show the hotel inventory to the user immediately from reading from the cache.

913
01:41:51,232 --> 01:41:54,512
But in case it is not in the cache, we have cache miss.

914
01:41:55,086 --> 01:41:58,112
This means that we need to read it from the database.

915
01:41:58,350 --> 01:42:04,420
Then we will also populate it after reading in the Redis cache so that next time this will be a cache hit.

916
01:42:04,992 --> 01:42:13,952
And also if we read it from the cache, so it was cache hit, but the TTL is expired, then we basically repeat the same flow.

917
01:42:14,032 --> 01:42:19,236
So we read it from the database, we store it in the cache, and then we show it to the user.

918
01:42:19,694 --> 01:42:26,980
After we show this to the user, let's say the hotel is available, user can click on booking, so to book the hotel.

919
01:42:27,502 --> 01:42:35,364
This is where we will need to validate the booking against database and update the database with the booking transaction.

920
01:42:35,854 --> 01:42:39,844
Again, this is where we will invalidate the affected cache keys.

921
01:42:40,192 --> 01:42:48,512
If they decide to book it and the booking goes successful, then we need to basically invalidate the cache, update the fields,

922
01:42:48,672 --> 01:42:57,152
and on the next user search, we will check the Redis again, and this will be up to date with what just happens when this user, let's say,

923
01:42:57,358 --> 01:42:58,272
books the hotel.

924
01:42:58,352 --> 01:43:06,020
So next time we don't show it as available, but we show that this room type is no longer available if it was booked with the previous search.

925
01:43:06,446 --> 01:43:15,632
Let's also discuss the search optimization with Elasticsearch and also a bit more in detail on what's the use case for Elasticsearch.

926
01:43:15,758 --> 01:43:28,592
So Elasticsearch is a search and analytics engine that is a specialized database for specifically search operations and not transactional workloads like with PostgreSQL or MySQL.

927
01:43:28,846 --> 01:43:41,712
So PostgreSQL or MySQL are still great with give me the exact row where ID equals to 123 or insert this into the database where the hotel ID equals to something.

928
01:43:41,952 --> 01:43:49,892
But on the other hand, Elasticsearch is great at Finding complex filters, for example, find all documents

929
01:43:50,512 --> 01:44:00,516
that kind of match with this text and rank them by relevance, filter by multiple criteria and do it fast across millions of documents.

930
01:44:01,038 --> 01:44:09,392
For example, in our case, it will be find all hotel rooms where hotel is located within Georgia,

931
01:44:09,552 --> 01:44:16,832
let's say the price range is between 80 to 180 bucks per day.

932
01:44:17,152 --> 01:44:23,712
The stars are above eight plus and some more stuff, and again do it quickly.

933
01:44:23,886 --> 01:44:27,472
This is why we use Elasticsearch for bookings as well.

934
01:44:27,790 --> 01:44:34,148
If we were to just use PostgreSQL for search, this is what we would have to do: like select all from hotels.

935
01:44:34,702 --> 01:44:37,072
where location is like Paris.

936
01:44:37,152 --> 01:44:49,472
let's say, where amenities is like Wi-Fi included, where base price is within 200, where the description contains something like luxury. because we have this in the search.

937
01:44:49,632 --> 01:44:56,196
Basically, every time you use the like keyword, this kills the performance of SQL because that is very slow.

938
01:44:56,590 --> 01:45:03,684
The problem here is that like and then some text, this does full table scan in case of SQL.

939
01:45:03,792 --> 01:45:13,828
And even indexes won't help us here, because in case of like, this is basically a full table scan, and you can imagine having so many hotels or room types,

940
01:45:14,112 --> 01:45:23,072
and we basically have to scan the full table just for getting the hotels available in Paris, which is probably less than 10%

941
01:45:23,392 --> 01:45:24,388
of the full table.

942
01:45:24,942 --> 01:45:31,632
Also, it cannot rank by relevance, so it cannot get which hotel is most relevant.

943
01:45:31,918 --> 01:45:37,316
It cannot handle typos if the user misses the last S, let's say, from Paris,

944
01:45:37,966 --> 01:45:45,924
then again you need to use the like keyword, or otherwise, if you set it to exactly party, then you won't find any results.

945
01:45:46,574 --> 01:45:56,388
It is very bad at geographical like distance calculations, and also aggregations are expensive in SQL where we are aggregating data together.

946
01:45:56,846 --> 01:46:02,948
Instead, if we use Elasticsearch, we have the full text search with relevance ranking.

947
01:46:03,406 --> 01:46:06,916
So when user let's say searches luxury hotels in Paris,

948
01:46:07,566 --> 01:46:11,712
we first tokenize the luxury hotel and Paris, all the keywords.

949
01:46:11,952 --> 01:46:14,884
We find all documents that contain these terms.

950
01:46:15,374 --> 01:46:22,148
Then we also rank them by relevance by using an algorithm, one of these algorithms that Elasticsearch uses.

951
01:46:22,592 --> 01:46:34,032
It can also detect the typos, so if in the Paris you missed the I here, it can detect that user was searching for Paris and edit the distance of one.

952
01:46:34,592 --> 01:46:43,792
And it's also great at hotels with ranking, so it can give you the hotels within 5 kilometers of coordinates close to Paris.

953
01:46:43,872 --> 01:46:49,764
So maybe you are fine with booking a hotel near Paris in a smaller town with a cheaper price.

954
01:46:50,222 --> 01:46:53,732
Elasticsearch basically has that indexes optimized for this.

955
01:46:54,350 --> 01:47:00,112
But how do we basically store this data in Elasticsearch? Do we duplicate or what do we do?

956
01:47:00,352 --> 01:47:03,152
And yes, we kind of duplicate the data.

957
01:47:03,342 --> 01:47:14,244
So let's say you have the hotels table in SQL, you have the hotel ID, the name, address, and then you have the room types within this hotel that belong to this hotel specifically,

958
01:47:14,702 --> 01:47:18,672
you have the amenities for this hotel, and anything else.

959
01:47:19,054 --> 01:47:29,232
Basically, Elasticsearch stores this in its own way, which means it will convert this into this JSON-like format where it has the hotel ID, the name,

960
01:47:29,392 --> 01:47:33,952
address, and then it will nest the location and the room types in it.

961
01:47:34,112 --> 01:47:42,992
So here, as you can see, there are no joins, it basically condenses everything into one document, which will also be able to use

962
01:47:43,312 --> 01:47:44,580
to search quickly.

963
01:47:45,070 --> 01:47:49,860
And then how do we also keep Elasticsearch in sync with PostgreSQL? Because

964
01:47:50,894 --> 01:47:53,952
our single source of truth is still PostgreSQL.

965
01:47:54,032 --> 01:48:03,140
This is where we check whether the room is available or not, or whether we can do overbooking or anything else basically that you check.

966
01:48:03,502 --> 01:48:12,832
PostgreSQL is the single source of truth. That's why when we are doing transactions, we are doing transactions by checking the PostgreSQL and not Elasticsearch.

967
01:48:12,912 --> 01:48:16,580
But when we are doing searches, we use Elasticsearch in that case.

968
01:48:17,262 --> 01:48:24,992
So we can use change data capture CDC to keep Elasticsearch eventually consistent with PostgreSQL.

969
01:48:25,232 --> 01:48:26,340
This is how it works.

970
01:48:26,894 --> 01:48:30,244
We have PostgreSQL, which is again single source of truth.

971
01:48:30,832 --> 01:48:39,312
Then it uses write ahead log to send it to the Bisium connector, which reads this write-ahead

972
01:48:39,552 --> 01:48:39,792
log.

973
01:48:40,334 --> 01:48:44,548
It publishes this to Kafka and starts the event stream.

974
01:48:45,070 --> 01:48:48,432
Then Elasticsearch Consumer will process the events.

975
01:48:48,558 --> 01:48:59,108
So we will have some consumer that processes these events and it updates the index in Elasticsearch and makes it eventually consistent with the PostgreSQL database.

976
01:48:59,598 --> 01:49:01,632
This is also for crash recovery.

977
01:49:01,712 --> 01:49:08,676
So if even the PostgreSQL is crashing, then you can read it from the transaction log.

978
01:49:09,326 --> 01:49:12,912
You have Debezium, which is an open source CDC tool.

979
01:49:13,072 --> 01:49:17,220
It connects to PostgreSQL and reads the logs in real time.

980
01:49:17,646 --> 01:49:19,232
Then it publishes the changes.

981
01:49:19,312 --> 01:49:25,764
Whenever changes are happening, it publishes it to a Kafka topic, and each table gets its own topic.

982
01:49:25,952 --> 01:49:26,272
So

983
01:49:26,830 --> 01:49:34,596
then the consumer reads it from the Kafka events from each topic for each table, meaning, and it updates it with the Elasticsearch.

984
01:49:35,054 --> 01:49:41,924
That is overall the use case of Elasticsearch, why we use it and how we keep it up to date with our SQL database.

985
01:49:42,606 --> 01:49:49,636
Next, it will also need database replication, this system, because we are handling 1000 queries per second.

986
01:49:50,032 --> 01:49:51,364
So one option is

987
01:49:52,078 --> 01:50:00,100
the sharding, which is scaling the scans in the tables and it's splitting them based on the geographical locations.

988
01:50:00,718 --> 01:50:03,908
But you can also scale it for the reads by replicating it.

989
01:50:04,494 --> 01:50:13,632
So you can have the primary write database and basically replicate it to a read replica one in different availability

990
01:50:13,872 --> 01:50:26,192
zone and a different replica in another availability zone. So then the user will connect to the closest available database and the search or the query will be faster in this

991
01:50:26,192 --> 01:50:39,236
case. If we imagine the reservations, let's say we are querying the reservations for this user with the specific user ID, we can have a variable which detects whether we should use the primary or not.

992
01:50:39,854 --> 01:50:47,072
If it's a recent write, then we should use the primary because the read replica might not be up to date yet.

993
01:50:47,470 --> 01:50:54,244
If that's the case, then we will get the primary connection and read it from this database here.

994
01:50:54,766 --> 01:51:01,072
But if it's not the case, if it's not a recent write, then we can basically read from the closest replica.

995
01:51:01,232 --> 01:51:08,644
We will get the replica connection and run the query against this replica instead of against the primary database.

996
01:51:09,230 --> 01:51:13,316
Next, let's see also the load balancing that we will use here.

997
01:51:13,934 --> 01:51:24,112
We can have users from very different various locations, right? We can have users from US, from Europe, from Asia, from other continents of the world.

998
01:51:24,432 --> 01:51:32,032
So the algorithm for this, of course, it makes sense to use geo latency algorithm, which is based on their region.

999
01:51:32,622 --> 01:51:39,632
Of course, there might be exceptions, maybe a US person is looking to travel to Europe, so that is a different story.

1000
01:51:39,950 --> 01:51:47,300
But in general, the users in Europe are looking to book hotels in Europe, the users in US are looking to book hotels in US.

1001
01:51:47,552 --> 01:51:51,204
So that's the algorithm that makes the most sense here.

1002
01:51:51,662 --> 01:52:00,004
And the thing that we can replicate here is based on the locations, we can have different database replicas which are read-only.

1003
01:52:00,430 --> 01:52:08,432
We can have the cache for Redis again in each location because we will probably cache the US region users'

1004
01:52:08,846 --> 01:52:15,312
hotels in this region's Redis, and it doesn't make sense to also cache the Europe hotels here.

1005
01:52:15,406 --> 01:52:18,752
So we will have it in a different replica in a different region.

1006
01:52:19,360 --> 01:52:26,596
And also the elastic search because users again are more likely from US to search for hotels within the US.

1007
01:52:27,040 --> 01:52:31,460
So we have all that data in one region which is quickly accessible.

1008
01:52:31,886 --> 01:52:37,840
And similarly, we have it for Europe, we have it for Asia and any other continents that we support.

1009
01:52:38,080 --> 01:52:47,524
But the primary database stays one here, at least even if this is one of the shards, let's say shard 7, this stays as a single database

1010
01:52:48,240 --> 01:52:55,012
to avoid having conflicts between different regions rewriting to the database, and then we have to manage the conflict.

1011
01:52:55,120 --> 01:53:03,204
So we can have one primary database since the transactions per second are not a lot, as we saw in the calculations.

1012
01:53:03,630 --> 01:53:09,444
We can have one primary chart and multiple replicas and regions for each of these replicas.

1013
01:53:10,094 --> 01:53:18,320
This is the most in-depth breakdown of designing a messaging app like WhatsApp from scratch that you will be able to find on the internet.

1014
01:53:18,862 --> 01:53:27,236
Messaging apps like this show up all the time in senior and staff level interviews, and that is because they force you to handle stateful connections.

1015
01:53:27,630 --> 01:53:31,876
think about real-time delivery, consistency and scale of the system.

1016
01:53:32,206 --> 01:53:41,120
failures of pieces of the system, or overall disaster recoveries and multi-device sync in case of users using multiple devices

1017
01:53:41,678 --> 01:53:44,000
like mobile phones and desktop apps.

1018
01:53:44,302 --> 01:53:52,160
So, in a short interview, you need to show that you understand not just the happy path, but also all the things that can go wrong in messages when

1019
01:53:52,160 --> 01:53:56,420
they are flying around between disconnected users and mobile networks.

1020
01:53:56,942 --> 01:54:04,292
And we are not going to ignore the hard parts and pretend that everything just works without us preventing the failures.

1021
01:54:04,782 --> 01:54:11,460
So we will start by first building this from scratch, starting with a simple version that works for a few thousand users.

1022
01:54:11,982 --> 01:54:17,860
Then we'll scale it up to handle billions of users sending messages across unreliable mobile networks.

1023
01:54:18,320 --> 01:54:22,756
And after that, we'll identify where it breaks and how to fix it properly.

1024
01:54:23,310 --> 01:54:34,212
At the end, you'll also find the full set of footnotes explaining every technical concept that I mentioned while designing this, so you can revisit anything and you can go deeper on each topic.

1025
01:54:34,720 --> 01:54:42,276
Before getting to the high-level design and sketching diagrams, we need to start with the requirements to understand what we are building here.

1026
01:54:42,766 --> 01:54:49,988
So the core requirements that we have is that users need to be able to send and receive messages in one-on-one setup.

1027
01:54:50,510 --> 01:54:56,676
So on top of that, we also have group chats with up to 100 participants that we need to support.

1028
01:54:56,974 --> 01:55:02,000
And messages are delivered not only when users are online but also when they are offline.

1029
01:55:02,160 --> 01:55:04,292
We need to handle that scenario as well.

1030
01:55:05,006 --> 01:55:10,560
They can attach media files like images, videos, or audio clips like voice notes.

1031
01:55:10,800 --> 01:55:19,812
We need to track the statuses of messages like when they are sent, when they are delivered, and when the message is read from the second user.

1032
01:55:20,302 --> 01:55:27,812
And of course, we need to track the online offline status to show them the last scene date of when the user was online.

1033
01:55:28,398 --> 01:55:32,196
These are the core requirements when it comes to non-functional requirements.

1034
01:55:32,480 --> 01:55:38,980
We need to provide low latency delivery, so under 500 milliseconds when users are online.

1035
01:55:39,790 --> 01:55:46,564
We also need guaranteed message delivery, so of course, messages can't just disappear and not reach to the second user.

1036
01:55:47,118 --> 01:55:51,588
In the scaling phase, we need to handle billions of users with high throughput.

1037
01:55:52,174 --> 01:55:58,212
And to keep the storage manageable, we don't store messages on servers longer than they are necessary.

1038
01:55:58,830 --> 01:56:02,688
So it needs to also stay resilient when individual components fail.

1039
01:56:02,848 --> 01:56:09,328
Let's say one of the components fails, like the server that needs to push the message to the second user.

1040
01:56:09,488 --> 01:56:16,100
We need to handle these scenarios as well to make sure that the user eventually receives the message when the server is back online.

1041
01:56:16,622 --> 01:56:24,164
With that, we can do some back of the envelope calculations to understand the scale of the system, and then we'll start with the high-level design.

1042
01:56:24,814 --> 01:56:26,276
Let's start with user metrics.

1043
01:56:26,608 --> 01:56:33,156
We have at the scale phase 1 billion registered users and 500 million daily active users.

1044
01:56:33,486 --> 01:56:43,876
During peak hours, it will be handling 50 million concurrent connections, and on average we will expect to have 10 to 20 messages per user daily.

1045
01:56:44,206 --> 01:56:46,916
With this, we can start the message estimations.

1046
01:56:47,168 --> 01:56:56,836
For example, for the daily messages, we need to multiply the active users with the average daily messages that will be sent, which is 20 at max,

1047
01:56:57,294 --> 01:57:05,572
and that will be 10 billion messages sent every day in the system, which means 10 billion writes to the database every day.

1048
01:57:06,126 --> 01:57:14,128
That comes down to 115,000 messages per second on average, so 115,000 writes to the database.

1049
01:57:14,350 --> 01:57:23,568
And during peak hours, we can expect to get 3 to 5 times more of the traffic than on the average, which will be 350 to

1050
01:57:23,728 --> 01:57:27,524
500,000 users' writes on average per second.

1051
01:57:28,142 --> 01:57:30,448
Now let's also estimate the storage.

1052
01:57:30,638 --> 01:57:36,388
So each message with metadata, let's just consider it to be 1 kilobytes.

1053
01:57:36,622 --> 01:57:44,932
It should include the text content, the server and receiver IDs, the timestamp when the message was sent, and the status flux.

1054
01:57:45,262 --> 01:57:54,948
Since we have 10 billion messages per day, it will be 10 billion times the 1 kilobytes and we will be storing 10 terabytes of storage per day for the messages.

1055
01:57:55,662 --> 01:58:03,620
And on annual basis, this comes down to 3.6 petabytes per year, which is 1000TB for each petabyte.

1056
01:58:04,302 --> 01:58:14,724
And the bandwidth will be 50 million concurrent connections at the peak traffic time, and each connection will average out to 10 kilobytes per second for active messages,

1057
01:58:15,088 --> 01:58:19,300
which comes down to 500 gigabytes per second of bandwidth.

1058
01:58:19,648 --> 01:58:28,676
One important thing here is that most messages get delivered and cleared from the server within seconds, so we are not going to store this

1059
01:58:28,848 --> 01:58:29,888
everything forever.

1060
01:58:30,128 --> 01:58:41,956
Users download the messages to their devices and also backup if they want to, for example, to Google Drive, and then we can clean up the server site to not store any unnecessary data.

1061
01:58:42,254 --> 01:58:50,308
Here in the beginning of requirements, we have to set it to be stored for 30-day retention for undelivered messages,

1062
01:58:50,688 --> 01:58:54,692
and maybe let's say 10% of users keeping history on servers.

1063
01:58:55,118 --> 01:59:02,756
So this allows WhatsApp to run on minimal infrastructure because they are very efficient about not sending the unnecessary data.

1064
01:59:03,022 --> 01:59:09,828
Now with this row of calculations, we can start with a very high-level design of what the system will look like.

1065
01:59:10,126 --> 01:59:18,052
Let's start simple and add complexity only when we will scale to more users, but for now this is the initial setup.

1066
01:59:18,638 --> 01:59:22,212
We will have the mobile app, which is the client, like the user's phone.

1067
01:59:22,638 --> 01:59:33,956
It is responsible to maintain a consistent connection with the chat server, handle the UI, and manage the local message storage and also retries when failed operations happen.

1068
01:59:34,414 --> 01:59:40,452
The next piece is the load balancer, which sits between the mobile app and the chat servers.

1069
01:59:40,848 --> 01:59:49,252
This will be responsible for routing the incoming traffic between multiple chat servers to handle the peak load and traffic.

1070
01:59:49,966 --> 01:59:58,404
These chat servers will maintain a WebSocket connection to the client persistent two-way communication channel that will stay open

1071
01:59:58,768 --> 02:00:04,740
and they will route messages between users and track who's online and handle messages persistence.

1072
02:00:05,166 --> 02:00:11,300
Then we'll introduce a message queue which will be for decoupling message writing from delivery.

1073
02:00:11,408 --> 02:00:15,908
And this is also one of the things that will allow us to handle the failures.

1074
02:00:16,910 --> 02:00:25,796
So when one of the chat servers receives the message, it will immediately acknowledge and tell the mobile app that the message was sent,

1075
02:00:25,936 --> 02:00:31,812
and then it asynchronously pushes the message to the message queue for storage and delivery.

1076
02:00:32,016 --> 02:00:38,016
And as an example, this can be either a Kafka message queue or RabbitMQ that will work here.

1077
02:00:38,222 --> 02:00:47,748
Now this message storage will consume that message that is coming from the server, from the chat server, and persist it to the message storage database.

1078
02:00:48,096 --> 02:00:58,576
As a database solution, NoSQL will work better here, something like Cassandra or DynamoDB, because we need high write throughput, as you saw in the estimations,

1079
02:00:58,816 --> 02:01:01,856
and our query patterns are mostly straightforward.

1080
02:01:02,016 --> 02:01:06,736
We will be fetching messages by user, by conversation, or by timestamp.

1081
02:01:06,896 --> 02:01:14,416
And since we're writing billions of messages per day, we need something that can handle the massive write volume without slowing down,

1082
02:01:14,496 --> 02:01:19,396
and that's why like something like Cassandra or DynamoDB is the best fit here.

1083
02:01:19,822 --> 02:01:29,444
Next, we'll need also some user connection cache, something like Redis, which will be tracking which users are online, which chat server they are connected to,

1084
02:01:29,616 --> 02:01:31,652
and their last activity timestamp.

1085
02:01:32,622 --> 02:01:43,936
This will help these chat servers to make the routing decisions faster because checking this Redis storage takes something like microseconds versus querying it from the database,

1086
02:01:44,016 --> 02:01:45,412
which will take milliseconds.

1087
02:01:45,696 --> 02:01:48,772
And at scale that difference really starts to show up.

1088
02:01:49,262 --> 02:01:56,976
In the requirements, we also have the requirement to store media files like messages with voice notes or with images.

1089
02:01:57,166 --> 02:01:59,812
For that, we will use some sort of blob storage.

1090
02:02:00,622 --> 02:02:09,316
This can be like the AWSS3 or Google Cloud storage, and this will be storing the media files and all the actual files.

1091
02:02:09,934 --> 02:02:17,668
But then we will also distribute this to a CDN network which will cache the popular files at edge locations worldwide

1092
02:02:18,094 --> 02:02:26,468
so that downloads are fast regardless of where the users are, because one user might be in US and the second user might be in Europe.

1093
02:02:26,976 --> 02:02:32,068
And in this case, you will have the same copy of the file in Europe region as well.

1094
02:02:32,416 --> 02:02:37,700
And for the Europe user, he will download it from the CDN, which is closer to his location.

1095
02:02:38,574 --> 02:02:40,964
Next, we need also a notification service.

1096
02:02:41,136 --> 02:02:49,936
This is mainly for the offline users which need to receive the notifications either through Apple push notification service or Firebase

1097
02:02:50,096 --> 02:02:51,780
Cloud Messaging for Android.

1098
02:02:52,270 --> 02:03:01,348
And when someone messages you while you are offline, like let's say user A messages to user B and then user B is offline at the moment,

1099
02:03:01,646 --> 02:03:07,972
they will receive instead the push notification and then later they can read the message when they open the app.

1100
02:03:08,334 --> 02:03:16,256
And lastly, we need also a dedicated presence service which will be used for managing online and offline statuses

1101
02:03:16,416 --> 02:03:17,056
of the users.

1102
02:03:17,216 --> 02:03:22,084
Let's say user A again is online and user B is offline.

1103
02:03:22,496 --> 02:03:32,256
This presence server will receive the heartbeats from users, updates from the Redis, and also publish the presence changes to the interested subscribers

1104
02:03:32,416 --> 02:03:37,380
like the user A, which is interested whether the user B is online or offline.

1105
02:03:38,030 --> 02:03:44,196
Now let's choose the communication method between the mobile app and the services, the chat servers.

1106
02:03:44,416 --> 02:03:49,252
We have three options here polling, long polling, and WebSocket communication.

1107
02:03:49,710 --> 02:03:58,084
Of course, most messaging apps use WebSocket for communication, but let's understand why is that the case by looking at their pros and cons.

1108
02:03:58,510 --> 02:04:00,656
Let's start with the polling option.

1109
02:04:00,846 --> 02:04:04,932
Let's say you have the client on this side and then chat server on that side.

1110
02:04:05,520 --> 02:04:08,400
With regular polling option, this is how it works.

1111
02:04:08,560 --> 02:04:16,880
We will do periodic polling from the front end to backend asking whether they have any new messages or not with some API endpoint.

1112
02:04:17,198 --> 02:04:24,720
And then let's say we don't have any new messages, so this will be an empty response saying that there are no new messages in the server.

1113
02:04:25,038 --> 02:04:31,492
And then from some time again, we will also need to query the same thing and ask whether there are any new messages.

1114
02:04:31,680 --> 02:04:37,892
And then let's say for the second time there are new messages, so they will receive the messages that they received.

1115
02:04:38,240 --> 02:04:47,204
This, as you can see, wastes a lot of bandwidth and adds latency because if you're checking every two seconds and the message arrives right after you check,

1116
02:04:47,520 --> 02:04:51,012
you wait two seconds to see it while you could get it instantly.

1117
02:04:51,566 --> 02:04:59,716
And if you also multiply that by the millions of users constantly asking for updates, even when there is nothing new in the chat server,

1118
02:05:00,046 --> 02:05:02,788
you're burning a lot of resources for no reason.

1119
02:05:03,120 --> 02:05:07,268
That's why one better approach than this is the long polling option.

1120
02:05:07,726 --> 02:05:11,120
In this case, we wait for the new messages or timeout.

1121
02:05:11,406 --> 02:05:19,040
So let's say we request the new messages from the server, and then the chat server will wait for some time until the timeout.

1122
02:05:19,280 --> 02:05:22,980
If there are any new messages, it will push them back to the client.

1123
02:05:23,438 --> 02:05:33,188
And if there are no new messages, then after some timeout, it will push the response, which will be again an empty response, saying that there are no new messages in the chat.

1124
02:05:33,360 --> 02:05:40,260
And if there are no new messages, then this needs to again query to the chat server, and then basically this process occurs again.

1125
02:05:40,880 --> 02:05:48,960
This is still problematic because you're constantly opening and closing connections, and each message requires a full HTTP handshake.

1126
02:05:49,120 --> 02:05:57,828
At scale, this is again an overhead because your chat servers spend more time managing connection lifecycles than actually delivering messages.

1127
02:05:58,286 --> 02:06:09,828
Plus, there will be some idle HTTP connections like in this one where they are waiting for the response, and they will consume the server resources like memory for connection state

1128
02:06:10,080 --> 02:06:14,916
and thread pool slots without the efficiency benefits of WebSockets.

1129
02:06:15,726 --> 02:06:20,036
That's why most of the chat services work with WebSocket communication.

1130
02:06:20,320 --> 02:06:26,212
This is one persistent bidirectional connection between the chat server and between the mobile app.

1131
02:06:26,670 --> 02:06:30,080
So client and server can both push data at any time.

1132
02:06:30,414 --> 02:06:37,380
First client will initiate the HTTP connection with the server, and then they will perform the WebSocket handshake.

1133
02:06:37,760 --> 02:06:43,524
And after this point, the connection is established and it is persistent throughout the session.

1134
02:06:43,886 --> 02:06:48,740
This way, let's say user A sends a message to user B, which is this user.

1135
02:06:49,230 --> 02:06:59,680
Server is able to independently push the new messages to the user B, which received the message, so push the new messages as soon as they are available.

1136
02:06:59,920 --> 02:07:07,556
And also the client, which is the user B, is able to send message or acknowledge anytime without making a new connection.

1137
02:07:08,238 --> 02:07:16,740
And this connection is persistent and allows real-time bidirectional updates both from the chat server side and from the client side.

1138
02:07:17,358 --> 02:07:22,756
The trade-offs of WebSockets is that it requires special load balancers that support them.

1139
02:07:23,182 --> 02:07:26,948
So we'll need to use layer 4 load balancing instead of layer 7.

1140
02:07:27,374 --> 02:07:35,280
They also use more memory per connection on the server side because you're keeping connections open, but it's still worth it because of the real

1141
02:07:35,440 --> 02:07:39,012
time messaging and the latency of the message requirements.

1142
02:07:39,598 --> 02:07:45,668
Next, let's see how we are going to manage the chat servers which are routed through the load balancer.

1143
02:07:46,606 --> 02:07:53,700
In production and in reality, chat servers come and go, they will crash, get deployed again, scale up and down.

1144
02:07:54,096 --> 02:07:57,348
And how does the system keep track of what's available?

1145
02:07:57,998 --> 02:08:07,216
So the problem here is that the load balancer needs some way to know which servers are available, like chat server 1 and 2 are available at this moment,

1146
02:08:07,376 --> 02:08:14,116
and chat server 3 is unhealthy. Let's say it either throws timeouts or it's throwing errors on requests.

1147
02:08:14,510 --> 02:08:23,364
So one solution that we can use here is service discovery cluster, like a service registry similar to console or AWS Cloud Map.

1148
02:08:23,822 --> 02:08:25,156
And here is how it will work.

1149
02:08:25,422 --> 02:08:35,684
Whenever these chat servers start, let's say this server one boots up on some IP address, it will register itself in this service discovery cluster.

1150
02:08:36,016 --> 02:08:44,292
And after that, we will do health checks every 10 seconds with this server one to see if the server is up and running.

1151
02:08:44,910 --> 02:08:48,740
And then similarly, we'll do that for server 2 and server 3.

1152
02:08:49,230 --> 02:08:53,616
And now let's say the load balancer needs the list of available servers.

1153
02:08:53,776 --> 02:09:02,788
It will first query the healthy servers from the service discovery cluster, and then the cluster will send the server list to the load balancer.

1154
02:09:03,278 --> 02:09:08,868
With this new list, let's say we forwarded only the IP address of the chat server 1 and 2.

1155
02:09:09,294 --> 02:09:21,376
So the load balancer will be able to update the routing table and then route all the traffic only to server 1 and to server 2, and it will know that server 3 is removed from the routing,

1156
02:09:21,536 --> 02:09:24,644
so we are not making any requests to the server 3.

1157
02:09:24,896 --> 02:09:28,736
That's how we will manage the load balancing and traffic routing.

1158
02:09:28,910 --> 02:09:35,396
Now let's move on to the data model and designing the data tables that we will have, the primary ones.

1159
02:09:35,982 --> 02:09:44,388
We will first need a user's table, and this one can be actually stored in a SQL database like PostgreSQL or MySQL.

1160
02:09:44,974 --> 02:09:53,696
So the user will have an ID which will be the primary key, then a phone number, which needs to be unique for all users, then a username

1161
02:09:53,936 --> 02:10:03,376
created at which is a timestamp, and last scene, which we will use for controlling and showing to the other users when this user was last online.

1162
02:10:03,854 --> 02:10:08,740
And of course, more fields will be here, but these are let's say the primary ones that we will need.

1163
02:10:09,166 --> 02:10:17,696
The next table will be messages, which is the table in Cassandra actually, where we will be storing messages since this is a high

1164
02:10:17,856 --> 02:10:22,340
volume database we will be writing and reading a lot of messages from.

1165
02:10:22,958 --> 02:10:29,700
We will need the sender ID here, which is the connection with the user ID from the users table.

1166
02:10:30,158 --> 02:10:39,172
We will need the receiver ID, which is again a connection to the same field in the users, and a message ID, which will be the primary key for this table.

1167
02:10:39,536 --> 02:10:45,936
Apart from that, any other things that relate with the message like the content, which is the text, media URL.

1168
02:10:46,016 --> 02:10:54,496
if we are storing any voice notes or any image video files, the status of the message, which will be either sent, delivered

1169
02:10:54,736 --> 02:10:55,332
or read,

1170
02:10:56,110 --> 02:11:03,908
the timestamp of when this message was delivered, and whether it's deleted or not, because they are also able to delete messages on WhatsApp.

1171
02:11:04,336 --> 02:11:13,444
For managing the groups on WhatsApp, we will also need groups and group members tables, and this can be again in the PostgreSQL database.

1172
02:11:13,998 --> 02:11:22,496
For the groups, we will need ID for each group, which will be the primary key which we will use for identifying the group, the name of the group,

1173
02:11:22,656 --> 02:11:26,116
when it was created, and who's the user that created this group.

1174
02:11:26,798 --> 02:11:37,636
And then, this is a one-to-many connection with the groups and group members where we will be adding new members, and this is the ID of the group where they are added,

1175
02:11:38,256 --> 02:11:47,076
the role of each member when they joined, and the user ID, which links to the users table. User ID, and that's the primary key of users.

1176
02:11:47,456 --> 02:11:56,292
Apart from this, one more important thing is to have user connections registry where let's say this can be stored in a cache like Redis.

1177
02:11:56,590 --> 02:12:04,964
This will store the user IDs and the server ID where they are connected to, also the connection ID if it is open,

1178
02:12:05,376 --> 02:12:07,556
last active time and device type.

1179
02:12:08,270 --> 02:12:16,420
And we can add this to the Redis cache and then update every let's say 300 seconds to refresh it with each heart grid.

1180
02:12:17,006 --> 02:12:26,052
Once we have the database ROV schema, we can move on to the API design, specifically for WebSocket and REST API flows for the messaging app.

1181
02:12:26,734 --> 02:12:34,340
The first step from the client will be to authenticate with the server by sending the user ID, the token and device information.

1182
02:12:34,862 --> 02:12:42,800
If it is successful, the server will respond with off success, sending the session ID, the server time, and the unread

1183
02:12:42,928 --> 02:12:43,204
count.

1184
02:12:43,488 --> 02:12:47,488
After that point, the client can start sending and receiving messages.

1185
02:12:47,648 --> 02:12:55,728
Let's say it sends a message to the receiver, so it will send the information about who's the receiver, the user ID, the content,

1186
02:12:55,968 --> 02:12:59,488
the media if there are any, and the timestamp of the message.

1187
02:12:59,662 --> 02:13:03,408
And at this point, the WebSocket connection is already established.

1188
02:13:03,568 --> 02:13:11,716
So there is a bi-directional communication, which means that server is also able to push messages or acknowledgments to the client.

1189
02:13:11,808 --> 02:13:15,332
So it will acknowledge, for example, when the message is received.

1190
02:13:15,822 --> 02:13:24,164
It will also deliver any new messages that there are from other users like the message ID, the sender, the content, and the media files.

1191
02:13:24,368 --> 02:13:29,328
And then client will be able to also acknowledge when they receive the messages from the server.

1192
02:13:29,614 --> 02:13:34,128
The receiving is one part, maybe they received but haven't opened the message.

1193
02:13:34,382 --> 02:13:41,412
Once they open the message, they will also send the read receipt with message ID and the timestamp when that was read.

1194
02:13:42,094 --> 02:13:51,008
Whenever let's say the message was read, the server needs to also update the read receipt with the message ID and when it was read

1195
02:13:51,168 --> 02:13:55,972
so that the sender who's on the other side also knows when the message was read.

1196
02:13:56,462 --> 02:14:04,292
Also, the client and server need to maintain the presence heartbeat between each other every let's say 5 or 10 seconds.

1197
02:14:04,528 --> 02:14:15,328
So client will check whether the server is still available, and similarly, the server needs to check whether the client is available because maybe you close the app on your phone or you close

1198
02:14:16,078 --> 02:14:21,892
the browser tab of WhatsApp, and in this case the connection needs to be closed to not waste the bandwidth.

1199
02:14:22,208 --> 02:14:26,404
For uploading the media files, we will handle this through a REST API.

1200
02:14:26,830 --> 02:14:36,048
For example, for uploading a file, we will use a post endpoint, something like slash API slash media, where we will send the file type,

1201
02:14:36,208 --> 02:14:42,628
the size of the file, and also of course the file and conversation where this file is uploaded to.

1202
02:14:43,438 --> 02:14:49,956
So this will be sent to REST API, which will be responsible for handling the media uploads.

1203
02:14:50,128 --> 02:14:54,692
And then on the other side we have also the media storage where this will be stored.

1204
02:14:54,926 --> 02:15:00,740
So server will respond with the upload URL, the media ID, and when this expires.

1205
02:15:01,230 --> 02:15:08,548
And after that, the client will be able to send the upload file to the pre-signed URL for the media storage.

1206
02:15:08,974 --> 02:15:16,292
And once upload is successful, the media storage will just respond with the upload success and the client will acknowledge it.

1207
02:15:16,878 --> 02:15:21,924
And as a last step, let's say it included the media file to be uploaded.

1208
02:15:22,208 --> 02:15:30,368
The server will just acknowledge that the message was delivered, or if there was any errors while uploading the file or sending the message,

1209
02:15:30,528 --> 02:15:35,332
it will send the message delivery failed response with the error message in the body.

1210
02:15:35,630 --> 02:15:37,888
Now let's cover the multi-device sync.

1211
02:15:37,968 --> 02:15:43,648
Let's say the user A can have three devices and user B can have two devices.

1212
02:15:43,808 --> 02:15:47,140
How are we going to sync messages between devices as well?

1213
02:15:47,598 --> 02:15:53,168
We have on this side user A's phone, user A's tablet, and user A's desktop.

1214
02:15:53,248 --> 02:15:55,748
These are the three devices for user A.

1215
02:15:56,206 --> 02:15:59,972
And on the other side we have user B's phone and user B's laptop.

1216
02:16:00,622 --> 02:16:08,720
So, first thing, let's say first interaction that happens is user A sends a message to the chat server A, and then

1217
02:16:08,860 --> 02:16:15,040
chat server will acknowledge and display the status as sent for this first message.

1218
02:16:15,374 --> 02:16:23,812
Since we agreed that we are going to store the statuses of user's availability in a Redis cache, so the chat server A

1219
02:16:24,240 --> 02:16:26,640
will then ask, where is the user B?

1220
02:16:26,800 --> 02:16:32,324
because let's say this message was sent to the user B, and then Redis responds.

1221
02:16:32,480 --> 02:16:38,500
that user B has two devices, and it is connected on server B, and server E.

1222
02:16:38,720 --> 02:16:39,908
each of these devices.

1223
02:16:40,320 --> 02:16:49,892
The other two devices of first user will be connected, let's say one of them is connected to chat server C and the second one is to chat server D.

1224
02:16:50,400 --> 02:17:00,644
So the once the chat server stores the sent message, it will also display through the chat server C, which is connected to user A's tablet.

1225
02:17:00,800 --> 02:17:07,908
It will display the sent message, and the chat server D will display the sent message for user A's desktop.

1226
02:17:08,494 --> 02:17:13,732
This is how we will sync the same message between three devices of first user.

1227
02:17:14,126 --> 02:17:21,060
Now let's see how these messages are sent to the user B and synced with the two devices that the second user has.

1228
02:17:21,582 --> 02:17:28,960
So user B's phone is connected to chat server B and user B's laptop is connected to chat server E.

1229
02:17:29,520 --> 02:17:39,588
The chat server B will read the message from database and deliver it to the phone, and this chat server will deliver the same message to the laptop.

1230
02:17:40,302 --> 02:17:48,612
And let's say, user B checks the message from the phone, so it will deliver the acknowledgement to chat server B, which sends the message to the phone,

1231
02:17:49,120 --> 02:18:02,884
after which the chat server B will mark this as delivered for the chat server A, which then it will be able to send to the first user's mobile device and update the status to delivered.

1232
02:18:03,120 --> 02:18:07,120
And next action that will happen is user B will read the message.

1233
02:18:07,214 --> 02:18:12,160
So once it reads the message, we will send the read receipt to the chat server B.

1234
02:18:12,334 --> 02:18:19,780
And then this chat server B will again forward this to the chat server A with the message read status in this case.

1235
02:18:20,238 --> 02:18:25,316
And then the chat server A will update the status, and now it will display as red.

1236
02:18:25,774 --> 02:18:30,564
And since this is red, now we need to also sync that between the user B's devices.

1237
02:18:30,800 --> 02:18:37,252
So the chat server B will mark it as red also on user B's laptop, which is here.

1238
02:18:37,646 --> 02:18:50,960
So this way the user A's tablet, mobile phone and desktop, and also user B's phone and laptop always stay synced with the latest data, whether the message was sent, whether it was delivered

1239
02:18:51,120 --> 02:18:52,068
or it was read.

1240
02:18:52,302 --> 02:19:01,412
And in case there are conflicts between multiple devices, we will use the last right wins technique with version numbers to prevent the conflicts.

1241
02:19:01,902 --> 02:19:04,720
And of course, this is not the ideal solution.

1242
02:19:04,910 --> 02:19:07,920
First of all, we need to apply optimization here.

1243
02:19:08,142 --> 02:19:15,588
For example, this can be incremental sync because sending the entire message history to a new device is expensive.

1244
02:19:15,760 --> 02:19:23,280
Let's say we are syncing with this chat server B and user's phone and also user's laptop with chat server E.

1245
02:19:23,520 --> 02:19:26,948
We don't need to send the whole history in the device.

1246
02:19:27,374 --> 02:19:35,680
So instead, the device can request messages for last 30 days, like most users only need the recent history, and older

1247
02:19:35,840 --> 02:19:44,196
messages are fetched on demand when needed. When they scroll up, the user scrolls up and then the old messages will start to load.

1248
02:19:44,640 --> 02:19:50,560
And very old messages will be archived in cold storage and they will be loaded lazily.

1249
02:19:50,720 --> 02:19:59,428
Adding this multi-device sync comes also with a trade-off because we need to support roughly double of the delivery workload.

1250
02:19:59,600 --> 02:20:02,900
Each message goes to multiple devices per users.

1251
02:20:03,566 --> 02:20:12,560
But that is an important feature of chat messaging app like WhatsApp because users demand the same messages, the same chats to be synced

1252
02:20:12,720 --> 02:20:16,004
between their mobile phones and between their desktop apps.

1253
02:20:16,526 --> 02:20:25,796
Now let's see the core flows on how messages actually move in case of users both being online and sometimes one of the users might be offline.

1254
02:20:26,382 --> 02:20:28,900
So let's start with both online version.

1255
02:20:29,326 --> 02:20:36,228
In this case, user A let's say sends the message to user B and it's connected with the chat server A.

1256
02:20:36,846 --> 02:20:40,740
Message goes through WebSocket and reaches to this chat server.

1257
02:20:41,294 --> 02:20:49,508
Now this chat server will generate the message ID, which is a unique ID timestamp based for this message,

1258
02:20:49,966 --> 02:20:54,820
and then it will send the acknowledgement with status of the message being sent.

1259
02:20:55,470 --> 02:20:59,588
Then this server will push the message to the message queue

1260
02:21:00,398 --> 02:21:01,380
for persistence.

1261
02:21:01,838 --> 02:21:10,496
After that, we need to check where user B is and which server it's connected to so that we can send forward this message to user B.

1262
02:21:10,798 --> 02:21:15,376
And we can check that from the Redis cache where we have the user connection cache.

1263
02:21:15,616 --> 02:21:19,044
We will read where the user B is from this cache.

1264
02:21:19,438 --> 02:21:23,296
And then let's say it responds that user B is on server B.

1265
02:21:23,376 --> 02:21:30,884
And once we have that information, the server A can route message to server B via Redis PubSub model.

1266
02:21:31,534 --> 02:21:36,080
And on this side we have the server B and user B connected with WebSocket.

1267
02:21:36,416 --> 02:21:43,684
So server B will now deliver message in real time, and user B's device will acknowledge the delivery.

1268
02:21:44,078 --> 02:21:50,212
Next we need to let the first server know that the message was forwarded and delivered.

1269
02:21:50,416 --> 02:21:59,616
So server B will make a request to the server A acknowledging the forward delivery, and then we can update the status with

1270
02:21:59,856 --> 02:22:01,216
delivered instead of sent.

1271
02:22:01,536 --> 02:22:09,636
And then whenever user B opens the message, this same process will repeat and we will now mark the message as read instead.

1272
02:22:09,936 --> 02:22:20,068
And optionally we can also enqueue the message for analytics or archival by using async processing between server A and between the message queue.

1273
02:22:20,336 --> 02:22:29,060
And meanwhile, the message queue will be writing the message to the message database like Cassandra for storing the history.

1274
02:22:29,614 --> 02:22:37,668
Now let's see this same flow, but in case when the user B is offline, which makes it a bit harder to manage,

1275
02:22:38,318 --> 02:22:46,276
but still we need to make sure that the message is received and we also notify the user B when they received, even when they were offline.

1276
02:22:46,798 --> 02:22:50,416
So the initial steps are the same for this situation.

1277
02:22:50,736 --> 02:22:58,816
User A sends the message to user B through chat server A, and then we check whether the user B is online

1278
02:22:59,056 --> 02:23:00,644
from the Redis cache.

1279
02:23:00,976 --> 02:23:10,404
And in case the user B is offline, which is our situation here, we will add the message to user B's inbox with Redis,

1280
02:23:10,926 --> 02:23:15,236
and then we will store this in inbox table in the database inbox.

1281
02:23:15,936 --> 02:23:24,816
And after that, this chat server will connect with the notification service that we have here and send push notifications for user B.

1282
02:23:24,976 --> 02:23:33,796
Once the notification service receives the message, it also triggers push notification through Apple's or Google's notification system.

1283
02:23:34,382 --> 02:23:39,460
And then we enqueue the message for further processing to the message queue.

1284
02:23:39,936 --> 02:23:43,780
And after that, message queue will persist this into the database.

1285
02:23:44,496 --> 02:23:52,896
And once the user B comes back online, let's say they open the app and establish the WebSocket connection with chat server

1286
02:23:53,998 --> 02:24:01,316
we will forward the message for delivery to the chat server C, which has the WebSocket connection with user B.

1287
02:24:01,696 --> 02:24:06,212
And after that, we will deliver the message in real time to the user B's device.

1288
02:24:07,630 --> 02:24:18,052
In case user B was online during this time, we would just forward the message for delivery to the chat server where the user B is connected. Let's say it's the chat server C.

1289
02:24:18,446 --> 02:24:25,540
But if user B was offline first and now they become available, this is the flow that we will have.

1290
02:24:25,902 --> 02:24:29,616
So let's say user B comes online after being offline for a while.

1291
02:24:29,776 --> 02:24:36,576
We already sent the push notification and they connect to one of the chat servers, like chat server C.

1292
02:24:36,750 --> 02:24:43,332
First thing we will do from this server is we will retrieve the pending messages from the inbox from Redis.

1293
02:24:43,936 --> 02:24:55,300
And if there are any messages that are pending, we will retrieve them, any missed messages from the database inbox, and we will deliver the pending messages to user B.

1294
02:24:55,726 --> 02:25:01,668
And similar to the previous flow, we will also notify the user A that the message was read.

1295
02:25:02,158 --> 02:25:11,332
Next, we need to design this for group messages as well and see how we can handle 50 to 100 person groups in WhatsApp.

1296
02:25:11,696 --> 02:25:14,096
This is how it will look like at a high level.

1297
02:25:14,190 --> 02:25:17,412
Let's say we have multiple users in the same group chat.

1298
02:25:17,966 --> 02:25:22,576
We will first of all need a group service which will be managing these groups.

1299
02:25:22,766 --> 02:25:31,492
And as an example, let's say user A is part of this group, and we have another 50 to 100 persons in this group.

1300
02:25:32,110 --> 02:25:36,656
First flow is when user A sends a message to the group.

1301
02:25:36,910 --> 02:25:41,252
Let's say for each group we have ID as we described in the data model.

1302
02:25:41,696 --> 02:25:45,444
This gets sent to the chat server where they are connected.

1303
02:25:46,030 --> 02:25:55,844
And then the chat server, first request it will make to the group service, is to check who's in this group, which is ID of 789 as an example.

1304
02:25:56,096 --> 02:26:02,976
And it will retrieve the list of users, like user IDs who are in this group, part of this group.

1305
02:26:03,216 --> 02:26:14,532
And after that, we can check the status of these 50 users. We can use Redis to cache the status of users to not waste the database queries for reading this.

1306
02:26:14,990 --> 02:26:23,616
And let's say we check the status of the 50 users and find out that 30 of them are online and the rest, the 20 other users,

1307
02:26:23,776 --> 02:26:24,420
are offline.

1308
02:26:25,006 --> 02:26:29,764
For each online user, we need to look up which chat server they are on.

1309
02:26:30,126 --> 02:26:38,176
So we will publish to channel user 1, user 2 to user 30 to the Redis pop sub model.

1310
02:26:38,670 --> 02:26:42,496
And then this will parallelly deliver to online users.

1311
02:26:42,656 --> 02:26:48,496
Whoever is online, they will receive their messages in their own chat server where they are connected.

1312
02:26:48,686 --> 02:26:55,536
Let's say 15 of the users are connected to server B and the other 15 are connected to server C.

1313
02:26:55,696 --> 02:27:02,916
We will in parallel distribute the message to both servers, and then these servers will distribute them via WebSockets.

1314
02:27:03,296 --> 02:27:12,164
So it will deliver via WebSocket to the 15 users from server B and to the other 15 users from server C.

1315
02:27:12,686 --> 02:27:23,396
For each of these deliveries, they will acknowledge from user's device that the message was delivered, and same for the other 15 who are connected to the other server,

1316
02:27:23,822 --> 02:27:31,044
and then we will forward these acknowledgements to the server A, which originally sent the message.

1317
02:27:31,534 --> 02:27:38,820
So that it can update the status of the message to send or delivered in the user A's device.

1318
02:27:39,216 --> 02:27:48,120
For the other 20 users who are offline, we need to use the same thing generally that we did for the offline users in one-on-one

1319
02:27:48,256 --> 02:27:48,804
connection.

1320
02:27:49,390 --> 02:28:00,388
We'll need a Redis inbox where we will write the messages that they need to receive, which is let's say these other 20 users who were offline, we will write it to user inboxes.

1321
02:28:00,750 --> 02:28:05,636
And we will write to 20 inbox records in the database inbox.

1322
02:28:06,606 --> 02:28:15,456
And for all of them, we need to send the push notification either through Apple's or Google's push notification system to notify them through

1323
02:28:15,662 --> 02:28:19,696
notification service that the message was sent in the group.

1324
02:28:20,014 --> 02:28:26,212
And then the notification service will push this through either Apple's or Google's notification system.

1325
02:28:27,150 --> 02:28:29,572
So this way we are just storing message once.

1326
02:28:29,696 --> 02:28:38,016
So we are doing one write operation from chat server A, which received the message from the user to the message queue.

1327
02:28:38,096 --> 02:28:40,256
And then message queue writes this to the database.

1328
02:28:40,398 --> 02:28:43,652
So eventually it is just one write operation per message.

1329
02:28:43,936 --> 02:28:53,376
And lastly, we will also update the status that it was delivered to 30 and it is pending to 20 users for the user A's device.

1330
02:28:53,806 --> 02:29:00,292
Now, so far we haven't covered how we are going to manage the media files, the image assets, the videos.

1331
02:29:00,416 --> 02:29:02,148
So let's cover that next.

1332
02:29:02,606 --> 02:29:12,816
Of course, the wrong way here will be to clients upload the image to the chat server and then chat server store it in database and then recipient downloads from

1333
02:29:12,976 --> 02:29:13,668
chat server.

1334
02:29:13,998 --> 02:29:15,616
This is of course not scalable.

1335
02:29:15,776 --> 02:29:24,164
Your chat servers are now bandwidth limited by file transfers, and they are not doing what they are good at, which is routing messages quickly,

1336
02:29:24,398 --> 02:29:28,996
and instead they are doing what they are terrible at, which is moving large blobs around.

1337
02:29:29,550 --> 02:29:32,964
So the right approach is using pre-signed URLs.

1338
02:29:33,056 --> 02:29:41,856
And for all these technical terms like pre-signed URLs, notification services, Apple's notification service, these kind of things will be included in the footnotes.

1339
02:29:41,998 --> 02:29:45,284
If you're not sure what they are, feel free to check it there.

1340
02:29:45,870 --> 02:29:49,284
So let's look at the correct approach for uploading the media files.

1341
02:29:49,456 --> 02:29:51,332
Let's start with the upload flow.

1342
02:29:51,470 --> 02:30:01,444
Let's say we have user A, who's the sender of the file on this site, and we have user A's device, which is the app, and we have user B who will be receiving the file.

1343
02:30:02,158 --> 02:30:10,976
So, user A taps to send a message, and this is where the application will request pre-signed upload URL from the chat server

1344
02:30:11,136 --> 02:30:14,276
which they are connected to. Let's say this is the server A.

1345
02:30:14,734 --> 02:30:23,136
At this step, the chat server will generate pre-signed upload URL to upload this into some blob storage like AWSS3.

1346
02:30:23,630 --> 02:30:31,616
The blob storage will return the upload URL to the chat server, and then we will forward this URL to user A's application.

1347
02:30:31,822 --> 02:30:37,296
Once they have this upload URL, the user A's application can do a direct upload.

1348
02:30:37,456 --> 02:30:45,696
It can upload the media using the pre-signed URL we just got from the server, and this will upload it directly to the S3 bucket.

1349
02:30:45,902 --> 02:30:51,908
And then S3 will confirm that upload and return the media URL for accessing it later.

1350
02:30:52,270 --> 02:31:01,296
And in the next step, we can already send that chat message, including the media file URL, to the chat server A, which we are connected to.

1351
02:31:01,518 --> 02:31:08,548
And this is where we need to notify the user B that they received the message and also include this media file URL.

1352
02:31:09,038 --> 02:31:14,736
So it will deliver the message with media URL to user B's application.

1353
02:31:15,022 --> 02:31:17,796
So first user B will receive the notification.

1354
02:31:18,542 --> 02:31:26,276
Next, they will connect to the chat server. Let's say this is the chat server B that they are connected to with via web sockets.

1355
02:31:26,576 --> 02:31:30,500
So they will receive the messages along with the media file URL.

1356
02:31:31,056 --> 02:31:33,776
And now then they tap to view the media.

1357
02:31:33,936 --> 02:31:37,156
This is where we will request the pre-signed download URL.

1358
02:31:37,616 --> 02:31:45,988
And in this case, we will generate the pre-signed download URL from the chat server B with the blob storage that we have on S3.

1359
02:31:46,446 --> 02:31:54,948
And then S3 will return the download URL to this chat server B, which we can now forward to the user B's application

1360
02:31:55,296 --> 02:31:57,988
for it to open and review the media file.

1361
02:31:58,336 --> 02:32:02,692
And then here we have two approaches for downloading and reading the media file.

1362
02:32:03,056 --> 02:32:10,916
The more scalable approach is via CDNs, so we can replicate the media files across CDNs to be closer to each user.

1363
02:32:11,406 --> 02:32:15,076
And in this case, we will download the media file through CDN.

1364
02:32:15,216 --> 02:32:23,524
So this is the user application and this is the CDN, and they will read it from there and stream the media back to the user application.

1365
02:32:24,110 --> 02:32:28,016
And the simplest approach is to just read it directly from the storage.

1366
02:32:28,174 --> 02:32:33,124
So the user will request to download the media file directly from the S3 bucket.

1367
02:32:33,376 --> 02:32:35,056
It will stream back the media.

1368
02:32:35,182 --> 02:32:40,484
And of course, here we have a trade-off between directly reading from the storage or from CDN.

1369
02:32:40,718 --> 02:32:47,620
We have slightly more complex flow with CDNs because we need to do extra requests to get the pre-signed URL.

1370
02:32:48,016 --> 02:32:52,336
But the performance and cost benefits here are massive for this scale.

1371
02:32:52,416 --> 02:33:02,336
So CDN is most likely the best solution and option to go with because without this, a single chat server handling 100,000 let's say connections

1372
02:33:02,496 --> 02:33:10,496
can get crushed by just 100 users simultaneously uploading some small megabyte files into the blob

1373
02:33:10,656 --> 02:33:11,012
storage.

1374
02:33:11,566 --> 02:33:18,116
So with this setup, the chat servers like the server A only handles the small text and messages.

1375
02:33:18,542 --> 02:33:27,296
And whenever we need to upload some heavy files like videos, images, or voice notes, we will offload that heavy bandwidth to some blob

1376
02:33:27,456 --> 02:33:29,188
storage or to some CDN.

1377
02:33:29,614 --> 02:33:34,596
And we will use presigned URLs which will expire after the TTL that we will set.

1378
02:33:35,182 --> 02:33:41,220
And then the media TTL will align also with the retention policy that we have in the chat servers.

1379
02:33:41,616 --> 02:33:49,572
So with this setup, chat servers only route small messages with URLs, and blob storage and CDS will handle the heavy bandwidth.

1380
02:33:50,016 --> 02:33:57,456
and our chat servers stay fast and focused on their job, which is delivering the messages, and media files.

1381
02:33:57,456 --> 02:34:08,900
get the TTL, which stands for time to live, with automatic expiration of, let's say 30 days, which will match the message retention, and then they will get automatically deleted.

1382
02:34:09,614 --> 02:34:17,700
Now let's get into how we would scale this because our chat services now can handle thousands of users concurrently,

1383
02:34:18,192 --> 02:34:24,324
but if we want to scale it even further, we need to think of a way to decouple our servers.

1384
02:34:24,912 --> 02:34:30,372
And the most common approach here is using some sort of message queue like a Kafka.

1385
02:34:30,672 --> 02:34:37,712
For our setup, we can have message queue on this side, and then we can assign partitions to each instance over here.

1386
02:34:37,872 --> 02:34:40,644
We have the message storage service instances.

1387
02:34:40,832 --> 02:34:45,392
Let's say we scale it up to 2, 3, or even more instances.

1388
02:34:45,552 --> 02:34:51,300
Since this is like a stateless instance, we can scale it to as much, as many as needed.

1389
02:34:51,854 --> 02:34:55,232
So we can assign partitions to each of these instances.

1390
02:34:55,312 --> 02:35:05,956
And as an example, like this can be from 0 to 9, this can be 10 to 19, this can be 20 to 29 partitions that we will assign to each of these instances here.

1391
02:35:06,414 --> 02:35:15,872
And the way it will work is we will have messages that arrive to this message queue, and then each instance will consume these messages from Kafka.

1392
02:35:16,032 --> 02:35:23,556
We can even do batch writes for efficiency, and then we will handle retries and failures inside of an instance.

1393
02:35:23,792 --> 02:35:27,588
So first we have the Kafka consumer in each of these instances.

1394
02:35:28,142 --> 02:35:32,932
We will try to use batch writing to write this to the database cluster.

1395
02:35:33,392 --> 02:35:43,392
If for any reason this fails, we will have a retry logic inside of each of these clusters, and then we will retry to batch write after some timeout

1396
02:35:43,712 --> 02:35:45,956
and write this to the database cluster.

1397
02:35:46,432 --> 02:35:58,116
And if for any reason this still fails, we make sure that we don't delete the message from the message queue so that we don't end up losing the messages, or we can use something which is called

1398
02:35:58,510 --> 02:35:59,552
dead letter queue.

1399
02:35:59,872 --> 02:36:03,076
These are queues that you can use separate from the main queue.

1400
02:36:03,438 --> 02:36:11,652
So whenever some of the messages here fails to be processed from the instance, it will instead be stored in this dead letter queue,

1401
02:36:11,792 --> 02:36:19,524
and then we can have some other mechanisms here for processing it later inside of the same instance and writing it to the database.

1402
02:36:20,302 --> 02:36:25,444
And then once a database write is successful, we need to also update the indexes.

1403
02:36:25,552 --> 02:36:33,552
So inside of each instance we will have the index updater, which will update the indexing in the database for searching

1404
02:36:33,712 --> 02:36:36,032
later for querying them effectively.

1405
02:36:36,272 --> 02:36:41,792
But let's look at a more detailed flow of how the processing will work from message queues.

1406
02:36:41,902 --> 02:36:48,356
So again on this left side we have the message queue which receives all the messages that arrive from user devices.

1407
02:36:49,006 --> 02:36:56,772
And then we can set some batch size like 500 messages for each batch, and we will consume this batch inside of one instance.

1408
02:36:57,294 --> 02:36:59,072
We will have the Kafka consumer.

1409
02:36:59,232 --> 02:37:07,748
This consumer can poll for new messages every let's say 100 milliseconds so that it has time to process the previous ones,

1410
02:37:08,366 --> 02:37:11,312
and then it will process the batch in parallel.

1411
02:37:11,472 --> 02:37:17,060
Let's say we can have thread pool of 20 workers and we can process this in parallel.

1412
02:37:17,518 --> 02:37:26,832
For each of these single message we will validate the schema first of all and then do metadata checks to check the size of the file.

1413
02:37:26,992 --> 02:37:33,828
If they uploaded some media file that exceeds the limit, we will need to respond with an error message.

1414
02:37:34,318 --> 02:37:40,132
And otherwise, if it passes the criteria, we will validate and enrich the schema.

1415
02:37:40,494 --> 02:37:48,832
Here is where we can calculate the message hash for deduplication, extract the mentions, like if they mentioned some username, right?

1416
02:37:49,072 --> 02:37:52,832
In the WhatsApp you can mention someone with add symbol, for example.

1417
02:37:53,006 --> 02:38:02,272
So if they mention that you need to extract those mentions from the message content, we need to identify the links for preview generation because on WhatsApp

1418
02:38:02,432 --> 02:38:07,012
you also generate the links if they attached anything to the message.

1419
02:38:07,342 --> 02:38:12,740
And this is also where we need to generate the indexes for querying them later.

1420
02:38:13,294 --> 02:38:20,804
And next, instead of writing all these messages one by one, we can queue the validated messages into a batch writer.

1421
02:38:21,424 --> 02:38:28,196
We can accumulate some batch of messages in the memory, like 1000 messages every 5 seconds.

1422
02:38:28,784 --> 02:38:35,556
And whenever this buffer hits the 1000 limit, we will start sorting messages by the target chart.

1423
02:38:35,744 --> 02:38:42,340
We will group messages by conversation and then we will prepare the batch for inserting into the database.

1424
02:38:43,054 --> 02:38:47,584
And the reason we are doing this is to save write operations in the database.

1425
02:38:47,744 --> 02:38:59,744
Of course, if we can batch 1000 messages and then try to batch, write them into the database, so we will still prepare insert statements for each of those that cannot be inserted in one

1426
02:38:59,904 --> 02:39:00,144
go.

1427
02:39:00,624 --> 02:39:05,664
but at least that saves the database some time to process the current batch.

1428
02:39:05,744 --> 02:39:09,264
and then after the five seconds, we will send it the next batch.

1429
02:39:09,358 --> 02:39:15,716
So the first happy path is that it will be inserted with atomic batch insert into the database cluster.

1430
02:39:16,144 --> 02:39:24,388
We will also update the search and analytics and indexes via index updater, and then we will mark them as processed

1431
02:39:24,624 --> 02:39:33,444
if everything goes well. But in case this fails, we will have a failure, and then we will have the retry and dead letter queue handler.

1432
02:39:34,158 --> 02:39:42,212
So if these messages or one of these messages, let's say out of this thousand we have ten of them which failed to be processed,

1433
02:39:42,638 --> 02:39:44,932
we will write them into the dead letter queue.

1434
02:39:45,504 --> 02:39:51,104
And before writing it to dead letter queue, you can have some simple logic that tries to retry.

1435
02:39:51,344 --> 02:39:56,544
Maybe that was a rate limit we hit or something else simple that we can just retry.

1436
02:39:56,782 --> 02:40:00,164
If after that point it fails, we write it to the dead letter queue.

1437
02:40:00,590 --> 02:40:09,344
And then here we will have more mechanisms like step 1, step 2, to step something like 10, which are the ways we will try,

1438
02:40:09,504 --> 02:40:19,108
retry to insert this into the database before sending the user a notification that this message was not delivered because of some reason.

1439
02:40:19,662 --> 02:40:26,596
So the main benefit that we get with dispatching is a single database insert takes 5 milliseconds.

1440
02:40:26,784 --> 02:40:35,236
If we are inserting something here, it usually takes 5 milliseconds, and all of these thousand individual inserts will

1441
02:40:35,598 --> 02:40:37,984
be equal to 5 seconds instead.

1442
02:40:39,342 --> 02:40:41,924
1 second is 1000 milliseconds.

1443
02:40:42,414 --> 02:40:50,624
But if we do one batched insert of 1000 messages, it will instead end up costing us 50 milliseconds, which is actually

1444
02:40:50,784 --> 02:41:00,516
100x throughput improvement, and it will start to show up when we are starting to write millions of messages concurrently to the database.

1445
02:41:01,230 --> 02:41:08,964
And if you'd like to see this at the code level as an example, this is how we will write to Cassandra database with the message chart.

1446
02:41:09,518 --> 02:41:12,784
So this will be a transaction. We will begin the batch.

1447
02:41:12,864 --> 02:41:22,784
We will insert the messages into the messages table, and we will pass the values like the timestamp, the ID of the sender and receiver,

1448
02:41:23,024 --> 02:41:27,620
which are the user IDs, then the content, then anything else, like media files.

1449
02:41:28,174 --> 02:41:33,988
and then similarly, we will do all the other inserts of other messages into one batch.

1450
02:41:34,384 --> 02:41:42,852
and then this will be like one batch of writing 1000 messages into the messages table in Cassandra database.

1451
02:41:43,310 --> 02:41:51,396
The next important feature of WhatsApp that you might be asked to design is to add end-to-end encryption and key management.

1452
02:41:51,790 --> 02:41:58,692
Typically on WhatsApp, messages are encrypted on the sender's device and only decrypted on the recipient's device.

1453
02:41:59,086 --> 02:42:01,744
Even WhatsApp servers can't read the content.

1454
02:42:01,824 --> 02:42:10,180
So if you have user A and user B and you're sending some messages between each other, even the user that is in the middle

1455
02:42:10,464 --> 02:42:20,064
or the user that is connected with the chat server A and then the user B which is connected to chat server B, even these A and B servers cannot read the message.

1456
02:42:20,144 --> 02:42:23,824
So, how we are going to design something like that in messaging app.

1457
02:42:24,224 --> 02:42:26,820
The thing we can use here is a signal protocol.

1458
02:42:27,024 --> 02:42:32,228
So we can add end-to-end encryption, and this is how it will work in detail.

1459
02:42:32,622 --> 02:42:40,784
Whenever user A sends a message to user B, we upload the public identity token into the key server.

1460
02:42:40,944 --> 02:42:49,904
So each user generates an identity key pair on registration on WhatsApp, and then they have the public key uploaded to the server

1461
02:42:50,064 --> 02:42:52,612
and private key which never leaves the device.

1462
02:42:52,864 --> 02:43:01,028
So you have private key, which is on user A's device, and you will have the public key which server has access to.

1463
02:43:01,966 --> 02:43:04,480
And then on the other side we have the user B.

1464
02:43:04,720 --> 02:43:09,892
So user B will again upload the public identity key to the key server.

1465
02:43:10,240 --> 02:43:12,420
Plus, they will upload pre-keys.

1466
02:43:12,942 --> 02:43:18,640
These are generated, let's say 100 signed pre-keys, and it uploads them to the server.

1467
02:43:18,894 --> 02:43:21,572
Server stores them for future initiators.

1468
02:43:22,062 --> 02:43:30,880
And whenever user A sends a message, we will also fetch the user B's keys, which includes their identity key, plus one of these pre-keys that they stored.

1469
02:43:31,040 --> 02:43:33,840
Let's say they stored something like 100 keys here.

1470
02:43:33,920 --> 02:43:36,516
We will fetch one of these for user A.

1471
02:43:36,880 --> 02:43:43,760
And then once the key server returns the user B's keys to user A, this is where the session will be established.

1472
02:43:43,950 --> 02:43:49,476
So user A fetches the user B's public identity key and pre-key from the server.

1473
02:43:49,902 --> 02:43:54,340
It generates the key pair which will match for both user A and B.

1474
02:43:54,798 --> 02:43:57,280
And then it will perform the key exchange.

1475
02:43:57,440 --> 02:44:01,220
After that, both user A and B have access to the same keys.

1476
02:44:01,440 --> 02:44:08,836
So whenever they are sending messages between each other, all of the messages will be encrypted with user A's public key.

1477
02:44:09,614 --> 02:44:16,640
And then once user B receives the message, will decrypt the message by using the user A's public key.

1478
02:44:17,006 --> 02:44:25,124
So each of these messages will be encrypted with some of the algorithms, and then we will have the message, the session key,

1479
02:44:25,360 --> 02:44:27,684
which is exchanged between these users.

1480
02:44:28,080 --> 02:44:36,388
And even further, we can use something like message counter, which will increase after each back and forth messages between this user A and B.

1481
02:44:36,718 --> 02:44:40,720
So this way it's very hard, even if the session key is stolen.

1482
02:44:40,880 --> 02:44:52,324
It will be still very hard to decrypt each of the messages because for each message we are also adding the counter for encrypting it, and then we are decrypting it on the other user's end.

1483
02:44:53,070 --> 02:45:01,892
So this is how we will ensure the privacy between user A and B, and even the chat servers we won't be able to read the messages in between.

1484
02:45:02,478 --> 02:45:07,748
Now let's move on to security practices but in terms of the overall system.

1485
02:45:08,110 --> 02:45:11,236
Let's start from rate limiting and abuse prevention.

1486
02:45:11,534 --> 02:45:18,308
Without having any rate limits, any hacker can start scraping data or perform DDOs to the system.

1487
02:45:18,638 --> 02:45:22,564
So we need to build a protection in any kind of system like this.

1488
02:45:23,374 --> 02:45:31,908
First of all, we have the user actions here, which can be messages sent from the user or files uploaded to the media storage.

1489
02:45:32,270 --> 02:45:35,428
For every action type, we need to have some protection.

1490
02:45:35,680 --> 02:45:39,440
And these are the per user limits, for example, that we can send.

1491
02:45:39,520 --> 02:45:44,932
Whenever users are sending messages or they are creating groups or they are uploading media.

1492
02:45:45,486 --> 02:45:49,636
So whenever they are sending a message, we can have a limit for sending messages.

1493
02:45:50,254 --> 02:45:52,996
We need to check first the message token bucket.

1494
02:45:53,120 --> 02:45:55,120
And here we can have the rules set.

1495
02:45:55,280 --> 02:46:05,600
For example, this can be 100 messages per minute that a user can send, and anything above that doesn't seem reasonable, so we can apply

1496
02:46:05,840 --> 02:46:07,908
rate limit for that situation.

1497
02:46:08,462 --> 02:46:12,080
And if the rate limit is exceeded, we will block the action.

1498
02:46:12,240 --> 02:46:17,220
So we will notify the user and show an error message that they exceeded the limit.

1499
02:46:17,614 --> 02:46:27,364
And this can be something like too many requests in one minute, try again from 60 seconds, for example, or we can require some CAPTCHA to be solved,

1500
02:46:27,680 --> 02:46:35,300
so these boxes that they need to check and let's say pick which ones are motorcycles to verify that they are human.

1501
02:46:35,950 --> 02:46:38,724
And then we can also have progressive penalty.

1502
02:46:38,960 --> 02:46:43,748
So the first one is the warning that we show, which is too many requests.

1503
02:46:44,000 --> 02:46:48,990
If they start abusing the service again, like we will have restriction for one hours.

1504
02:46:49,440 --> 02:46:58,180
This is how you see, for example, in passwords where you're trying to type your password for an account and you fail multiple times, they will first

1505
02:46:58,400 --> 02:47:00,720
restrict you from for some time, right?

1506
02:47:00,910 --> 02:47:04,548
The second time, they might restrict you even more, like 24 hours.

1507
02:47:04,974 --> 02:47:17,200
and then the next step after that is we will suspect the account, and we will deactivate it for some time for a human to manually review their account and verify whether this was a bot.

1508
02:47:17,200 --> 02:47:28,260
or this was a hacker trying to steal information, or it might be still the user who just didn't understand the requirements. and they kept abusing the service.

1509
02:47:28,480 --> 02:47:34,080
So we need to contact them, let them know the issue, and then they will reopen the account.

1510
02:47:34,240 --> 02:47:42,560
Of course, with the same rate limits applied, and they might even get a tag on their user that this account is suspicious, so

1511
02:47:43,054 --> 02:47:47,908
if anything happens one more time, their account might be permanently deleted from WhatsApp.

1512
02:47:48,910 --> 02:47:53,024
And now this same process repeats for all the other actions as well.

1513
02:47:53,134 --> 02:47:55,428
Let's say users can create group as well, right?

1514
02:47:55,854 --> 02:47:59,824
So once they create a group, we will check the group create counter.

1515
02:47:59,918 --> 02:48:02,864
And this is where we need to set reasonable limits.

1516
02:48:03,054 --> 02:48:08,224
For example, if they are creating five or more groups per hour, that might seem too much.

1517
02:48:08,398 --> 02:48:11,396
So we can apply a rate limit for that situation.

1518
02:48:11,904 --> 02:48:20,864
Or if they upload media and they upload more than let's say 10 or 100 media files per minute, this can be another restriction.

1519
02:48:21,038 --> 02:48:27,588
Of course, these numbers are just examples, and these are some things that will be clarified in the blueprinting phase.

1520
02:48:28,064 --> 02:48:34,852
But for each of these actions, you need to have some rate limit, and then the process is usually identical for enforcement.

1521
02:48:34,944 --> 02:48:38,788
So we need to check if the rate limit is exceeded, we will block it.

1522
02:48:39,118 --> 02:48:41,184
Otherwise, it will work as expected.

1523
02:48:41,344 --> 02:48:49,540
So we will allow and then we will continue with writing to the database, whether it's a media file or it's a message or it's creating a group.

1524
02:48:50,286 --> 02:48:55,264
This is an example code block, like how this can be implemented, the rate limiter.

1525
02:48:55,424 --> 02:49:01,828
So we will initialize with the capacity and with the refill rate, which can be different for each action.

1526
02:49:02,318 --> 02:49:06,144
And then we can have something like tokens which show the capacity.

1527
02:49:06,318 --> 02:49:12,464
So for every request, we will check whether they exceeded the tokens or they are still under the limit.

1528
02:49:12,686 --> 02:49:23,110
So it can be some unit like one, and if this is more than one, which means they haven't received the rate limit, we will return true and this will not really

1529
02:49:23,264 --> 02:49:26,628
process with other actions like storing the message to the database.

1530
02:49:27,184 --> 02:49:30,340
And then we will deduct one from each step.

1531
02:49:30,544 --> 02:49:35,424
So if they try this next time, this will be zero for the tokens.

1532
02:49:35,566 --> 02:49:45,984
So that will end up not getting into these if blocks, and then instead they will get into the else block and we will return false because they exceeded the rate limits, and this will block them.

1533
02:49:46,190 --> 02:49:47,584
This is the step here.

1534
02:49:47,744 --> 02:49:58,948
So we will block them for processing the message or creating the group, and we will notify them. And alternatively, we can also ask them to solve a CAPTCHA problem.

1535
02:49:59,694 --> 02:50:03,264
And then for that, we will set a limit right for let's say one hour.

1536
02:50:03,470 --> 02:50:05,668
So this is where we will have to refill.

1537
02:50:05,904 --> 02:50:15,744
We will check the time that we have now, we will set the last refill time, and then based on this, we will calculate when is the next time they can repeat the same action,

1538
02:50:15,904 --> 02:50:18,084
which can be from one hour from now.

1539
02:50:18,510 --> 02:50:24,324
The next action type that we need to limit is connections, because we can have connection flow the tags.

1540
02:50:24,942 --> 02:50:31,104
And let's say we get five connection attempts in the single minute for different IP addresses.

1541
02:50:31,264 --> 02:50:38,304
This is where we need to check their IP connection counter and again apply the rate limit for this specific situation.

1542
02:50:38,464 --> 02:50:47,076
So if within a minute they have tried to log in from five different IP addresses, that is something that looks suspicious.

1543
02:50:47,264 --> 02:50:54,692
So this is again where we need to apply the rate limiting and it will follow the same process as with the other actions.

1544
02:50:55,214 --> 02:50:58,532
Another example is we can have group invite limits.

1545
02:50:58,864 --> 02:51:05,264
So if they invite users, they create a group and then the user starts inviting other users to the group.

1546
02:51:05,344 --> 02:51:08,356
We will check the group invite counter in this case.

1547
02:51:09,584 --> 02:51:13,504
And similar to all the other actions, it will follow the same process.

1548
02:51:13,584 --> 02:51:17,604
Here, just the counter and the limit is different for each of the actions.

1549
02:51:19,022 --> 02:51:27,264
So for this to work, we need to also have some centralized store for all servers because each of the users is connected to one server, right,

1550
02:51:27,424 --> 02:51:28,944
with a WebSocket connection.

1551
02:51:29,184 --> 02:51:37,664
So all of them need to use some distributed central store, something like Redis, because this is something we keep up to, let's say,

1552
02:51:37,824 --> 02:51:39,652
24 hours in the memory.

1553
02:51:39,758 --> 02:51:44,132
And that is in the case they get restricted with most attempts.

1554
02:51:44,590 --> 02:51:53,700
Or we can still use database for keeping this data, but if it's something we are going to keep up to 24 hours, then Redis might be a good option here.

1555
02:51:54,158 --> 02:52:03,344
And then our atomic operations will ensure that we have the right accuracy, and there is no way to bypass the server hopping by connecting to

1556
02:52:03,504 --> 02:52:09,252
other server and wishing that it will work and the rate limit isn't applied for that server.

1557
02:52:10,222 --> 02:52:21,988
Next, we need to scale the database as well. Of course, with this scale, now we scaled the servers and applied rate limiting, but the database, a single database, cannot handle all this traffic.

1558
02:52:22,414 --> 02:52:28,448
So we need to split data across multiple database instances, which is also called sharding.

1559
02:52:28,910 --> 02:52:36,196
When it comes to sharding, one of the important decisions is on which field we are going to shard by for the databases.

1560
02:52:36,814 --> 02:52:40,676
So one approach can be to shard it based on user ID.

1561
02:52:40,814 --> 02:52:48,768
So this way we will make sure that all the writes, all the messages for user A end up in one shard in one database,

1562
02:52:48,928 --> 02:52:58,308
so that whenever we are fetching messages, we are fetching only from one database and we don't have to connect to multiple shards to read the messages for the same user.

1563
02:52:58,608 --> 02:53:06,916
The catch here is that individual users can become hotspots because some users naturally use it way more often than others,

1564
02:53:07,168 --> 02:53:17,156
and this way these users will start creating big databases, big tables of messages where the users who use it less often will create smaller shards.

1565
02:53:17,648 --> 02:53:20,868
So this will create an imbalanced sharding.

1566
02:53:21,198 --> 02:53:24,868
In this case, another option is to shard it by message IDs.

1567
02:53:25,168 --> 02:53:32,964
This will create even distribution of data, but fetching all messages for a user will require querying multiple shards

1568
02:53:33,488 --> 02:53:35,748
because there will be in different shards.

1569
02:53:35,888 --> 02:53:41,924
So your query will hit every shard, aggregate the results, and then return them to the server.

1570
02:53:42,288 --> 02:53:48,036
So the downside in this case is it will become slow and expensive compared to the user ID.

1571
02:53:48,430 --> 02:53:52,132
And the downside with the user ID is that we will have imbalanced shards.

1572
02:53:52,288 --> 02:53:59,808
So one shard might be very small compared to the other shard, which uses users who are using it very often.

1573
02:54:00,238 --> 02:54:09,316
So the main goal also for these interviews is not to just choose one of these but just tell them the different approaches that there are

1574
02:54:09,488 --> 02:54:14,756
and what is the best solution after all, or what is the solution you will go with.

1575
02:54:15,278 --> 02:54:22,532
So one of the good approaches here it can be sharding still by user ID but applying replication.

1576
02:54:23,406 --> 02:54:33,088
So let's say user A is sharded to this shard A database, all of the user A's conversations will be stored in the shard A.

1577
02:54:33,728 --> 02:54:40,996
But if he talks to user B who's connected to shard B, we will also duplicate the writes.

1578
02:54:41,168 --> 02:54:49,508
So we will write the copy of each user's shard into the shard B as well. And of course, this comes with a downside.

1579
02:54:49,808 --> 02:54:55,140
This doubles the write volume, but on the other hand, it makes the reads blazingly fast.

1580
02:54:55,408 --> 02:55:03,008
Each user will query only their own shard, user B will query it from shard B, and user A will query it from shard A.

1581
02:55:03,662 --> 02:55:06,436
And we can even replicate each of these shards.

1582
02:55:06,608 --> 02:55:13,828
So shard B will be replicated into B replica, and shard A will be replicated into A replica.

1583
02:55:14,368 --> 02:55:23,648
And user A will now read this history of messages only from the replica A, and user B will read it from replica B.

1584
02:55:23,808 --> 02:55:31,328
They won't affect the shards, the primary ones, which are responsible for write operations, and instead they will be reading here.

1585
02:55:31,488 --> 02:55:34,368
So it will increase the read performance a lot.

1586
02:55:34,574 --> 02:55:37,796
The trade-off here is that we are writing each message twice.

1587
02:55:38,030 --> 02:55:44,036
So that's why it's also important to clarify such things in the beginning in the requirements phase.

1588
02:55:44,128 --> 02:55:54,288
You need to see this in advance, like later, we are going to optimize the messages either for write operations or for read operations, and which is the more important.

1589
02:55:54,448 --> 02:56:01,156
And if you still failed to clarify that at the beginning, this is still a good place to ask instead of trying to guess.

1590
02:56:01,614 --> 02:56:07,888
So ask them whether reading is the more important in this situation or writing, and then based on that,

1591
02:56:07,968 --> 02:56:14,436
you will either replicate or you will go with message ID sharding, or you can even go with timestamp ID sharding.

1592
02:56:14,702 --> 02:56:20,356
There are different approaches here, and the main thing here is to understand the trade-offs between each approach.

1593
02:56:21,102 --> 02:56:27,568
So typically, when it comes to keeping the data available, we usually shard for write scalability.

1594
02:56:27,662 --> 02:56:35,044
So if you're applying sharding, which means splitting the database into smaller chunks, this is improving the write performance.

1595
02:56:35,534 --> 02:56:40,484
And when we apply replication, this is for improving the read scalability.

1596
02:56:40,688 --> 02:56:45,860
So if you just replicate one of the shards, this is usually for the read performance.

1597
02:56:46,766 --> 02:56:53,412
So once you decide on the sharding strategy, and let's say you decided to go with user ID sharding.

1598
02:56:55,406 --> 02:57:02,020
All the client rights will arrive to the shards to the primary node for each of the shards.

1599
02:57:02,144 --> 02:57:09,024
Let's say user IDs from 1 to 1000 are stored into the shard one.

1600
02:57:09,486 --> 02:57:11,172
This will be the primary node.

1601
02:57:11,566 --> 02:57:19,344
And then we can basically replicate this and use asynchronous syncing between the primary node and replicas.

1602
02:57:19,630 --> 02:57:29,476
We can have three replicas and they will be eventually consistent with the primary node by reading and duplicating the data into the replicas.

1603
02:57:30,094 --> 02:57:38,784
And then whenever we have read operations like opening the messages and seeing the unread messages, this is where we will need to perform read

1604
02:57:38,944 --> 02:57:39,332
queries.

1605
02:57:39,504 --> 02:57:45,220
And for read queries, we will only use the replicas, we won't be connecting to the primary node at all.

1606
02:57:45,518 --> 02:57:54,596
We will connect here only when we have write messages, which is someone writing to the user A or user A writing to someone else.

1607
02:57:55,182 --> 02:58:03,664
Now, next for such big systems where we are storing too much media files, too many messages, you need some cleanup way,

1608
02:58:03,824 --> 02:58:08,580
some way to clean up the garbage data that is never read again.

1609
02:58:09,006 --> 02:58:14,436
So this can be expired media, old undelivered messages, or stale connections.

1610
02:58:14,624 --> 02:58:23,268
We need to build a cleanup infrastructure to run some cron job over time and try to clean the expired medias and stuff.

1611
02:58:23,822 --> 02:58:33,828
So you need to have some job scheduler, something like Airflow or some cron job that will be scheduled every either daily basis or weekly basis,

1612
02:58:34,144 --> 02:58:37,668
and this needs to check the job status from monitoring.

1613
02:58:38,318 --> 02:58:43,428
Then on this side we have a worker pool which is containerized job executors.

1614
02:58:44,430 --> 02:58:48,292
So this job queue that you see here this is separate from message queue.

1615
02:58:48,384 --> 02:58:54,500
This is for enqueuing scheduled tasks and pooling the jobs to the worker pool for processing.

1616
02:58:54,990 --> 02:59:00,772
Then we will acknowledge that we received the message, or if we failed, then we will re-queue it on failure.

1617
02:59:01,198 --> 02:59:10,340
And then we will start executing the tasks in this worker pool, which can be cleaning up expired media by checking the date that they were published

1618
02:59:10,624 --> 02:59:12,304
or the undelivered messages.

1619
02:59:12,464 --> 02:59:21,344
This can be messages that we failed to write to the database, but we still have them somewhere in the memory, so we can remove this or stale

1620
02:59:21,504 --> 02:59:27,104
connections that we have and any other kind of data that we don't need anymore.

1621
02:59:27,264 --> 02:59:38,144
This is what the responsibility of this worker pool will be to delete all the data and then submit the job results to the monitoring so that we can see over time how well it is doing

1622
02:59:38,304 --> 02:59:44,132
and if there are any improvements here as well, because this is not something that you will set up once and then forget.

1623
02:59:44,304 --> 02:59:47,184
This is also something that will be improved over time.

1624
02:59:48,142 --> 02:59:56,804
So the trade-off here is that these scheduled jobs will add operational complexity because it is another big thing to monitor and debug.

1625
02:59:57,344 --> 03:00:07,184
But the alternative to this is or the other trade-off is to let the garbage accumulate until the system becomes so big that you have to do this either way.

1626
03:00:07,406 --> 03:00:17,796
So a regular cleanup like this is essential, and for long-term health, this is important to set up some worker pool that will start cleaning up the junk data that is not used.

1627
03:00:18,862 --> 03:00:27,984
That's the general flow of designing such large system like WhatsApp, but you won't usually end there because that is just the happy path

1628
03:00:28,558 --> 03:00:32,944
of all services work together, everyone is happy, customers are happy, right?

1629
03:00:33,230 --> 03:00:35,744
But that's never the case in reality.

1630
03:00:35,824 --> 03:00:45,824
So there can be failures in every part of the system, and one of the major things that you can discuss, for example, for a messaging app, is cache failure.

1631
03:00:45,984 --> 03:00:49,220
For example, what happens to undelivered messages?

1632
03:00:49,424 --> 03:00:56,132
Because Redis holds our undelivered messages inboxes, and what if the Redis cluster goes down itself?

1633
03:00:56,590 --> 03:01:06,784
Let's start with the problem example. Let's say user A sends a message and they are connected to chat server, so this is where they established the WebSocket communication

1634
03:01:07,022 --> 03:01:07,620
both ways.

1635
03:01:08,270 --> 03:01:16,868
Now, chat server will first write the undelivered messages to the Redis inbox, which is the fastest to compare to the database.

1636
03:01:17,358 --> 03:01:21,184
And then the second step is we don't only write to the Redis store, right?

1637
03:01:21,344 --> 03:01:25,504
We also write the backup which is more durable to the database.

1638
03:01:25,584 --> 03:01:30,884
So this is what our permanent storage is, in case the Redis cluster goes down.

1639
03:01:31,726 --> 03:01:38,084
So we will first try to fetch the pending messages from Redis for the chat server to forward it.

1640
03:01:38,400 --> 03:01:44,548
But in case this is unavailable, let's say Redis goes down, we need to fall back to the database instead.

1641
03:01:44,640 --> 03:01:52,240
So in this case, if Redis is empty or it's unavailable, then we need to fall back and read from the database.

1642
03:01:52,320 --> 03:01:58,320
So we need to retrieve the undelivered message from the database and then forward it to the server.

1643
03:01:58,560 --> 03:02:11,040
And either way, we need to at the end be able to deliver the message to user B, even if they are offline, and then they come back online and fetch the messages from the database in case the Redis

1644
03:02:11,200 --> 03:02:12,240
cache was down.

1645
03:02:12,622 --> 03:02:21,860
And whenever the Redis recovers and comes back online, we will first have a background job that will sync the inbox from database to Redis,

1646
03:02:22,160 --> 03:02:30,020
and then we will query something like select all from messages. inbox, where the delivered is null, which is not set in inbox.

1647
03:02:30,480 --> 03:02:37,316
and then for each undelivered message, we will write to Redis inbox, and then we will keep the database record as well.

1648
03:02:37,774 --> 03:02:45,188
So after that, we will resume to the normal delivery, which is writing to Redis and then reading from Redis again.

1649
03:02:46,382 --> 03:02:52,640
So to visualize this at the code level, this is for example what the write with fallback will look like.

1650
03:02:52,814 --> 03:03:00,548
So we will have some function like store undelivered message, which will be the user ID of the message and then the message itself.

1651
03:03:01,102 --> 03:03:04,800
So we will first try reading Redis from the cache.

1652
03:03:04,880 --> 03:03:10,948
So we will try to get the message with this user ID with the message timestamp from Redis cache.

1653
03:03:11,374 --> 03:03:18,880
But in case this goes wrong, we need to log that Redis is unavailable and we will need to start using database again.

1654
03:03:18,990 --> 03:03:21,508
So we will need to query this from database.

1655
03:03:21,774 --> 03:03:30,400
This can be either insert operation if the message was written to the other user, or this can be a read operation like select operation

1656
03:03:30,560 --> 03:03:34,724
if the user is trying to fetch the messages and Redis is unavailable.

1657
03:03:35,630 --> 03:03:41,920
So that is the second scenario. For example, we are trying to get undelivered messages, so we are trying to read it from Redis.

1658
03:03:42,000 --> 03:03:46,948
This is again trying to read from Redis because that is the fastest if it's available first.

1659
03:03:47,470 --> 03:03:54,020
If we see that messages length is greater than zero, then we will map parse and then send it to the user.

1660
03:03:54,400 --> 03:03:59,360
But in case something goes wrong in this try block, that means that the Redis is unavailable.

1661
03:03:59,470 --> 03:04:05,200
So again, we will log for us for tracing it later in CloudWatch or somewhere else.

1662
03:04:05,440 --> 03:04:17,120
And after that, we'll need to again read this from the database. So we'll do the select query instead to the database, which is again slower, but it's better than just failing the whole operation.

1663
03:04:17,262 --> 03:04:23,680
And then once the Redis is back online, it will sync with the database, have all the messages here.

1664
03:04:23,840 --> 03:04:26,692
So next time we will be reading from this tri block.

1665
03:04:27,982 --> 03:04:36,240
The next topic that you can expect to discuss is about regions and global distribution because WhatsApp is not only in let's say

1666
03:04:36,480 --> 03:04:38,800
US or it's not only in Europe,

1667
03:04:39,854 --> 03:04:47,520
it has users from all parts of the world, so you need to have different regions for each of these users.

1668
03:04:47,822 --> 03:04:49,920
For this, we can use the regions.

1669
03:04:50,000 --> 03:05:02,240
For example, if we are using US, we can use the US East 1, US West 2, or these kind of regions that are available to us in the AWS or the cloud provider that we are using.

1670
03:05:02,864 --> 03:05:11,332
And each region should have the full stack chat servers, Redis clusters, database charts so that it can operate independently.

1671
03:05:11,854 --> 03:05:16,944
Let's say, for example, user A opens their app and they are located in Tokyo.

1672
03:05:17,198 --> 03:05:25,664
So we will first do the DNS lookup to see where they should connect first, and then they will connect to the nearest IP address,

1673
03:05:25,824 --> 03:05:28,144
which will be the Singapore region.

1674
03:05:28,942 --> 03:05:35,024
And in this region, we will have the full stack, which will be the load balancer that they will first connect to.

1675
03:05:35,104 --> 03:05:44,144
They will make a request here to route them to the chat server. Here we will have more chat servers replicated, and for each region, this can be different.

1676
03:05:44,304 --> 03:05:52,772
For example, for US, maybe we are, or let's say for Ireland, we have a lot of users using the app, so here it can be many chat servers.

1677
03:05:53,166 --> 03:05:59,140
And let's say in Tokyo or in Singapore we have less users, so we will have less chat server replicas.

1678
03:05:59,598 --> 03:06:07,332
Either way, they will connect with chat server with WebSocket connection, and they will have the Redis cache for each user.

1679
03:06:07,584 --> 03:06:14,020
This is, for example, where we will look up where user B is to be able to send the message to their region.

1680
03:06:14,624 --> 03:06:18,532
And that is how the user B, let's say in London, will connect.

1681
03:06:18,702 --> 03:06:29,344
So first again, they will do DNS lookup, they will connect to the nearest IP address, they will make a request to the load balancer, and they will receive the message that was sent from user A to B

1682
03:06:29,664 --> 03:06:33,732
through the US West region, which is the one that they are connected to.

1683
03:06:34,958 --> 03:06:39,584
Now, another thing that can go wrong here is the Kafka cluster that we have.

1684
03:06:40,014 --> 03:06:48,420
Kafka itself is very scalable, it can handle thousands of messages per second without any issues, but still we need to plan

1685
03:06:48,624 --> 03:06:53,156
what happens in case this goes down or something happens with the Kafka cluster.

1686
03:06:54,574 --> 03:07:01,156
Inside of the cluster, we typically have multiple brokers, which are called servers, in the cluster.

1687
03:07:01,614 --> 03:07:07,620
So topics are split into partitions, and each partition has leader and it has replicas.

1688
03:07:08,142 --> 03:07:16,548
So producers usually write to the leader, similar like write database actions, and replicas just provide us with redundancy.

1689
03:07:17,006 --> 03:07:22,532
So scenario one is that one of the brokers fail inside of the Kafka cluster.

1690
03:07:22,862 --> 03:07:33,984
So if a broker dies, we need to replace the broker, let's say this was broker number something like three, we need to replace it and rejoin another broker to the cluster.

1691
03:07:34,158 --> 03:07:40,304
So Kafka will rebalance the partitions over time if we add this broker to the cluster.

1692
03:07:40,622 --> 03:07:44,164
But what if the entire Kafka cluster goes down?

1693
03:07:44,526 --> 03:07:46,944
This is very rare, but it's still possible.

1694
03:07:47,150 --> 03:07:50,304
So this means that chat servers can't publish any messages.

1695
03:07:50,464 --> 03:07:54,224
All of the chat servers were sending messages to this cluster, right?

1696
03:07:54,446 --> 03:07:57,104
And now they are no longer able to do that.

1697
03:07:57,262 --> 03:08:05,344
So message storage service can't consume the messages, which means that messages are not persisted, and this can be data loss.

1698
03:08:05,742 --> 03:08:09,664
In such cases, we need to have a mitigation of a local buffer.

1699
03:08:09,774 --> 03:08:19,184
So let's say we create a message buffer before trying to publish them to Kafka in the code, and this is what the publish message would look like as a function.

1700
03:08:19,470 --> 03:08:27,104
So first we have a try block, and this is the happy path, which is we are trying to publish the message to this Kafka cluster.

1701
03:08:27,344 --> 03:08:31,204
But in case it fails, it means that this cluster is down.

1702
03:08:31,566 --> 03:08:34,596
So we need to have some rollback mechanism for that.

1703
03:08:35,022 --> 03:08:38,884
So we have the catch block for that situation with Kafka error.

1704
03:08:39,184 --> 03:08:45,892
So first we will persist this error to our logging system to then identify why this message buffer failed.

1705
03:08:46,382 --> 03:08:50,784
But then we will try to push the message to the local message buffer instead.

1706
03:08:50,864 --> 03:08:52,836
This is just one approach that we can use.

1707
03:08:53,358 --> 03:09:00,612
But then this buffer is limited, so we can store up to let's say 100,000 messages in the buffer.

1708
03:09:00,942 --> 03:09:08,420
In case that also fails, we don't fail the whole operation. So we'll just start dropping the oldest ones from the buffer instead.

1709
03:09:08,942 --> 03:09:17,956
And then we will need to add some interval for waiting for the Kafka to recover, and once it recovers, we will try to publish it again to the Kafka.

1710
03:09:18,464 --> 03:09:26,980
And this time needs to be the exact time that takes us to spin up a new Kafka. So if this is entirely like

1711
03:09:27,854 --> 03:09:31,524
Destroyed or deleted, we need to spin up a new Kafka.

1712
03:09:31,632 --> 03:09:33,604
We need to know exactly how much this takes.

1713
03:09:33,712 --> 03:09:41,028
Let's say this takes five, like not five, even it needs to take less than five. So let's say two seconds to load, which is

1714
03:09:41,792 --> 03:09:43,620
two thousand milliseconds.

1715
03:09:44,014 --> 03:09:49,412
So we know that this number is greater than the time we need to spin up a new Kafka.

1716
03:09:49,792 --> 03:09:58,628
So we are just waiting for that time in the memory in the chat server, and then once this is back online with the new Kafka,

1717
03:09:58,752 --> 03:10:03,364
we are publishing this to that one instead of losing the whole memory of messages.

1718
03:10:04,462 --> 03:10:12,752
So this prevents the memory loss in case the Kafka goes down. But what if this server even like both fail at the same time?

1719
03:10:12,832 --> 03:10:18,948
Like we are keeping this in the memory, but then Kafka failed, and now this server also fails.

1720
03:10:20,206 --> 03:10:25,188
This is where we can also store these files into the server's local disk.

1721
03:10:25,486 --> 03:10:33,604
So append only the log file, and whenever the chat server also recovers, we will replay it from the disk, this log file,

1722
03:10:33,966 --> 03:10:40,068
and then we will delete the log after we successfully publish this to the Kafka cluster.

1723
03:10:41,294 --> 03:10:43,552
So, in summary, this is how it will look like.

1724
03:10:43,712 --> 03:10:53,152
If Kafka is completely destroyed, we are returning the messages from the message storage service, and then we request messages since last

1725
03:10:53,392 --> 03:10:53,860
ID.

1726
03:10:54,702 --> 03:11:05,700
We will query the processed messages from database, and then we will check which are the messages that are still not published to the database that we still have in the memory.

1727
03:11:06,112 --> 03:11:12,272
So this is where we will republish the missing messages into the Kafka once it is back online.

1728
03:11:12,832 --> 03:11:20,388
And once we write this all to the persistent storage like a database, this is where we will resume back to the normal consumption.

1729
03:11:20,846 --> 03:11:33,252
So you need to have such mechanisms to ensure that you are avoiding data loss or at least minimizing the data loss that can happen in case many parts of the system fail concurrently.

1730
03:11:33,678 --> 03:11:45,152
And lastly, you can expect to also talk about some feature control in such big systems because you don't usually release new features for all users, you usually do gradual rollout.

1731
03:11:45,518 --> 03:11:48,592
In such cases, we usually use feature flux store.

1732
03:11:48,752 --> 03:11:53,700
All of the cloud providers come with such solutions to provide you with feature flux.

1733
03:11:53,952 --> 03:12:00,272
For example, on Azure you have such system, for AWS you have it, and you even have external tools that you can use,

1734
03:12:00,352 --> 03:12:06,352
like LaunchDarkly is one of the popular ones that we used, for example, in the previous company.

1735
03:12:06,592 --> 03:12:10,564
So these are just tools that allow you to set key value pairs.

1736
03:12:10,992 --> 03:12:15,364
It can be like is new theme enabled, and it will be true or false.

1737
03:12:15,792 --> 03:12:20,112
And the true or false is also controlled based on the environment.

1738
03:12:20,238 --> 03:12:25,072
So it can be, for example, true for staging, but it can still be false for production.

1739
03:12:25,230 --> 03:12:32,388
So this way, let's say developers are testing on staging, they see the new feature, but users on production don't see it.

1740
03:12:32,752 --> 03:12:39,792
And when you decide to also roll out it for production, you can still set it to true for only 20% of users.

1741
03:12:39,872 --> 03:12:47,844
So only 20% of users will start this beta feature testing it, and then if it goes well, you don't notice any

1742
03:12:48,334 --> 03:12:49,312
failure logs.

1743
03:12:49,392 --> 03:12:51,588
That is when you can enable it for everyone.

1744
03:12:52,078 --> 03:13:01,472
So in this case, the chat server will check the supported features from this feature flag store, which can be LashDarkly, it can be the cloud provider's native solution,

1745
03:13:01,712 --> 03:13:06,112
and then we will use gradual enablement per user or per group.

1746
03:13:06,272 --> 03:13:10,212
For example, per user will be that 20% users have access to this.

1747
03:13:11,182 --> 03:13:16,676
And then we will return the enabled features from this feature flag store to the chat server.

1748
03:13:17,070 --> 03:13:25,796
And by having this, for example, the chat server can send messages with new features to the client apps that are above this version

1749
03:13:26,032 --> 03:13:34,916
and strip the unsupported fields or features for the client apps that are below 2.2 or some version that you set.

1750
03:13:35,790 --> 03:13:39,524
So that is for example discussing the complete architecture.

1751
03:13:39,776 --> 03:13:44,548
Now we can also talk about how usually to approach this like a framework.

1752
03:13:44,910 --> 03:13:54,596
So as you can see, we are starting simple. You usually start with the clarifying requirements and then calculating the estimates because this is important for you to

1753
03:13:55,150 --> 03:14:04,292
estimate what are the storage solutions you are going to use, what are the services are you going to introduce a Kafka broker, or maybe it's not needed.

1754
03:14:04,654 --> 03:14:14,372
So you don't jump straight into Redis, PubSub or Database Sharding. You usually begin with the high-level simple architecture with just thousands of users.

1755
03:14:14,896 --> 03:14:25,216
Then you build it naturally by talking with the interviewer, and then you identify bottlenecks, like you could see here, where, for example, the Kafka message can break,

1756
03:14:25,376 --> 03:14:32,772
we can have rate limiting issues, we can have database that goes down, so you need to start talking about this as well.

1757
03:14:33,294 --> 03:14:36,896
So it's not something that you can usually memorize, you need to just

1758
03:14:37,710 --> 03:14:40,196
know the steps that are there, and then

1759
03:14:40,846 --> 03:14:45,860
for each system, you need to go with the flow and then discuss the trade-offs.

1760
03:14:46,382 --> 03:14:51,428
So understanding trade-offs is another important piece of this, every decision has costs.

1761
03:14:51,616 --> 03:15:00,740
For example, if we are using WebSockets, it's not all green because, for example, they use more memory than HTTP, but they provide real-time delivery.

1762
03:15:00,896 --> 03:15:04,580
And for example, for messaging app, that trade-off is worth it.

1763
03:15:04,736 --> 03:15:12,656
For some other situation, the trade-off might not be worth it. Maybe you will go with something like just a polling option instead of just a web socket.

1764
03:15:13,134 --> 03:15:19,236
So you'll need to also be ready to discuss the back of the envelope calculations before getting to this.

1765
03:15:19,456 --> 03:15:28,548
So be ready to calculate these numbers, maybe do some examples and then check whether you calculated it properly or not.

1766
03:15:29,454 --> 03:15:34,256
And then once you have something stable, then only you will start thinking about failures.

1767
03:15:34,336 --> 03:15:43,056
You don't need to rush into that because you still didn't decide on the main components and haven't designed the full system,

1768
03:15:43,216 --> 03:15:45,604
you don't want to get into the failures before that.

1769
03:15:46,016 --> 03:15:53,696
And don't worry if this was too much in detail, you are not expected to design a perfect system in one or two hours.

1770
03:15:53,870 --> 03:15:55,556
That's not the goal in the interviews.

1771
03:15:55,776 --> 03:16:02,916
They will usually want to see how you think, how you discuss trade-offs, or are you just picking something because you like it?

1772
03:16:03,278 --> 03:16:11,056
So you need to show your thinking process, say this out loud whenever you are designing something, and that will also help you to get direction.

1773
03:16:11,136 --> 03:16:20,964
If you're going to the wrong path, the interviewer will correct you and they will give you hints on choosing the right solution for the trade-off that you're thinking about.

1774
03:16:21,230 --> 03:16:26,736
By the way, if you'd like to learn all these concepts that I'm using for designing these systems from scratch,

1775
03:16:26,976 --> 03:16:34,052
I have a whole system design guide covering all these concepts that you need for system design interviews on my YouTube channel.

1776
03:16:34,510 --> 03:16:42,496
You can check out the first link in the description to find the link to my channel, and the first pinned video in my channel is that system

1777
03:16:42,496 --> 03:16:47,364
design guide explaining all these concepts you need for passing these system design interviews.

